      ],
      "lineAnnotations": {
        "2": {"synopsis":"The DEF statement (token $F7): defines a function as a variable that holds where its body\nis, to be evaluated when the name is used.\n1. The name must be a variable (class 1 or 3 from NEXT_ITEM), else error 1.\n2. In a running program (bit 2 of $1700), the reference must have just created the variable\n   (bit 7 of its flag byte), else error 15 (\"Variable declared twice\"). The flag byte gets\n   bit 2 (a function) instead of bit 7, and the value becomes two words: the current line\n   ($170C) and the text pointer just past the name; $1726 (the end of the variables) moves\n   to just past them.\n3. Skips the body (DEF_SKIP) and goes on at the statement loop. In a direct command the\n   definition is only skipped.","comment":"Class 1 or 3: a variable?"},
        "9": {"comment":"A program running?"},
        "15": {"comment":"Save the text pointer and the variable address"},
        "19": {"comment":"HL = the variable's value"},
        "20": {"comment":"Back to its flag byte"},
        "21": {"comment":"Created by this reference?"},
        "25": {"comment":"No longer new: a function now"},
        "30": {"comment":"The current line"},
        "34": {"comment":"Is the first word of the value"},
        "38": {"comment":"The text pointer past the name"},
        "39": {"comment":"Is the second word"},
        "43": {"comment":"The variables end past the 4 bytes"},
        "46": {"synopsis":"Skips a function's body: up to the end of the line or the first ':' outside parentheses,\ncounting depth in C.","comment":"To the text pointer"},
        "47": {"comment":"Depth 0 after the DEC"},
        "49": {"synopsis":"A ')' in the body: one level out. A jump to $0033 lands on the $0C of LD A,$0C, INC C:\none level in for a '('.","comment":"One level out"},
        "50": {"comment":"$0033 is INC C: one level in, from a '('"},
        "52": {"synopsis":"The loop over the body's bytes.","comment":"The end of the line?"},
        "58": {"comment":"Past the byte"},
        "59": {"comment":"A '('?"},
        "63": {"comment":"A ')'?"},
        "67": {"comment":"A ':'?"},
        "71": {"comment":"Outside all parentheses?"},
        "75": {"synopsis":"The body is skipped: the statement loop takes the next statement or the end of the line.","comment":"Run on at the statement loop (STMT_LOOP)"},
        "78": {"synopsis":"Error 15, \"Variable declared twice\" (DEF and DIM of a name already in use).","comment":"Error $0F: Variable declared twice"},
        "80": {"synopsis":"After a ',': fetches the next name and declares it (falls into DIM_CMD).","comment":"The next name"},
        "83": {"synopsis":"The DIM statement (token $F5): declares arrays and string lengths, names separated by ','.\n1. The name must be a variable not used before (bit 7 or bit 3 of C', else error 15); bit 3\n   has VAR_CREATE create it. Its flag byte is XORed with $81: no longer new, an array (bit\n   0); a dimension count byte $FF follows it.\n2. A string name followed by '*' and no '(' is a single string of that maximum length\n   (0-254, 255 is error 4): the count byte is dropped and the array bit cleared.\n3. Otherwise '(' and subscripts: each bound n (0-32767, else error 4) is stored as the word\n   n + 1 and multiplied into the element count on the calculator stack (CALC_MULTIPLY); then\n   ')'. A string array takes '*' and its length (18 by default).\n4. A count of 32768 or more is error 6 (\"No memory\"); otherwise the elements are allocated\n   one by one (VAR_NEW_VALUE).","comment":"A token?"},
        "87": {"comment":"Class 1 or 3: a variable?"},
        "93": {"comment":"The variable's flags"},
        "94": {"comment":"New (bit 7) or not created yet (bit 3)?"},
        "98": {"comment":"Not created yet: create it (VAR_CREATE)"},
        "103": {"comment":"Back to the flag byte"},
        "105": {"comment":"No longer new; an array"},
        "110": {"comment":"Is the variable's flag byte"},
        "112": {"comment":"The dimension count, $FF so far"},
        "115": {"comment":"Keep its address"},
        "116": {"comment":"The variables end past it"},
        "122": {"comment":"The token after the name"},
        "125": {"comment":"A numeric variable?"},
        "131": {"comment":"A '*'? (a string length)"},
        "135": {"comment":"The length after it"},
        "138": {"comment":"255?"},
        "141": {"comment":"C = the maximum length"},
        "143": {"comment":"The count byte's address"},
        "144": {"comment":"Drop the count byte: not an array"},
        "147": {"comment":"Back to the flag byte"},
        "148": {"comment":"Not an array"},
        "150": {"comment":"Allocate the string (VAR_NEW_STR)"},
        "153": {"synopsis":"After a name: a ',' declares another one, anything else ends the statement.","comment":"The current token"},
        "156": {"comment":"A ','?"},
        "160": {"comment":"No: the end of the statement (STMT_END + 3)"},
        "163": {"synopsis":"An array: '(' and the bounds, separated by ','.","comment":"Expect '(' (EXPECT_TOKEN)"},
        "168": {"comment":"Skip the fetch: the bound's first token is read"},
        "169": {"synopsis":"One bound: stores bound + 1 and multiplies it into the element count.","comment":"Fetch the next token (after a ',')"},
        "172": {"comment":"The bound as an integer"},
        "175": {"comment":"32768 or more: error 4"},
        "178": {"comment":"Bound + 1 elements"},
        "180": {"comment":"Store the word at the variables end"},
        "187": {"comment":"Past it"},
        "190": {"comment":"HL = bound + 1"},
        "191": {"comment":"Onto the calculator stack"},
        "194": {"comment":"One more dimension"},
        "197": {"comment":"Not the first: multiply into the count (op 2)"},
        "200": {"comment":"The current token"},
        "203": {"comment":"A ',': another bound"},
        "207": {"comment":"Expect ')'"},
        "212": {"comment":"The count byte: now the number of dimensions"},
        "214": {"comment":"A numeric array?"},
        "220": {"comment":"A '*'? (the element length)"},
        "222": {"comment":"18 characters by default"},
        "227": {"comment":"255?"},
        "228": {"synopsis":"A string length of 255: error 4 (\"Bad argument\").","comment":"Error 4: Bad argument"},
        "232": {"comment":"C = the element length"},
        "233": {"synopsis":"Checks the element count against 32768, then allocates the elements.","comment":"Keep the element length"},
        "234": {"comment":"Copy the element count and load 32768 (constant 38)"},
        "238": {"comment":"Compare them (NUM_COMPARE)"},
        "241": {"comment":"32768 or more: error 6 (NO_MEMORY)"},
        "244": {"comment":"HL = the element count"},
        "248": {"synopsis":"Allocates one element per pass (VAR_NEW_VALUE: a number, or a string of length C)."},
        "249": {"comment":"Allocate one element"},
        "253": {"comment":"Count down the elements"},
        "258": {"comment":"Then a ',' or the end of the statement"},
        "260": {"synopsis":"The ELSE statement (token $F4), reached by running the statement before it: after an IF or\nON on this line (bit 0 of $1702, cleared at each line's start) it skips the rest of the\nline (NEXT_LINE); anywhere else it is error 1.","comment":"After IF or ON on this line?"},
        "267": {"comment":"Skip the rest of the line (NEXT_LINE)"},
        "270": {"synopsis":"The END statement (token $F3), also where the SYS low chip's NEXT_LINE goes when a running\nprogram runs out of lines: clears $170E (nothing to CONTINUE) and goes to COMMAND_LOOP."},
        "273": {"comment":"Nothing to CONTINUE"},
        "276": {"comment":"Back to the command loop (COMMAND_LOOP)"},
        "279": {"synopsis":"The EXT statement (token $D1): EXT n [,a [,b [,c]]] calls the machine code routine whose\naddress is in RAM word $0021 + 2n, n = 0-6 (else error 4); a zero word does nothing. The\nroutine is called through the JP at RAM $0015 (its operand at $0016 is patched) and returns\nto\nSTMT_END.\nEntry of the routine: HL = a, DE = b, BC = c (as integers; those not given are undefined).","comment":"n as a byte"},
        "282": {"comment":"0-6?"},
        "287": {"comment":"2n"},
        "289": {"comment":"The current token"},
        "292": {"comment":"A ','? (a first argument)"},
        "296": {"comment":"Fetch and read it as an integer"},
        "299": {"comment":"DE = a"},
        "300": {"comment":"Another ','?"},
        "304": {"comment":"HL = b"},
        "307": {"comment":"Another ','?"},
        "312": {"comment":"BC = c"},
        "318": {"synopsis":"Looks up the routine's address and jumps to it through RAM $0015.","comment":"H = 2n; keep b"},
        "320": {"comment":"DE = 2n"},
        "323": {"comment":"The vectors at RAM $0021"},
        "327": {"comment":"DE = the routine's address"},
        "330": {"comment":"None?"},
        "335": {"comment":"Patch it into the JP at RAM $0015"},
        "339": {"comment":"DE = a"},
        "340": {"comment":"It returns to STMT_END"},
        "344": {"comment":"HL = a, DE = b"},
        "345": {"comment":"Call it through RAM $0015"},
        "348": {"synopsis":"The FOR statement (token $F2): FOR v = start TO limit [STEP step].\n1. v must be a numeric variable (class 3); a string is error 14 (\"Type mismatch\"), anything\n   else error 1. Its address comes from VAR_ADDR (subscripts included).\n2. '=' and the start value, stored in v; TO and the limit; STEP and the step, or 1. The\n   statement must end there, else error 1.\n3. Pushes a FOR entry (type $2B, 43 bytes) on the expression stack and keeps it there: +0\n   $2B, +1 the text pointer after the statement, +3 the current line ($170C), +5 v's\n   address, +7 the step (a 9-byte number entry), +16 18 bytes of working space for NEXT, +34\n   the limit.","comment":"A numeric variable?"},
        "352": {"comment":"Not a variable at all?"},
        "357": {"comment":"Error $0E: Type mismatch"},
        "359": {"synopsis":"A numeric variable: stores the start value, then evaluates the limit and the step.","comment":"HL = the variable's address (VAR_ADDR)"},
        "362": {"comment":"Expect '='"},
        "367": {"comment":"Room left? (ROOM_256)"},
        "370": {"comment":"Keep the address"},
        "371": {"comment":"Evaluate the start value"},
        "376": {"comment":"Store it in the variable"},
        "379": {"comment":"Expect TO"},
        "384": {"comment":"Evaluate the limit"},
        "387": {"comment":"Leave 18 bytes of working space above it"},
        "392": {"comment":"STEP?"},
        "396": {"comment":"No: the step is 1"},
        "400": {"comment":"Onto the calculator stack"},
        "404": {"comment":"Skip the CALL"},
        "405": {"synopsis":"Evaluates the step after STEP, then builds the FOR entry below it.","comment":"STEP given: evaluate it"},
        "408": {"comment":"The end of the statement?"},
        "413": {"comment":"HL = the expression stack's top"},
        "416": {"comment":"The variable's address"},
        "421": {"comment":"The current line"},
        "429": {"comment":"The text pointer after FOR"},
        "437": {"comment":"Type $2B: a FOR entry, 43 bytes"},
        "440": {"comment":"Is the new top"},
        "443": {"comment":"The next statement (STMT_END)"},
        "446": {"synopsis":"The line read for INPUT came back with an error: a break ($F5) stops at the INPUT\nstatement (INPUT_BREAK), any other error code is raised.","comment":"A break?"},
        "448": {"comment":"No: raise the error in A"},
        "450": {"synopsis":"Steps the text pointer back to the INPUT token and stops there, as STOP does (STOP_BREAK),\nso CONTINUE runs the INPUT again.","comment":"Back one byte"},
        "452": {"comment":"The INPUT token?"},
        "456": {"comment":"Stop here (the STOP code)"},
        "459": {"synopsis":"The INPUT statement (token $EC): INPUT [#n] [PROMPT text] variables.\n1. Output goes to channel $20 ((ix+5)); the prompt is \"? \" (INPUT_PROMPT_TXT). PROMPT and a\n   string expression replace it; '#' and a channel (CHANNEL_DEFAULT) choose the input. These\n   are separated by ','; after one of them a ':' introduces the variables (a variable\n   straight after them is error 1).\n2. On channel $20 the prompt is printed (PRINT_LSTR), RST $30 code $24 marks the cursor as\n   the start of the line read, and KEY_LINE reads the reply into the buffer at $1831 (a\n   length byte, the text, $FF). A break stops at the statement (INPUT_ERROR).\n3. $1716 points into the reply, and the variables are filled as by READ (READ_NEXT_VAR) with\n   F' carry clear: items separated by ',' from the reply.","comment":"F' carry: nothing given before the variables yet"},
        "462": {"comment":"The default prompt, \"? \""},
        "465": {"comment":"Output to channel $20"},
        "469": {"comment":"Skip the fetch: the first token is read"},
        "470": {"synopsis":"One item: PROMPT and its text, a channel, or the first variable.","comment":"Fetch the next token (after a ',' or ':')"},
        "473": {"comment":"PROMPT?"},
        "477": {"comment":"Fetch the text's first token"},
        "480": {"comment":"Evaluate a string (EVAL_STR)"},
        "483": {"comment":"Drop it, keeping its address"},
        "488": {"comment":"Past the type byte: HL = the length-prefixed text"},
        "493": {"synopsis":"After PROMPT or a channel: a ',' takes another item, a ':' or the end the variables.","comment":"F' carry clear: something given"},
        "496": {"comment":"A ','?"},
        "500": {"comment":"A ':'? (the variables follow)"},
        "504": {"comment":"The end of the line: no variables"},
        "506": {"synopsis":"Error 1, \"Not understood\".","comment":"Error $01: Not understood"},
        "508": {"synopsis":"A '#' and a channel (CHANNEL_DEFAULT, Z when given), or the first variable.","comment":"A channel? (Z: given)"},
        "513": {"comment":"A variable after PROMPT or '#' without ':'?"},
        "516": {"synopsis":"After the ':': fetches the first variable.","comment":"Fetch the token after the ':'"},
        "519": {"synopsis":"Prints the prompt (on channel $20 only) and reads the reply line.","comment":"The input channel"},
        "522": {"comment":"The screen editor's? Print the prompt"},
        "527": {"comment":"Mark the cursor as the line's start"},
        "529": {"comment":"Read a line into $1831 (Z: no error)"},
        "532": {"comment":"An error or a break"},
        "534": {"comment":"Past the length byte: the reply's text"},
        "535": {"comment":"Where the next item is read from"},
        "538": {"comment":"Carry clear (INPUT); $021B is SCF, READ's entry"},
        "539": {"synopsis":"The READ statement (token $DB), and the variable loop INPUT falls into with F' carry clear\n(F' carry set: READ).\n1. Each variable (class 1 or 3, else error 1) gets its address from VAR_ADDR_ALT.\n2. READ takes the next item of the DATA statements: $1712 is the line of the current DATA,\n   $1714 the position in it (0: search from the start of the line in $1712). Items are\n   separated by ',' and end at a token; the next DATA statement is searched for statement by\n   statement and line by line; the end of the program is error 7 (\"No DATA\").\n3. A number is read by READ_NUMBER; no digits is error 12 (\"Cannot READ\"), an exponent\n   overflow error 13; a string by READ_STR_ITEM. An item followed by anything but ',', a '!'\n   or a token is error 12.\n4. INPUT reads the items of the reply from $1716 instead (INPUT_NUMBER for a number).\n   Variables are separated by ','."},
        "541": {"comment":"The current token"},
        "544": {"comment":"Skip the fetch: the first variable is read"},
        "545": {"synopsis":"One variable: checks it and finds where its value comes from.","comment":"Fetch the next variable (after a ',')"},
        "548": {"comment":"The end of the statement?"},
        "553": {"comment":"Class 1 or 3: a variable?"},
        "560": {"comment":"INPUT?"},
        "563": {"comment":"The position in the current DATA"},
        "566": {"comment":"None? Search from its line's start"},
        "570": {"synopsis":"Skips spaces in a DATA statement; at an item reads it, at its end searches on.","comment":"Skip spaces"},
        "576": {"comment":"Back to the first non-space"},
        "577": {"comment":"A '!' comment? On to the next line"},
        "581": {"comment":"An item?"},
        "585": {"synopsis":"Finds the start of the next statement on this line (NEXT_KW_STMT).","comment":"Keep the program's text pointer"},
        "589": {"comment":"To the next statement or the line's end"},
        "597": {"comment":"Past its token"},
        "598": {"synopsis":"Is the statement DATA? Its items are read; another statement is skipped; the line's end goes\non to the next line.","comment":"DATA?"},
        "602": {"comment":"Another statement on this line?"},
        "606": {"synopsis":"Steps $1712 to the next line by its length byte.","comment":"The DATA line"},
        "609": {"comment":"Plus its length"},
        "613": {"comment":"Is the next line"},
        "616": {"synopsis":"Starts a line's search: the end of the program is error 7; otherwise its first statement.","comment":"The line to search"},
        "619": {"comment":"The end of the program?"},
        "623": {"comment":"Past the length and the line number"},
        "626": {"synopsis":"Skips the spaces at the line's start.","comment":"Skip spaces"},
        "632": {"comment":"Is it DATA?"},
        "634": {"synopsis":"Error 7, \"No DATA\".","comment":"Error $07: No DATA"},
        "636": {"synopsis":"Reads one item (from DATA or the reply, HL pointing at it) into the variable.","comment":"Keep the mode in F'; the item's address"},
        "639": {"comment":"The variable's address (VAR_ADDR_ALT)"},
        "644": {"comment":"A numeric variable?"},
        "646": {"comment":"HL = the item; the variable's address kept"},
        "649": {"comment":"Read a string item (READ_STR_ITEM)"},
        "652": {"comment":"HL = the variable's address"},
        "653": {"comment":"Store the string"},
        "658": {"synopsis":"Error 12, \"Cannot READ\": an item that does not fit the variable.","comment":"Error $0C: Cannot READ"},
        "660": {"synopsis":"A numeric variable: reads a number from the item (READ_NUMBER).","comment":"Room left?"},
        "663": {"comment":"Read a number (carry: digits read)"},
        "666": {"comment":"INPUT?"},
        "669": {"comment":"READ: digits read?"},
        "672": {"comment":"An exponent overflow?"},
        "673": {"comment":"Error 13 (OVERFLOW_ERR)"},
        "678": {"synopsis":"INPUT: an overflowing reply becomes the largest number (NUM_CLAMP)."},
        "680": {"comment":"Limit an overflow"},
        "684": {"synopsis":"Stores the number in the variable.","comment":"HL = the variable's address"},
        "685": {"comment":"Store the number"},
        "688": {"synopsis":"After an item: checks what follows it in DATA, or skips to the next item of the reply.","comment":"The item's end"},
        "689": {"comment":"INPUT?"},
        "693": {"synopsis":"Skips spaces after a DATA item.","comment":"Skip spaces"},
        "699": {"comment":"A ','? Past it"},
        "703": {"comment":"Back to it"},
        "704": {"comment":"A '!'?"},
        "708": {"comment":"A token ends the item; anything else is error 12"},
        "713": {"synopsis":"Keeps the position in the DATA statement for the next READ.","comment":"Where the next READ goes on"},
        "718": {"synopsis":"INPUT: skips the rest of the item in the reply, up to past a ',' or to its $FF end.","comment":"Mode back to F'"},
        "719": {"synopsis":"The loop over the reply's characters.","comment":"The next character"},
        "721": {"comment":"A ','?"},
        "725": {"comment":"The end ($FF)?"},
        "728": {"comment":"Stay at the end"},
        "729": {"synopsis":"Keeps the position in the reply for the next variable.","comment":"Where the next item is read from"},
        "732": {"synopsis":"After a variable: a ',' takes the next one, the end of the statement ends it, anything else\nis error 1.","comment":"The current token"},
        "735": {"comment":"The end of the statement?"},
        "740": {"comment":"A ','? The next variable"},
        "745": {"comment":"Error $01: Not understood"},
        "747": {"synopsis":"INPUT's default prompt, length-prefixed: \"? \" (INPUT_CMD points HL here; PRINT_LSTR prints\nit)."},
        "750": {"synopsis":"The IF statement (token $ED): IF condition THEN statement or line [ELSE statement or line].\nAllows ELSE on this line (bit 0 of $1702) and evaluates the condition; it is true when the\nnumber's first BCD digit pair (IY+6) is not zero. True: runs what follows THEN (a line\nnumber after it is a GOTO, IF_THEN_PART). False: searches the rest of the line for an ELSE\nstatement and runs what follows it (IF_ELSE_PART), or goes to the next line.","comment":"ELSE allowed on this line"},
        "754": {"comment":"Evaluate the condition"},
        "757": {"comment":"Its leading digits: zero?"},
        "761": {"comment":"Keep Z: false"},
        "762": {"comment":"Drop it"},
        "765": {"comment":"HL = the text pointer, past THEN"},
        "769": {"comment":"Expect THEN"},
        "774": {"comment":"False?"},
        "780": {"synopsis":"An ELSE found: runs what follows it, as THEN's part.","comment":"Past the ELSE token"},
        "781": {"comment":"Is the text pointer"},
        "785": {"comment":"The first token after it"},
        "788": {"synopsis":"A line number is a GOTO; anything else is a statement for the statement loop.","comment":"A number? GOTO it"},
        "793": {"comment":"Run the statement at HL (STMT_LOOP)"},
        "796": {"synopsis":"The condition is false: resets the expression stack and looks for ELSE.","comment":"The expression stack as the statement found it"},
        "800": {"synopsis":"Steps statement by statement (NEXT_KW_STMT): ELSE is run, the line's end goes to NEXT_LINE.","comment":"To the next statement"},
        "803": {"comment":"ELSE?"},
        "807": {"comment":"The end of the line: the next line (NEXT_LINE)"},
        "812": {"comment":"Search on from here"},
        "818": {"synopsis":"The ON statement (token $E3): ON n GOTO l1, l2, ... or ON n GOSUB l1, l2, ...; allows ELSE\non\nthis line (bit 0 of $1702). The nth line number is taken (ON_SELECT). When the list has\nfewer than n, an ELSE statement on the line is run (ON_NO_LINE), else error 4. Anything but\nGOTO or GOSUB is error 1. GOSUB returns to the statement after the ON.","comment":"ELSE allowed on this line"},
        "822": {"comment":"n as a byte"},
        "825": {"comment":"B = n"},
        "827": {"comment":"The token after it"},
        "829": {"comment":"GOTO?"},
        "833": {"comment":"GOSUB?"},
        "837": {"comment":"Find the nth line number"},
        "840": {"comment":"HL = its line (FIND_LINE_ARG)"},
        "843": {"comment":"The expression stack as the statement found it"},
        "848": {"comment":"The next statement: the return point"},
        "851": {"comment":"DE = the return point"},
        "853": {"comment":"Push a GOSUB entry and go"},
        "855": {"synopsis":"Steps through the line numbers after GOTO or GOSUB, counting B down; returns at the nth one,\nread onto the calculator stack (A = 2). Too few: drops its return address and goes to\nON_NO_LINE.\nEntry: B = n.","comment":"The next line number"},
        "858": {"comment":"The nth?"},
        "860": {"comment":"Keep the text pointer"},
        "864": {"comment":"The token after it"},
        "867": {"comment":"The end of the statement: too few"},
        "869": {"comment":"A ','? On to the next"},
        "873": {"synopsis":"Error 1, \"Not understood\".","comment":"Error $01: Not understood"},
        "875": {"synopsis":"No nth line number: runs an ELSE on the line, else error 4.","comment":"Drop ON_SELECT's return address"},
        "876": {"comment":"The expression stack as the statement found it"},
        "880": {"synopsis":"Steps statement by statement looking for ELSE."},
        "881": {"comment":"To the next statement"},
        "884": {"comment":"ELSE? Run what follows it"},
        "888": {"comment":"The end of the line: error 4"},
        "893": {"comment":"Search on from here"},
        "898": {"synopsis":"The GOSUB statement (token $F0): GOSUB line. Finds the line (FIND_LINE_ARG; error 2 when it\nis missing), which must end the statement (else error 1), and pushes a GOSUB entry (type\n$06, 6 bytes) on the expression stack: +0 $06, +1 the text pointer after the statement, +3\nthe current line ($170C), +5 the byte at $1702 (ELSE allowed). Then runs the line, as GOTO.","comment":"HL = the line"},
        "901": {"comment":"The token after the number"},
        "904": {"comment":"Not the end of the statement?"},
        "906": {"comment":"DE = the text pointer: the return point"},
        "910": {"synopsis":"Pushes the GOSUB entry (return point in DE) and runs the line at HL. ON GOSUB comes here.","comment":"Keep the line"},
        "911": {"comment":"Room left?"},
        "914": {"comment":"HL = the expression stack's top"},
        "918": {"comment":"The ELSE flag of this line"},
        "922": {"comment":"The current line"},
        "930": {"comment":"The return point"},
        "934": {"comment":"Type $06: a GOSUB entry"},
        "937": {"comment":"Is the new top"},
        "941": {"comment":"Run the line"},
        "943": {"synopsis":"ON ... GOTO: finds the nth line number and falls into GOTO_CMD.","comment":"Find the nth line number"},
        "946": {"synopsis":"The GOTO statement (token $EF): GOTO line. Finds the line (FIND_LINE_ARG; error 2 when it is\nmissing), resets the expression stack to the statement's start and runs the line (the SYS\nlow chip's RUN_CMD from $DE29: marks a program running).","comment":"HL = the line"},
        "949": {"comment":"The expression stack as the statement found it"},
        "953": {"synopsis":"Runs the line at HL, marked as a running program (RUN_CMD + $0E, $DE29).","comment":"Run the line at HL (RUN_CMD + $0E)"},
        "956": {"synopsis":"An assignment without LET: the SYS low chip's STMT_LOOP comes here with a byte below $CA at\na statement's start. Steps back to that byte, reads it again as a variable (NEXT_ITEM) and\nfalls into LET_CMD.","comment":"Back to the statement's first byte"},
        "958": {"comment":"Read it as an item"},
        "961": {"synopsis":"The LET statement (token $EB): LET variable = expression, or LET a$(i:j) = text.\n1. The target must be a variable (class 1 or 3) and not a DEF function (bit 2 of C'), else\n   error 1; bit 3 has VAR_CREATE create it. Its flags go to $1701.\n2. Its address from VAR_ADDR (subscripts included), then LET_VALUE: '=' and the expression,\n   or a slice.\n3. Stores the value (STORE_VALUE: a number, or a string, which may not be longer than the\n   variable's maximum length: error 13) and ends the statement.","comment":"Class 1 or 3: a variable?"},
        "969": {"comment":"A function name?"},
        "974": {"comment":"Not created yet: create it"},
        "979": {"comment":"Its flags: bit 1 numeric"},
        "983": {"comment":"HL = the variable's address"},
        "986": {"comment":"The current token"},
        "990": {"comment":"Evaluate the value"},
        "994": {"comment":"Store it in the variable"},
        "997": {"comment":"The next statement (STMT_END)"},
        "1000": {"synopsis":"Evaluates the value to assign. After a string variable a '(' starts a slice a$(i:j)\n(SLICE_ARGS: E = i, D = j), whose characters are replaced by the value: the characters after\nj are saved below the expression stack, the value is evaluated under them, the characters\nbefore i are copied in front of it, and the three become one string entry (longer than 254\ncharacters: error 13).\nEntry: A = the current token, HL = the variable's address, C = its flags.\nExit: the value on the expression stack.","comment":"A '('? A slice"},
        "1004": {"comment":"A numeric variable cannot be sliced"},
        "1009": {"comment":"Its current length byte"},
        "1011": {"comment":"C = the current length, B = 0"},
        "1013": {"comment":"E = i, D = j (SLICE_ARGS)"},
        "1016": {"comment":"j below i?"},
        "1020": {"comment":"Then j = i - 1: an empty slice"},
        "1022": {"synopsis":"Sets j; are there characters after it to keep?"},
        "1023": {"comment":"Characters after j"},
        "1025": {"comment":"HL = the current length byte"},
        "1026": {"comment":"Keep the stack's top, the address, i and j"},
        "1031": {"comment":"None after j?"},
        "1035": {"comment":"HL = the last character"},
        "1036": {"comment":"C = how many after j"},
        "1037": {"comment":"Copy them below the expression stack"},
        "1043": {"comment":"They are the new top"},
        "1047": {"synopsis":"Evaluates the value below the saved tail, then copies the characters before i in front of\nit.","comment":"Expect '=' and evaluate the text"},
        "1053": {"comment":"i"},
        "1054": {"comment":"DE = the value's length byte"},
        "1058": {"comment":"i = 1: nothing before the slice"},
        "1062": {"comment":"i - 1 characters before it"},
        "1063": {"comment":"More than the string has?"},
        "1067": {"synopsis":"Copies the characters before i in front of the value's text.","comment":"HL = the last of them"},
        "1068": {"comment":"None?"},
        "1072": {"comment":"In front of the value"},
        "1074": {"synopsis":"Makes the head, the value and the tail one string entry.","comment":"The top before the tail was saved"},
        "1075": {"comment":"The joined length"},
        "1079": {"comment":"256 or more?"},
        "1084": {"comment":"255?"},
        "1089": {"comment":"HL = the new length byte"},
        "1092": {"comment":"Type 1: a string entry"},
        "1094": {"comment":"Is the new top"},
        "1098": {"synopsis":"Expects '=' and evaluates a number or a string, as the variable needs (EVAL_TYPED).","comment":"Expect '='"},
        "1103": {"comment":"Evaluate by bit 1 of $1701"},
        "1106": {"synopsis":"The LOMEM statement (token $E7): LOMEM address moves the program to start at the address.\nBelow $1720 (the lowest start) is error 4. Moving up checks the room (ROOM_CHECK) and opens\na gap in front of the program (MAKE_ROOM); moving down closes it (the SYS low chip's $DCE9,\nin DELETE_LINE). $1722 becomes the new start; in a running program the current line ($170C)\nand the text pointer move with it. RESET_RUN then clears the variables and the expression\nstack.","comment":"HL = the address"},
        "1109": {"comment":"Below the lowest start?"},
        "1120": {"comment":"HL = the distance from the current start"},
        "1126": {"comment":"Not moving: the end of the statement"},
        "1129": {"comment":"Keep the distance"},
        "1130": {"comment":"Moving down: HL = how far"},
        "1133": {"comment":"BC = how far"},
        "1135": {"comment":"HL = the current start"},
        "1138": {"comment":"Moving up: the distance"},
        "1141": {"comment":"Too far?"},
        "1145": {"comment":"Room for it?"},
        "1150": {"comment":"Open a gap in front of the program (MAKE_ROOM)"},
        "1154": {"comment":"HL = the new start"},
        "1157": {"synopsis":"Moving down: moves the program from its start down to the new one.","comment":"HL = the new start"},
        "1161": {"comment":"Move the program down (DELETE_LINE + 3)"},
        "1165": {"synopsis":"Records the new start and moves the running position with it.","comment":"The program's new start"},
        "1168": {"comment":"BC = the distance"},
        "1169": {"comment":"A program running?"},
        "1175": {"comment":"Move the current line"},
        "1183": {"comment":"And the text pointer"},
        "1187": {"synopsis":"Clears the variables (RESET_RUN) and ends the statement.","comment":"Clear the variables (RESET_RUN)"},
        "1193": {"synopsis":"Error 8, \"No FOR\": NEXT found no matching FOR entry.","comment":"Error $08: No FOR"},
        "1195": {"synopsis":"The loop has ended (its FOR entry stays dropped): a ',' takes the next variable, else the\nstatement ends.","comment":"The current token"},
        "1198": {"comment":"A ','?"},
        "1203": {"synopsis":"Fetches the next variable after a ',' and falls into NEXT_CMD.","comment":"The next variable"},
        "1206": {"synopsis":"The NEXT statement (token $E5): NEXT [v [, v ...]]; marks a program running (bit 2 of\n$1700).\n1. With a variable (numeric, else error 14 or 1), drops the FOR entries above the one for it\n   (FIND_STACK_TYPE finds each); without, takes the innermost. None: error 8.\n2. Copies the step and the variable's value into the entry's working space (+16), adds them\n   (CALC_ADD), stores the sum in the variable and compares it with the limit (NUM_COMPARE).\n3. Not past the limit (above it for a positive step, below it for a negative one): keeps the\n   entry and runs on after the FOR (its line into $170C). Past it: the entry stays dropped\n   and NEXT_DONE takes the next variable or ends the statement.","comment":"Running (and C: a variable follows)"},
        "1210": {"comment":"No variable: the innermost FOR"},
        "1212": {"comment":"A numeric variable?"},
        "1216": {"comment":"Not a variable at all?"},
        "1221": {"comment":"Error $0E: Type mismatch"},
        "1223": {"synopsis":"Finds the FOR entry for the variable, dropping inner ones (a jump to $04CC is ADD IY,BC:\ndrop one).","comment":"HL = the variable's address"},
        "1226": {"comment":"DE = it"},
        "1227": {"comment":"$04CC is ADD IY,BC: drops the entry just checked"},
        "1230": {"comment":"Find a FOR entry"},
        "1235": {"comment":"None: error 8"},
        "1237": {"comment":"HL = its variable's address field"},
        "1240": {"comment":"Its length, for the drop"},
        "1242": {"comment":"The same variable?"},
        "1251": {"synopsis":"Takes the FOR entry at the top (finding it when NEXT has no variable) and steps its\nvariable.","comment":"Find a FOR entry"},
        "1258": {"comment":"Push the text pointer after FOR"},
        "1263": {"comment":"The FOR's line"},
        "1268": {"comment":"The variable's address"},
        "1273": {"comment":"DE = the step entry (+7)"},
        "1275": {"comment":"IY = the limit (+34)"},
        "1280": {"comment":"Push the variable's value"},
        "1283": {"comment":"Copy the step below it"},
        "1293": {"comment":"Is the new top"},
        "1297": {"comment":"The step negative?"},
        "1302": {"comment":"Value + step"},
        "1306": {"comment":"HL = the variable's address"},
        "1307": {"comment":"Keep the sign test and the sum"},
        "1310": {"comment":"Store the sum in the variable"},
        "1315": {"comment":"Compare the sum with the limit (NUM_COMPARE)"},
        "1318": {"comment":"C = the sign test's flags"},
        "1319": {"comment":"The FOR's line and text pointer"},
        "1321": {"comment":"Carry: the step is negative"},
        "1323": {"comment":"Positive step"},
        "1325": {"comment":"Equal: loop again"},
        "1327": {"comment":"Negative step, past the limit"},
        "1330": {"comment":"Below it: make the next test fail"},
        "1331": {"synopsis":"A positive step: past the limit ends the loop.","comment":"Past the limit: the loop ends"},
        "1334": {"synopsis":"Loops again: the FOR's line becomes current, its entry is kept, and the body runs.","comment":"The FOR's line is current"},
        "1338": {"comment":"Keep the FOR entry (43 bytes)"},
        "1343": {"comment":"Run on after the FOR (STMT_LOOP)"},
        "1346": {"synopsis":"The OUT statement (token $E0): OUT port, value writes the byte to the I/O port.","comment":"The port"},
        "1350": {"comment":"Expect ','"},
        "1355": {"comment":"The value"},
        "1358": {"comment":"Write it"},
        "1363": {"synopsis":"The POKE statement (token $DE): POKE address, value. The address may be -32768 to 65535\n(ADDRESS_ARG). $C000-$FFFF, where SYS is paged in, reaches the video RAM: the byte goes to\naddress - $4000 with port 02h = $50 (video RAM in page 2). Afterwards port 02h is $70 (U2 in\npage 2) and interrupts are on.","comment":"HL = the address"},
        "1366": {"comment":"Expect ','"},
        "1371": {"comment":"The value"},
        "1375": {"comment":"$C000 up? HL - $4000, A = $50"},
        "1380": {"comment":"Video RAM into page 2"},
        "1383": {"synopsis":"Writes the byte and restores the paging (port 02h = $70).","comment":"Write the byte"},
        "1384": {"comment":"U2 into page 2"},
        "1392": {"synopsis":"The LPRINT statement (token $D0): PRINT to channel $40, the printer (the LD DE hides\nPRINT_CMD's LD C,$20).","comment":"Channel $40: the printer"},
        "1394": {"comment":"Hides PRINT_CMD: LD C,$20, channel $20 (the screen editor)"},
        "1395": {"synopsis":"The PRINT and OUTPUT statements (tokens $DD and $E1), and LPRINT from LPRINT_CMD with C =\n$40.\n1. Output goes to channel C ((ix+5)). Before the values, separated by ',': USING and a\n   format string (copied to the buffer at $1831, at most 127 characters; $172E/$1730 its\n   current position and end; bit 0 of A' says USING is on), AT row, column (the cursor,\n   through $068B), '#' and a channel (CHANNEL_HASH). A ':' introduces the values.\n2. The values follow from PRINT_VALUES: ',' moves to the next zone (character 9), ';' prints\n   nothing between them, TAB(n) moves to column n."},
        "1397": {"comment":"The output channel"},
        "1400": {"comment":"A' = 0: no USING, nothing given yet"},
        "1403": {"comment":"Skip the fetch: the first token is read"},
        "1404": {"synopsis":"One item before the values: USING, AT, a channel, or the first value.","comment":"Fetch the next token"},
        "1407": {"comment":"USING?"},
        "1411": {"comment":"Fetch the format's first token"},
        "1414": {"comment":"Evaluate a string"},
        "1417": {"comment":"The format buffer, at most 127 characters"},
        "1423": {"comment":"Copy the format into it"},
        "1427": {"comment":"E = its length"},
        "1431": {"comment":"The format's current position"},
        "1435": {"comment":"A $00 after its end"},
        "1437": {"comment":"The format's end"},
        "1440": {"comment":"USING on (bit 0 of A')"},
        "1446": {"synopsis":"AT row, column: positions the cursor ($068B).","comment":"AT?"},
        "1450": {"comment":"The row"},
        "1454": {"comment":"Expect ','"},
        "1459": {"comment":"The column"},
        "1463": {"comment":"Move the cursor there"},
        "1466": {"synopsis":"After USING, AT or a channel: a ',' takes another one, a ':' or the end starts the values.","comment":"The current token"},
        "1469": {"synopsis":"Clears F' carry (something given) and checks the separator.","comment":"F' carry clear: something given"},
        "1472": {"comment":"A ','?"},
        "1476": {"comment":"A ':'? The values follow"},
        "1480": {"comment":"The end of the line"},
        "1482": {"synopsis":"Error 1, \"Not understood\".","comment":"Error $01: Not understood"},
        "1484": {"synopsis":"A '#' and a channel (Z: given), or the first value.","comment":"A channel? (Z: given)"},
        "1489": {"comment":"A value where none may be?"},
        "1493": {"comment":"Skip the fetch"},
        "1494": {"synopsis":"After the ':': fetches the first value.","comment":"Fetch the token after the ':'"},
        "1497": {"synopsis":"The values start: F' carry set.","comment":"F' carry: values"},
        "1500": {"synopsis":"One value or separator. With USING on, the format's text up to its next field is printed\nfirst ($069C); then ',' ';' TAB or a value.","comment":"USING on?"},
        "1504": {"comment":"Print the format's text up to a field"},
        "1509": {"comment":"The current token"},
        "1512": {"comment":"A ','?"},
        "1516": {"comment":"To the next print zone"},
        "1521": {"comment":"Never jumps (Z from the ','): skips the CP"},
        "1524": {"comment":"Not ';'?"},
        "1526": {"comment":"The next token"},
        "1529": {"comment":"F' carry clear: a separator last"},
        "1534": {"synopsis":"TAB(n): moves to column n ($068B with C = 0); anything else is a value or the end.","comment":"TAB?"},
        "1536": {"comment":"Not TAB: an item or the statement's end"},
        "1538": {"comment":"TAB needs '('"},
        "1543": {"comment":"The column, 0-255"},
        "1547": {"comment":"Then ')'"},
        "1552": {"comment":"Row 0: keep the row"},
        "1554": {"comment":"Move the cursor to the column"},
        "1557": {"comment":"Counts as an item"},
        "1559": {"synopsis":"The PRINT statement's next item: the statement's end goes to PRINT_END; otherwise, with\nUSING (bit 0 of A'), the format text before the next format character is printed first,\nstarting the format again from its beginning ($1833) when it is used up; then the item is\nprinted as a string or a number.","comment":"':', '!' or the line's end?"},
        "1563": {"comment":"Bit 0 of A': USING active?"},
        "1566": {"comment":"Keep the USING flag for after the item"},
        "1570": {"comment":"The format pointer and its end"},
        "1577": {"comment":"Format text left?"},
        "1582": {"comment":"No: start the format again"},
        "1588": {"comment":"Print its literal text"},
        "1592": {"synopsis":"No USING: swaps AF back."},
        "1593": {"synopsis":"Prints one item: a string (token 0 or 1, a string literal or variable) or a number (token 2\nor more).","comment":"The current token"},
        "1596": {"comment":"A number or numeric variable?"},
        "1600": {"comment":"A string expression onto the IY stack"},
        "1603": {"comment":"Bit 7 of the next token: a separator or keyword?"},
        "1606": {"comment":"USING?"},
        "1607": {"comment":"EXT's $FF9C prints the string by the format"},
        "1610": {"comment":"Through EXTH_JUMP: EXT in page 3"},
        "1613": {"comment":"No padding"},
        "1615": {"comment":"HL = the string: length byte, then text"},
        "1620": {"comment":"Print it"},
        "1623": {"comment":"Drop the string from the IY stack"},
        "1626": {"synopsis":"Prints the number of spaces counted on the stack (none: PRINT_EXPR enters with $FF), then\ngoes on with PRINT_ITEM_DONE."},
        "1627": {"comment":"Spaces done?"},
        "1632": {"comment":"One space"},
        "1637": {"synopsis":"A numeric item: evaluates it and prints it with a sign place (a space for a positive number)\nand a space after, or by the format when USING is active.","comment":"Evaluate a numeric expression"},
        "1640": {"comment":"Bit 7 of the next token: a separator or keyword?"},
        "1641": {"synopsis":"An item must be followed by a token from $80 up (a separator or keyword), else error 1, \"Not\nunderstood\".","comment":"No: error 1, \"Not understood\""},
        "1644": {"comment":"USING?"},
        "1645": {"comment":"EXT's $FCB5 prints the number by the format"},
        "1648": {"comment":"Through EXTH_JUMP: EXT in page 3"},
        "1651": {"comment":"HL = the number's entry + 1"},
        "1655": {"comment":"Print it with its spaces (via NUM_TO_TEXT)"},
        "1658": {"comment":"Drop it from the IY stack"},
        "1661": {"synopsis":"An item was printed: sets carry in F', so the statement ends with a new line unless a\nseparator follows."},
        "1663": {"synopsis":"Back to the separator test of the PRINT loop at $05DC."},
        "1667": {"synopsis":"The end of the PRINT statement: a new line if the last item had no separator after it (carry\nin F'), then the statement's end (STMT_END's test at $DBB4, the token in A)."},
        "1668": {"comment":"CR LF when the last item had no separator"},
        "1672": {"comment":"STMT_END's test, the token in A"},
        "1675": {"synopsis":"Moves the output channel's cursor to column B, row C (0 keeps that coordinate, as\nGFX_CURSOR_AT in the SYS low chip documents), through function 3 of the channel; only for\nchannel $20 (the screen editor) and $00 (the graphics screen), other channels ignore it.\nPRINT AT and TAB use it.\nEntry: B = column, C = row.\nChanges: AF.","comment":"The output channel"},
        "1678": {"comment":"The screen editor?"},
        "1682": {"comment":"The graphics screen?"},
        "1684": {"comment":"Other channels: ignored"},
        "1685": {"synopsis":"Calls the channel's function 3 and raises the error it returns.","comment":"Function 3 of the channel"},
        "1687": {"comment":"System call with code A"},
        "1690": {"comment":"Raise the error it returned"},
        "1692": {"synopsis":"PRINT USING: prints the format string's literal characters from the format pointer ($172E)\nup to the next format character (one of USING_CHARS) or the format's end ($1730), advancing\nthe pointer.\nExit: Z = stopped at a format character (HL = it); no carry = the format's end.","comment":"The format's end"},
        "1696": {"comment":"The format pointer"},
        "1699": {"synopsis":"Next character: at the end, or a format character, stop; else print it."},
        "1701": {"comment":"At the format's end?"},
        "1706": {"comment":"Is it a format character?"},
        "1715": {"comment":"Yes: stop here"},
        "1716": {"comment":"No: print it literally"},
        "1719": {"comment":"Past it"},
        "1725": {"synopsis":"The ten format characters of PRINT USING, \"<>#*%+-$^.\", searched by USING_LITERAL ($06AA,\nCPIR over 10 bytes): the literal text of a format ends at any of them."},
        "1735": {"synopsis":"The RANDOMIZE statement (token $DC): seeds the random number generator from the R register\n($1709) and the frame counter $0B1D ($170A-$170B), steps it once (RANDOM_STEP) and ends the\nstatement.","comment":"R as the seed's first byte"},
        "1740": {"comment":"The frame counter as its other two"},
        "1746": {"comment":"Step the generator once"},
        "1752": {"synopsis":"The random number generator: shifts the 24-bit seed (A at $1709 above HL at $170A) left 16\ntimes; each new bit 0 is bit 1 XOR bit 6 of the top byte. The SYS low chip's TITLE_COLOUR\nand the RND function call it.\nExit: A, HL = the new seed (also in $1709-$170B).\nChanges: AF, BC, HL.","comment":"16 shifts"},
        "1754": {"comment":"The seed's top byte"},
        "1757": {"comment":"Its lower two bytes"},
        "1760": {"synopsis":"One shift: the feedback bit into HL's bit 0, HL's bit 15 into A's bit 0."},
        "1761": {"comment":"Bit 1 rotated to bit 6"},
        "1764": {"comment":"...XORed with bit 6"},
        "1765": {"comment":"The result's bit 6 into carry"},
        "1767": {"comment":"HL left, the new bit in"},
        "1769": {"comment":"A left, HL's old bit 15 in"},
        "1773": {"comment":"Store the new seed"},
        "1780": {"synopsis":"The RESTORE statement (token $DA): sets the DATA pointer $1712 to the program's start, or to\nthe line given (FIND_LINE_ARG: error 2, \"Line missing\", when it is not there), clears $1714\nand ends the statement.\nEntry: A = the token after RESTORE.","comment":"The program's start"},
        "1783": {"comment":"A line number follows?"},
        "1787": {"comment":"Find that line (error 2 if missing)"},
        "1790": {"comment":"The next token"},
        "1793": {"synopsis":"Stores the DATA pointer and ends the statement.","comment":"The DATA pointer"},
        "1796": {"comment":"Clear $1714 (as RESET_RUN does)"},
        "1802": {"comment":"STMT_END's test, the token in A"},
        "1805": {"synopsis":"Error 9, \"No GOSUB\": RETURN found no GOSUB entry.","comment":"Error 9, \"No GOSUB\""},
        "1807": {"synopsis":"The RETURN statement (token $D9): drops the IY stack's entries down to the newest GOSUB\nentry (type $06; error 9, \"No GOSUB\", when there is none) and goes back to where it was\nmade:\n1. Marks a program running (bit 2 of $1700).\n2. Takes from the entry the text pointer (IY+1), the current line (IY+3, into $170C) and the\n   byte at IY+5 (into $1702), as GOSUB stored them.\n3. Drops the entry and goes on at the statement loop (STMT_LOOP) with the text pointer in\n   HL.","comment":"A GOSUB entry's type"},
        "1809": {"comment":"Drop entries down to it"},
        "1812": {"comment":"The stack's bottom: error 9"},
        "1814": {"comment":"A program is running"},
        "1818": {"comment":"The text pointer after the GOSUB"},
        "1823": {"comment":"The line it was on"},
        "1826": {"comment":"...is the current line again"},
        "1831": {"comment":"The byte GOSUB saved from $1702"},
        "1835": {"comment":"Past the entry: dropped"},
        "1839": {"comment":"HL = the text pointer"},
        "1840": {"comment":"Go on after the GOSUB (STMT_LOOP)"},
        "1843": {"synopsis":"The GRAPHICS statement (token $EE): GRAPHICS 2, 4 or 16 sets the screen mode 0, 1 or 2 (2, 4\nor 16 colours) through device 0 function 4 (the SYS low chip's GFX_SET_MODE), which also\nclears the screen; any other number is error 4, \"Bad argument\".","comment":"The number of colours, 0-255"},
        "1846": {"comment":"Mode 0"},
        "1848": {"comment":"...for 2 colours"},
        "1852": {"comment":"Mode 1"},
        "1853": {"comment":"...for 4 colours"},
        "1857": {"comment":"Mode 2"},
        "1858": {"comment":"...for 16, else error 4, \"Bad argument\""},
        "1863": {"synopsis":"Sets the mode in C.","comment":"Device 0 function 4: set the mode C"},
        "1865": {"comment":"Raise the error it returned"},
        "1866": {"comment":"End the statement"},
        "1869": {"synopsis":"After ';': plots the point and puts the pen down (device 0 function 8), so the move to the\nnext point draws a line.","comment":"Plot the point, pen down"},
        "1871": {"synopsis":"Fetches the next token: the statement's end finishes PLOT, anything else is the next item.","comment":"Past the separator"},
        "1874": {"comment":"The end: finish"},
        "1876": {"synopsis":"The PLOT statement (token $DF): a list of points x,y separated by ';' (the next move draws a\nline: the pen goes down), ',' (the next move does not draw: the pen goes up) or nothing, and\nPAINT, through device 0's functions (the SYS low chip's GFX_FUNCS):\n1. PLOT alone plots the current point (function 8) and lifts the pen (function 9).\n2. PAINT fills the area around the current point (function A).\n3. A point x,y moves there (function 6), drawing a line when the pen is down.\n4. After a point or PAINT, ';' plots it with the pen down, ',' or no separator lifts the pen\n   (no separator also plots it), and PLOT goes on while the statement goes on.\nEntry: carry = something follows the keyword (the statement loop's test).","comment":"Nothing follows: plot and lift the pen"},
        "1878": {"comment":"PAINT?"},
        "1882": {"comment":"Device 0 function A: paint"},
        "1884": {"comment":"Raise the error it returned"},
        "1887": {"synopsis":"Not PAINT: a separator, or a point x,y.","comment":"';' with no point: plot, pen down"},
        "1891": {"comment":"',' with no point: lift the pen"},
        "1895": {"comment":"x into BC"},
        "1900": {"comment":"Then ','"},
        "1905": {"comment":"y into DE"},
        "1909": {"comment":"Keep the next token"},
        "1910": {"comment":"Device 0 function 6: move or draw to BC,DE"},
        "1912": {"comment":"Raise the error it returned"},
        "1914": {"synopsis":"After a point or PAINT: ';' plots with the pen down, ',' lifts the pen, anything else plots\nthe point and lifts the pen.","comment":"';': plot, pen down, go on"},
        "1918": {"comment":"',': lift the pen"},
        "1922": {"synopsis":"Plots the current point (device 0 function 8), then falls into PLOT_PEN_UP.","comment":"Device 0 function 8: plot"},
        "1924": {"synopsis":"Lifts the pen (device 0 function 9) and goes on with the next item while the statement goes\non.","comment":"Device 0 function 9: pen up"},
        "1926": {"comment":"The current token"},
        "1929": {"comment":"More to come?"},
        "1933": {"synopsis":"Ends the statement (STMT_END's test at $DBB4, the token in A)."},
        "1936": {"synopsis":"The SET statement (token $D6): items separated by ';', each a keyword from SET_KEYWORDS or a\nsystem call:\n1. MODE, STYLE, INK, PAPER, DELAY, RATE n store n in a graphics or keyboard variable ($0B4B\n   + E, SET_VARIABLE); BORDER n stores 2n as the border colour $0B4F.\n2. CHARACTER c, b1, b2, ... defines character c through device 0 function B (the SYS low\n   chip's GFX_DEF_CHAR); PALETTE a, b, c, d sets the palette through device 0 function C\n   (GFX_SET_PALETTE).\n3. Otherwise SET n, followed by bytes or a string, makes system call n with DE pointing at\n   them (SET_CALL).","comment":"SET's keywords"},
        "1939": {"comment":"A keyword item handled? On to ';'"},
        "1944": {"comment":"SET n: the system call code"},
        "1947": {"synopsis":"Makes the system call in A with the data that follow in a buffer at $1941: up to 127 values,\neach a byte or a string, then zeros; the first value needs a ',' before it unless E is 1 on\nentry.\n1. Puts the code into the RST $30 at RAM $001E (its code byte $001F).\n2. A string value is copied to $1940 (length byte, then text, at most 127 characters) and\n   ends the list.\n3. Byte values go to $1941 on; a byte of zero ends them.\n4. Calls RST $30 with DE = $1941 and C as the caller set it, raises the error it returns and\n   goes on with the next item after ';'.\nEntry: A = the system call code; C = its C argument (SET CHARACTER's code).","comment":"A ',' before the first value"},
        "1949": {"synopsis":"SET_CALL with E = 1: no ',' before the first value (SET PALETTE).","comment":"The code into the RST $30 at RAM $001E"},
        "1952": {"comment":"The data buffer"},
        "1955": {"comment":"Is DE for the call"},
        "1956": {"comment":"At most 127 values"},
        "1958": {"synopsis":"The next value: needs a ',' before it unless E says it is the first one.","comment":"The first value of SET PALETTE?"},
        "1962": {"comment":"The current token"},
        "1965": {"comment":"No ',': the values end"},
        "1969": {"comment":"Past the ','"},
        "1972": {"synopsis":"A value: a string (token 0 or 1) or a byte.","comment":"A number?"},
        "1976": {"comment":"No: a string expression"},
        "1979": {"comment":"At most 127 characters"},
        "1984": {"comment":"...copied to $1940 (length, text)"},
        "1989": {"synopsis":"A byte value into the buffer.","comment":"The byte, 0-255"},
        "1994": {"comment":"Up to 127 of them"},
        "1996": {"comment":"Then one zero"},
        "1997": {"synopsis":"Fills the rest of the buffer with zeros."},
        "2002": {"synopsis":"Makes the system call with DE = $1941.","comment":"DE = the buffer"},
        "2003": {"comment":"The system call, its code at $001F"},
        "2006": {"comment":"Raise the error it returned"},
        "2010": {"synopsis":"';' starts the next item, anything else ends the statement.","comment":"';'? Else end the statement"},
        "2015": {"comment":"Past the ';'"},
        "2018": {"comment":"The next item"},
        "2020": {"synopsis":"SET's keywords for KEYWORD_DISPATCH: pairs of a token and an offset byte, ended by\n0. The routine for an entry is at the entry's end plus the offset: MODE $07F7, STYLE $07FA,\n   INK $07FD, PAPER $0800, DELAY $0803, RATE $0806, CHARACTER $0816, PALETTE $0821, BORDER\n   $082A. Read by SET_CMD ($0793)."},
        "2039": {"synopsis":"SET MODE n: $0B4B, the put mode. MODE, STYLE, INK, PAPER, DELAY and RATE each load E (the\nfollowing LD HL,nn hide the next ones' LD E,n) and go on at SET_VARIABLE.","comment":"MODE: $0B4B, the put mode"},
        "2041": {"comment":"STYLE ($07FA): $0B4C, the line style"},
        "2042": {"synopsis":"SET STYLE n: $0B4C, the line style (entered inside the LD HL above it)."},
        "2044": {"comment":"INK ($07FD): $0B4D, the ink colour"},
        "2045": {"synopsis":"SET INK n: $0B4D, the ink colour (entered inside the LD HL above it)."},
        "2047": {"comment":"PAPER ($0800): $0B4E, the paper colour"},
        "2048": {"synopsis":"SET PAPER n: $0B4E, the paper colour (entered inside the LD HL above it)."},
        "2050": {"comment":"DELAY ($0803): $0B65, the key repeat delay"},
        "2051": {"synopsis":"SET DELAY n: $0B65, the keyboard's repeat delay (entered inside the LD HL above it)."},
        "2053": {"comment":"RATE ($0806): $0B67, the key repeat rate"},
        "2054": {"synopsis":"SET RATE n: $0B67, the keyboard's repeat rate (entered inside the LD HL above it)."},
        "2056": {"synopsis":"Stores a byte value in $0B4B + E and returns the current token to KEYWORD_DISPATCH.\nEntry: E = the variable's offset."},
        "2058": {"comment":"$0B4B + E"},
        "2062": {"comment":"The value, 0-255"},
        "2066": {"synopsis":"Returns the current token in A.","comment":"The current token"},
        "2070": {"synopsis":"SET CHARACTER c, rows...: drops KEYWORD_DISPATCH's two returns and defines character c\nthrough device 0 function B (GFX_DEF_CHAR) with the rows from SET_CALL.","comment":"Drop KEYWORD_DISPATCH's returns: SET_CALL ends it"},
        "2072": {"comment":"The character code into C"},
        "2076": {"comment":"Device 0 function B: define a character"},
        "2081": {"synopsis":"SET PALETTE a, b, c, d: drops KEYWORD_DISPATCH's two returns and sets the palette through\ndevice 0 function C (GFX_SET_PALETTE) with the bytes from SET_CALL, no ',' before the first.","comment":"Drop KEYWORD_DISPATCH's returns: SET_CALL ends it"},
        "2083": {"comment":"Device 0 function C: the palette"},
        "2085": {"comment":"No ',' before the first value"},
        "2090": {"synopsis":"SET BORDER n: 2n into $0B4F, the border colour (the interrupt writes it to port 00h).","comment":"The colour"},
        "2093": {"comment":"Doubled: port 00h's colour bits"},
        "2099": {"synopsis":"The SOUND statement (token $D5): plays sounds through device 3 function 3 (the SYS low\nchip's SND_START):\n1. Sets the defaults: pitch $0D15 ($172A), volume 7 ($002C) and duration 50 frames ($002D);\n   $0B15 = $FF, so the first sound starts at once.\n2. Takes PITCH, DURATION and VOLUME items (SOUND_KEYWORDS) separated by ','; a value without\n   a keyword is the pitch.\n3. Starts the sound and clears $0B15, so a later sound waits until the one playing has\n   (nearly) ended.\n4. ';' starts the next sound (a leading ';' only waits); the statement's end ends it.","comment":"The default pitch"},
        "2105": {"comment":"Default volume 7, duration 50"},
        "2111": {"comment":"$FF: do not wait for the sound playing"},
        "2116": {"comment":"First item: the token is fetched"},
        "2117": {"synopsis":"The next item: ';' waits, a keyword sets its value, ',' goes on to the next item; then the\nsound starts.","comment":"Past the separator"},
        "2120": {"comment":"';' with no sound: just wait"},
        "2124": {"comment":"SOUND's keywords"},
        "2130": {"comment":"',': the next item"},
        "2134": {"comment":"Keep the token"},
        "2135": {"comment":"The pitch"},
        "2139": {"comment":"C = the volume, B = the duration"},
        "2143": {"comment":"Device 3 function 3: start the sound"},
        "2145": {"comment":"Later sounds wait for this one"},
        "2150": {"comment":"The statement's end?"},
        "2154": {"comment":"';', else error 1, \"Not understood\""},
        "2159": {"synopsis":"';': the next sound waits for the one playing; ends the statement unless more follows."},
        "2163": {"comment":"Past the ';'"},
        "2166": {"comment":"More: the next sound"},
        "2168": {"synopsis":"Ends the statement (STMT_END's test at $DBB4, the token in A)."},
        "2171": {"synopsis":"SOUND's keywords for KEYWORD_DISPATCH, pairs of a token and an offset byte from the entry's\nend, ended by 0: PITCH $0882, DURATION $088B, VOLUME $088C. Read by SOUND_CMD ($084F)."},
        "2178": {"synopsis":"PITCH n (also a value without a keyword): the pitch, a number from 0 up, into $172A; a\nnegative one is error 4, \"Bad argument\".","comment":"The pitch as a whole number"},
        "2184": {"comment":"Not negative: done"},
        "2185": {"comment":"Error 4, \"Bad argument\""},
        "2187": {"synopsis":"DURATION n: the duration in frames into $002D. The OR $37 clears carry; SOUND_VOLUME enters\nat its second byte, SCF.","comment":"No carry: the duration; VOLUME enters at $088C"},
        "2188": {"synopsis":"VOLUME n: the volume into $002C (entered at the SCF inside the OR above)."},
        "2189": {"comment":"$002C, the volume"},
        "2192": {"comment":"...or $002D, the duration"},
        "2195": {"synopsis":"Stores the byte value at DE and returns the current token to KEYWORD_DISPATCH.","comment":"The value, 0-255"},
        "2199": {"comment":"The current token"},
        "2203": {"synopsis":"The CLOSE statement (token $FA): CLOSE [#n:] [INPUT | OUTPUT] closes a file through function\n4 of the channel (default 5, the cassette), on the input side (the RAM table $0B00, bit 7 of\nthe code) unless OUTPUT is given. Only channels $50 (the cassette) and $60 (a card) can be\nclosed; any other is error $FF.","comment":"Channel 5, the cassette, by default"},
        "2205": {"comment":"An optional '#n:' channel"},
        "2208": {"comment":"OUTPUT?"},
        "2212": {"comment":"Else the input side"},
        "2214": {"comment":"Nothing more: input"},
        "2218": {"comment":"INPUT, else error 1, \"Not understood\""},
        "2223": {"synopsis":"Past INPUT or OUTPUT."},
        "2226": {"synopsis":"Checks the channel: $50 or $60 only.","comment":"The channel without the side bit"},
        "2229": {"comment":"The cassette?"},
        "2233": {"comment":"A card?"},
        "2237": {"synopsis":"Calls the channel's function 4, the close.","comment":"Function 4: close"},
        "2240": {"comment":"System call with code A"},
        "2243": {"comment":"Raise the error it returned"},
        "2244": {"comment":"End the statement"},
        "2247": {"synopsis":"Error $FF (a system error, printed as \"System error 255\"): a channel that cannot hold a\nfile. CLOSE, OPEN, LOAD, SAVE and VERIFY use it.","comment":"Error $FF: not a file channel"},
        "2249": {"synopsis":"The OPEN statement (token $E2): OPEN [#n:] [INPUT | OUTPUT] [name] opens a file through\nfunction 3 of the channel (default 5, the cassette; only $50 and $60 can open, else error\n$FF), on the input side unless OUTPUT is given:\n1. The name is a string expression (left just below the IY stack, DE = its length byte), or\n   the empty name at $19CE.\n2. Stores the call code in $0B6B, calls it and raises the error it returns.","comment":"Channel 5, the cassette, by default"},
        "2251": {"comment":"An optional '#n:' channel"},
        "2254": {"comment":"Keep the token"},
        "2256": {"comment":"The cassette?"},
        "2260": {"comment":"A card? Else error $FF"},
        "2264": {"synopsis":"A file channel: function 3, the open.","comment":"Function 3: open"},
        "2268": {"comment":"The empty name by default"},
        "2271": {"comment":"OUTPUT?"},
        "2275": {"comment":"Else the input side"},
        "2277": {"comment":"A string follows at once: the name"},
        "2281": {"comment":"Nothing more: no name"},
        "2285": {"comment":"INPUT, else error 1, \"Not understood\""},
        "2290": {"synopsis":"Past INPUT or OUTPUT; a string may follow."},
        "2293": {"comment":"No string: no name"},
        "2297": {"synopsis":"The name: a string expression, dropped from the IY stack but still in memory below it."},
        "2298": {"comment":"The name onto the IY stack"},
        "2303": {"comment":"Drop it; its bytes stay"},
        "2307": {"comment":"DE = its length byte"},
        "2309": {"synopsis":"Makes the open call in C with DE = the name."},
        "2310": {"comment":"The open's call code into $0B6B"},
        "2313": {"comment":"System call with code A"},
        "2316": {"comment":"Raise the error it returned"},
        "2320": {"synopsis":"The GET statement (token $F1): GET [#n:] [var$] takes one character from the channel\n(default 1, the keyboard) through its function 1 on the input side, as a one-character\nstring (empty when the call returns $EC) on the IY stack, then assigns it to the string\nvariable, or drops it when none follows. Anything but a plain string variable is error 1,\n\"Not understood\".","comment":"Channel 1, the keyboard, by default"},
        "2322": {"comment":"An optional '#n:' channel"},
        "2325": {"comment":"Keep the token"},
        "2326": {"comment":"Function 1, input side: one character"},
        "2329": {"comment":"System call with code A; C = it"},
        "2332": {"comment":"HL = the top of the IY stack"},
        "2335": {"comment":"$EC: no character, an empty string"},
        "2339": {"comment":"Raise any other error"},
        "2341": {"comment":"The character"},
        "2343": {"comment":"...a string of length 1"},
        "2344": {"comment":"LD C,n hides the XOR A at $0929"},
        "2345": {"synopsis":"Result $EC: an empty string (entered at the XOR A inside the LD C above it)."},
        "2346": {"comment":"The length"},
        "2348": {"comment":"Type 1: a string entry"},
        "2351": {"comment":"The token after GET"},
        "2352": {"comment":"No variable: drop the string, end"},
        "2357": {"comment":"Push the string"},
        "2360": {"comment":"A string variable (token 1)?"},
        "2364": {"comment":"Bit 2 of C': not a plain variable?"},
        "2367": {"synopsis":"Error 1, \"Not understood\", unless the target is a plain string variable; then the string is\ncopied into it.","comment":"Not a string variable: error 1"},
        "2370": {"comment":"Bit 3 of C': create it (VAR_CREATE)"},
        "2376": {"comment":"The variable's address"},
        "2379": {"comment":"Copy the string into it"},
        "2385": {"synopsis":"The LOAD statement (token $E8): LOAD [#n:] [name] loads a BASIC program:\n1. Opens the file and reads its 16-byte header to $19DF (READ_HEADER; error $10, \"Bad file\",\n   when it is not a program).\n2. Clears the program (CLEAR_PROG), checks there is room for its length ($19E1; error 6, \"No\n   memory\") and reads that many bytes to the program's start through function 2 of the\n   channel (input side); after an error the program is cleared again.\n3. Resets the run state (RESET_RUN), closes the file and goes to COMMAND_LOOP, or runs the\n   program (RUN_CMD at $DE23) when the header's byte $19E3 is not zero.","comment":"Open the file, read its header"},
        "2388": {"comment":"Clear the program; HL = its start"},
        "2392": {"comment":"The program's length"},
        "2397": {"comment":"Room for it? Else error 6, \"No memory\""},
        "2400": {"comment":"BC = the length, DE = the start"},
        "2403": {"comment":"Function 2, input side: read a block"},
        "2408": {"comment":"System call with code A"},
        "2412": {"comment":"An error: clear the half-loaded program"},
        "2416": {"comment":"Raise the error it returned"},
        "2417": {"comment":"The program's end and run state"},
        "2420": {"comment":"Close the file"},
        "2424": {"comment":"The header's run flag"},
        "2428": {"comment":"Zero: back to the command loop"},
        "2431": {"comment":"Run it (RUN_CMD after its line search)"},
        "2434": {"synopsis":"The SAVE statement (token $D7): SAVE [#n:] [name] saves the BASIC program:\n1. Opens the file for writing (OPEN_FILE).\n2. Builds a 16-byte header at $19DF: zeros, $19E0 = 1 (a program), $19E1 = the program's\n   length with its end marker, $19E3 = $1707 (which is then cleared; LOAD runs the program\n   when it is not zero).\n3. Writes the header byte by byte through function 1 of the channel, then the program from\n   its start ($1722) as one block through function 2, closes the file and ends the\n   statement.","comment":"The output side"},
        "2435": {"comment":"Open the file"},
        "2438": {"comment":"Just past the 16-byte header"},
        "2444": {"synopsis":"Clears the header $19DF-$19EE."},
        "2448": {"comment":"DE = $19DF, the header"},
        "2449": {"comment":"$19E0 = 1: a BASIC program"},
        "2453": {"comment":"$19E3 = $1707, the run flag"},
        "2459": {"comment":"The program's end marker"},
        "2462": {"comment":"...counted in"},
        "2463": {"comment":"The program's start"},
        "2467": {"comment":"Clear $1707"},
        "2471": {"comment":"Its length"},
        "2473": {"comment":"...into the header"},
        "2476": {"comment":"HL = the header; length kept"},
        "2477": {"comment":"Keep the start"},
        "2478": {"comment":"16 header bytes"},
        "2480": {"comment":"Function 1: one character"},
        "2485": {"comment":"The code into the RST $30 at RAM $001E"},
        "2488": {"synopsis":"Writes the next header byte.","comment":"The byte in C"},
        "2491": {"comment":"The system call, its code at $001F"},
        "2494": {"comment":"Raise the error it returned"},
        "2498": {"comment":"DE = the start, BC = the length"},
        "2500": {"comment":"Function 2: write a block"},
        "2505": {"comment":"System call with code A"},
        "2508": {"comment":"Raise the error it returned"},
        "2509": {"comment":"Close the file"},
        "2512": {"comment":"End the statement"},
        "2515": {"synopsis":"The VERIFY statement (token $D2): VERIFY [#n:] [name] compares a saved program with the one\nin memory: reads the file's header (READ_HEADER), checks that its length matches the\nprogram's (error $10, \"Bad file\", if not), then has function 5 of the channel (input side)\ncompare the file's bytes with the program, closes the file and ends the statement.","comment":"Open the file, read its header"},
        "2518": {"comment":"The program's end marker"},
        "2521": {"comment":"...counted in"},
        "2522": {"comment":"The program's start"},
        "2529": {"comment":"The file's length"},
        "2533": {"comment":"The same length? Else error $10"},
        "2538": {"comment":"Function 5, input side: compare"},
        "2543": {"comment":"System call with code A"},
        "2546": {"comment":"Raise the error it returned"},
        "2547": {"comment":"Close the file"},
        "2553": {"synopsis":"OPEN_FILE for the input side (A = $80)."},
        "2555": {"synopsis":"Opens a file for LOAD, SAVE and VERIFY:\n1. Sets bit 3 of $1700 (a file is open: the error and break code close it, CLOSE_IF_OPEN).\n2. Takes an optional '#n:' channel (default 5, the cassette; only $50 and $60, else error\n   $FF) and makes it, with the side bit from A, the channel in $1705.\n3. Opens it with function 3, the name a string expression (DE = its length byte) or the\n   empty name at $19CE; clears $0B6B and raises the error the call returns.\nEntry: A = 0 for the output side, $80 for the input side.","comment":"A file is open"},
        "2559": {"comment":"Keep the side"},
        "2560": {"comment":"Channel 5, the cassette, by default"},
        "2562": {"comment":"An optional '#n:' channel"},
        "2565": {"comment":"Keep the token"},
        "2567": {"comment":"The cassette?"},
        "2571": {"comment":"A card? Else error $FF"},
        "2576": {"synopsis":"A file channel: adds the side bit and makes it the channel.","comment":"The side"},
        "2578": {"comment":"The file's channel"},
        "2581": {"comment":"Function 3: open"},
        "2583": {"comment":"The code into the RST $30 at RAM $001E"},
        "2587": {"comment":"The empty name by default"},
        "2590": {"comment":"A string: the name"},
        "2594": {"comment":"The name onto the IY stack"},
        "2599": {"comment":"Drop it; its bytes stay"},
        "2603": {"comment":"DE = its length byte"},
        "2604": {"synopsis":"Clears $0B6B and makes the open call."},
        "2608": {"comment":"The system call, its code at $001F"},
        "2611": {"comment":"Raise the error it returned"},
        "2613": {"synopsis":"Opens the file for reading (OPEN_FOR_READ) and reads its 16-byte header to $19DF through\nfunction 1 of the channel (input side): a BASIC program's has $19DF = 0 and $19E0 = 1, else\nerror $10, \"Bad file\". LOAD and VERIFY use it.","comment":"Open the file for reading"},
        "2616": {"comment":"Function 1, input side: one character"},
        "2621": {"comment":"The code into the RST $30 at RAM $001E"},
        "2624": {"comment":"The header's place"},
        "2628": {"comment":"16 bytes"},
        "2630": {"synopsis":"Reads the next header byte."},
        "2631": {"comment":"The system call, its code at $001F"},
        "2634": {"comment":"Raise the error it returned"},
        "2635": {"comment":"Store the byte"},
        "2641": {"comment":"$19DF zero?"},
        "2645": {"comment":"$19E0 one: a program?"},
        "2648": {"synopsis":"Error $10, \"Bad file\".","comment":"Error $10, \"Bad file\""},
        "2650": {"synopsis":"Closes the file of LOAD, SAVE and VERIFY: function 4 of the channel in $1705, raising its\nerror, then clears bit 3 of $1700 (no file open).\nChanges: AF.","comment":"The file's channel"},
        "2653": {"comment":"Function 4: close"},
        "2655": {"comment":"System call with code A"},
        "2658": {"comment":"Raise the error it returned"},
        "2659": {"comment":"No file open"},
        "2664": {"synopsis":"A function's numeric argument: '(' (error 1, \"Not understood\", if missing), a numeric\nexpression onto the IY stack, then ')'. Most numeric functions call it first. It is also\ncalculator operation 14 (CALC_OP_TABLE in the SYS low chip), where it reads the program text\nthe same way.\nExit: the value on the IY stack, the token after ')' in A.","comment":"'(' must come"},
        "2669": {"comment":"The numeric expression"},
        "2672": {"synopsis":"')' must come (error 1, \"Not understood\", if not); the next token is fetched.","comment":"')' must come"},
        "2677": {"synopsis":"A function's string argument: '(', a string expression onto the IY stack, then ')' (LEN, VAL\nand others).\nExit: the string on the IY stack, the token after ')' in A.","comment":"'(' must come"},
        "2682": {"comment":"The string expression"},
        "2685": {"comment":"')' must come"},
        "2690": {"synopsis":"Calculator operation 5: pushes constant k onto the IY stack, k being the next byte of the\noperation stream ($1718, the stream pointer), from NUM_CONSTANTS in the SYS low chip ($C111\n+ 7k).","comment":"The calculator's stream pointer"},
        "2693": {"comment":"k, the byte after the operation"},
        "2698": {"comment":"HL = k"},
        "2702": {"comment":"8k"},
        "2706": {"comment":"...- k = 7k"},
        "2708": {"comment":"The constant's address, $C111 + 7k"},
        "2714": {"synopsis":"Calculator operation 7: pushes the number kept at $19C7."},
        "2719": {"synopsis":"Calculator operation 6: pushes the number kept at $19C0."},
        "2722": {"synopsis":"Pushes the 7-byte number at HL onto the IY stack as a 9-byte entry: IY+0 = 9 (the type),\nIY+1..IY+6 = the six BCD bytes, IY+7 = 0 (the carry byte), IY+8 = the exponent. Error 6, \"No\nmemory\", without 256 bytes free.\nEntry: HL = the number.\nExit: IY = the new entry.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"Room? Else error 6, \"No memory\""},
        "2725": {"comment":"The number's last byte, the exponent"},
        "2729": {"comment":"Just below the stack's top"},
        "2733": {"comment":"The exponent to IY+8"},
        "2735": {"comment":"IY+7, the carry byte, = 0"},
        "2738": {"comment":"The six BCD bytes to IY+6..IY+1"},
        "2743": {"comment":"Type 9: a number"},
        "2746": {"comment":"The new top"},
        "2750": {"synopsis":"Calculator operation 11: moves the top number to $19C7 and drops it."},
        "2755": {"synopsis":"Calculator operation 10: moves the top number to $19C0 and drops it."},
        "2758": {"synopsis":"Stores the top number at DE (STORE_NUM7) and drops it from the IY stack.","comment":"Store it; HL = past the entry"},
        "2761": {"comment":"Drop it"},
        "2765": {"synopsis":"Calculator operation 9: stores a copy of the top number at $19C7."},
        "2770": {"synopsis":"Calculator operation 8: stores a copy of the top number at $19C0. Falls into STORE_NUM7."},
        "2773": {"synopsis":"Stores the IY stack's top number at DE as 7 bytes: the six BCD bytes (IY+1..IY+6), then the\nexponent (IY+8), leaving out IY+7, the carry byte.\nEntry: DE = where.\nExit: HL = IY + 9, past the entry; DE = past the stored number.\nChanges: BC, DE, HL.","comment":"The BCD bytes at IY+1"},
        "2777": {"comment":"Six of them"},
        "2782": {"comment":"Skip the carry byte; the exponent"},
        "2786": {"synopsis":"ABS's entry in the built-in function chain: link $0000 (the end of the chain), length 3,\n\"ABS\", type $0A (bit 3: built-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at ABS_FN."},
        "2793": {"synopsis":"The ABS function: takes the argument in brackets (CALC_PAREN_NUM) and clears the sign bit of\nits exponent byte.\nEntry: B' = the token after the name (\"(\").\nExit: |x| on the IY stack.","comment":"x = the number in brackets"},
        "2796": {"comment":"Positive: |x|"},
        "2801": {"synopsis":"The ATN function, reached through its token $B1 by the operand code at $F1C5 (not through\nthe chain). Works on |x| and gives the result the argument's sign at the end.\n1. x above 1 is replaced by 1/x (B = 2).\n2. A value above 2 - sqrt(3) (constant 2, tan 15 degrees) is mapped to (a*sqrt(3) - 1) / (a\n   + sqrt(3)) (B + 1), a*sqrt(3) worked out as 0.732a - 0.5 - 0.5 + a to keep digits.\n3. atan(t) = t + t * t^2 (c5 + c6 t^2) / (c7 + t^2 (c8 + t^2)), a rational approximation\n   with constants 5-8 (ATN_SERIES).\n4. B = 1 adds pi/6; B = 2 gives pi/2 - r; B = 3 pi/3 - r (ATN_ADD_ANGLE). Uses calculator\n   memory A ($19C0).\nExit: atan(x) on the IY stack.","comment":"Past the ATN token"},
        "2804": {"comment":"x = the number in brackets"},
        "2807": {"comment":"B = 0: no reduction yet"},
        "2810": {"comment":"C = the sign of x"},
        "2816": {"comment":"Keep the sign and the reduction count"},
        "2817": {"comment":"Negative: CALC_NEGATE, work on |x|"},
        "2820": {"comment":"Memory A = x, then push 1"},
        "2824": {"comment":"x against 1, both dropped (NUM_COMPARE)"},
        "2827": {"comment":"Below 1?"},
        "2830": {"comment":"Equal?"},
        "2832": {"comment":"B = 2: inverted"},
        "2836": {"comment":"Memory A = 1/x"},
        "2842": {"synopsis":"Compares the value in memory A with 2 - sqrt(3) (constant 2); above it, maps it to\n(a*sqrt(3) - 1) / (a + sqrt(3)) and counts one more in B.","comment":"Push memory A and 2 - sqrt(3)"},
        "2846": {"comment":"a against 2 - sqrt(3), both dropped (NUM_COMPARE)"},
        "2849": {"comment":"Below?"},
        "2852": {"comment":"Equal?"},
        "2855": {"comment":"B + 1: reduced by pi/6"},
        "2857": {"comment":"Memory A = (a*sqrt(3) - 1) / (a + sqrt(3))"},
        "2876": {"synopsis":"atan(t) for the reduced t in memory A: t + t * t^2 (c5 + c6 t^2) / (c7 + t^2 (c8 + t^2)),\nwith t^2 kept in memory A; then the angle correction for B and the sign.","comment":"t + t*t^2(c5 + c6 t^2) / (c7 + t^2(c8 + t^2))"},
        "2903": {"comment":"B = the reductions, C = the sign"},
        "2904": {"comment":"Inverted (B = 2 or 3)?"},
        "2907": {"comment":"Then the angle minus the result"},
        "2911": {"comment":"pi/6 (constant 37) for B = 1"},
        "2917": {"comment":"pi/2 (constant 35) for B = 2"},
        "2923": {"comment":"pi/3 (constant 36) for B = 3"},
        "2927": {"comment":"B = 0: no angle to add"},
        "2929": {"synopsis":"Pushes the angle constant at HL and adds it to the result.","comment":"Push the angle"},
        "2932": {"comment":"Add it"},
        "2935": {"synopsis":"Gives the result the argument's sign (C, bit 7) by XOR into the exponent byte."},
        "2936": {"comment":"The argument's sign"},
        "2937": {"comment":"into the result's exponent byte"},
        "2944": {"synopsis":"CHR$'s entry in the built-in function chain: link ABS_ENTRY, length 4, \"CHR$\", type $08 (bit\n3: built-in code, bit 1 clear: a string result). The code follows at CHRS_FN."},
        "2952": {"synopsis":"The CHR$ function: takes the number in brackets as a byte (TOP_TO_BYTE: error 4 above 255)\nand pushes a one-character string: a type byte 1, the length 1 and the character, written\nbelow IY.\nExit: the string on the IY stack.","comment":"The number in brackets"},
        "2955": {"comment":"A = it as a byte, dropped"},
        "2958": {"comment":"HL = the stack top"},
        "2962": {"comment":"The character"},
        "2964": {"comment":"The length, 1"},
        "2967": {"comment":"Type 1: a string"},
        "2969": {"comment":"The string is the new top"},
        "2973": {"synopsis":"COS's entry in the built-in function chain: link CHRS_ENTRY, length 3, \"COS\", type $0A (bit\n3: built-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at COS_FN."},
        "2980": {"synopsis":"The COS function: cos(x) = sin(x + pi/2), adding constant 35 (pi/2) and going on in\nSIN_BODY.","comment":"x = the number in brackets"},
        "2983": {"comment":"x + pi/2"},
        "2987": {"comment":"sin(x + pi/2)"},
        "2990": {"synopsis":"EXP's entry in the built-in function chain: link COS_ENTRY, length 3, \"EXP\", type $0A (bit\n3: built-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at EXP_FN."},
        "2997": {"synopsis":"The EXP function. EXP_BODY does the work and is also the end of POWER_OP.","comment":"x = the number in brackets"},
        "3000": {"synopsis":"e^x for the number on the IY stack.\n1. n = INT(x / (ln 10 / 2)) (constant 9 is 2 / ln 10), the number of half decades.\n2. |n| of 1000 or more, or n of 126 or more: error $0D for a positive x, 0 for a negative\n   one; n below -126 also gives 0 (EXP_ZERO).\n3. r = x - n * ln 10 / 2, with ln 10 / 2 in two parts (constants 10 and 11) to keep digits.\n4. e^r = 1 + 2rP / (Q - rP) with P = c12 + c13 r^2 + c14 r^4 and Q = c15 + c16 r^2 + r^4, a\n   rational approximation (EXP_SERIES).\n5. An odd n multiplies by sqrt(0.1) (constant 32) and counts n + 1; half of n is added to\n   the exponent byte (EXP_SCALE).\nUses calculator memories A and B.\nExit: e^x on the IY stack.","comment":"x and x * 2 / ln 10"},
        "3005": {"comment":"n = INT(2x / ln 10)"},
        "3008": {"comment":"C = n's exponent byte"},
        "3014": {"comment":"|n| below 1000?"},
        "3018": {"comment":"With the sign: positive?"},
        "3019": {"comment":"Error $0D: Overflow"},
        "3022": {"synopsis":"A result too small to hold: drops n and makes x zero, the result.","comment":"Drop n"},
        "3025": {"comment":"The result is 0"},
        "3028": {"synopsis":"Checks n as an integer: below -126 gives 0 (EXP_ZERO), 126 or more error $0D; keeps n in DE\nfor the scaling.","comment":"A copy of n"},
        "3031": {"comment":"HL = n, the copy dropped"},
        "3034": {"comment":"DE = n"},
        "3036": {"comment":"n + 126"},
        "3042": {"comment":"Below -126: the result is 0"},
        "3045": {"comment":"BC = 252"},
        "3047": {"comment":"n - 126"},
        "3049": {"comment":"126 or more: error $0D, Overflow"},
        "3052": {"comment":"Keep n"},
        "3053": {"synopsis":"The calculator works out r = x - n * ln 10 / 2 and e^r = 1 + 2rP / (Q - rP) into memory A (n\nin memory B, then Q; r^2 and rP in memory A).","comment":"Memory A = e^r, r = x - n*ln 10/2, by (Q+rP)/(Q-rP)"},
        "3101": {"synopsis":"Scales e^r in memory A by 10^(n/2): an odd n multiplies it by sqrt(0.1) and counts n + 1;\nthe half is added to the exponent byte ($19C6, 7 bits), then the result is pushed from\nmemory A.","comment":"DE = n"},
        "3102": {"comment":"Odd n?"},
        "3107": {"comment":"Memory A * sqrt(0.1)"},
        "3114": {"comment":"The test of D: n's sign"},
        "3116": {"comment":"n + 1, even now"},
        "3117": {"comment":"n from 0 up?"},
        "3119": {"comment":"Negative?"},
        "3122": {"comment":"Not reached for n below 126"},
        "3127": {"synopsis":"Adds half of E (n made even) to memory A's exponent byte, keeping 7 bits, and pushes memory\nA as the result (calculator operation 6's routine).","comment":"Memory A's exponent"},
        "3130": {"comment":"A = E / 2 (carry clear)"},
        "3133": {"comment":"Times 10^(n/2)"},
        "3134": {"comment":"The sign bit stays clear"},
        "3137": {"comment":"Push memory A: the result"},
        "3140": {"synopsis":"FREE's entry in the built-in function chain: link EXP_ENTRY, length 4, \"FREE\", type $0A (bit\n3: built-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at FREE_FN."},
        "3148": {"synopsis":"The FREE function, without an argument: the free memory, IY (the expression stack) - ($1726)\n(the end of the variables) - 256, pushed as 32767 + (free - 32767) so that a value up to\n65535 fits the signed PUSH_INT. Also called by the SYS low chip at $DAB7.\nExit: the number of free bytes on the IY stack.","comment":"HL = the expression stack's top"},
        "3151": {"comment":"The end of the variables"},
        "3156": {"comment":"The gap between them"},
        "3158": {"comment":"Less 256 kept free"},
        "3163": {"comment":"Less 32767"},
        "3168": {"comment":"Push 32767"},
        "3172": {"comment":"Push free - 32767"},
        "3176": {"comment":"Add them: the free bytes"},
        "3179": {"synopsis":"IN's entry in the built-in function chain: link FREE_ENTRY, length 2, \"IN\", type $0A (bit 3:\nbuilt-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at IN_FN."},
        "3185": {"synopsis":"The IN function: reads the port given in brackets (0-255) and pushes the byte.","comment":"The number in brackets"},
        "3188": {"comment":"A = it as a byte, dropped"},
        "3191": {"comment":"C = the port"},
        "3192": {"comment":"Read it"},
        "3196": {"comment":"Push the byte"},
        "3199": {"synopsis":"After READ_NUMBER has read a number from text (with its flags restored): when a number was\nread (carry) and its exponent overflowed (bit 7 of A, D bit 6), replaces it with the largest\nnumber, 9.999999999E62 (constant 39). Called from $E2A8 and VAL_FN.\nEntry: F and A as READ_NUMBER returned them.","comment":"No number read?"},
        "3200": {"comment":"Exponent overflow (D bit 6)?"},
        "3201": {"comment":"No: keep it"},
        "3202": {"comment":"Drop it"},
        "3205": {"comment":"Push 9.999999999E62"},
        "3209": {"synopsis":"The INKEY$ function, reached through its token $C5 by the operand code at $F348 (not through\nthe chain). Asks the keyboard whether a key is waiting (RST $30 code $93); if one is, takes\nit (code $91) and pushes it as a one-character string, otherwise an empty string.\nExit: the string on the IY stack; a system call's error is raised by RST $10.","comment":"Past the INKEY$ token"},
        "3214": {"comment":"Raise an error the call returned"},
        "3215": {"comment":"HL = the stack top"},
        "3219": {"comment":"C = $FF when a key is waiting"},
        "3221": {"comment":"None: an empty string"},
        "3223": {"comment":"Take the key into C"},
        "3226": {"comment":"The character"},
        "3228": {"comment":"Length 1"},
        "3229": {"synopsis":"Writes the length (A) and the type byte 1 below the character and makes the string the new\ntop.","comment":"The length: 0 or 1"},
        "3231": {"comment":"Type 1: a string"},
        "3237": {"synopsis":"INT's entry in the built-in function chain: link IN_ENTRY, length 3, \"INT\", type $0A (bit 3:\nbuilt-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at INT_FN."},
        "3244": {"synopsis":"The INT function: the largest integer not above x. INT_BODY does the work.","comment":"x = the number in brackets"},
        "3247": {"synopsis":"Rounds the number on the IY stack down to an integer (towards minus infinity). Below 1 in\nsize: 0, or -1 for a negative number (INT_SMALL). Otherwise a negative number is turned into\nits ten's complement (COMPL_IF_NEG), the digits after the integer part are cleared\n(SET_DIGIT for each position up to 15), and COMPL_IF_NEG turns it back, so truncating the\ncomplement rounds down; NORMALIZE normalises the result. Also called by EXP_BODY and\nMOD_TOP.","comment":"The exponent without the sign"},
        "3252": {"comment":"Below 1 in size?"},
        "3256": {"comment":"A = the digits before the point"},
        "3258": {"comment":"Ten's complement when negative"},
        "3261": {"comment":"Zero test"},
        "3265": {"comment":"B = the first digit position to clear"},
        "3268": {"synopsis":"Clears digit position B through SET_DIGIT, up to position 15.","comment":"A 0 digit"},
        "3270": {"comment":"Clear position B"},
        "3273": {"comment":"The next position"},
        "3275": {"comment":"Up to 15"},
        "3279": {"comment":"Back from the complement"},
        "3285": {"comment":"NORMALIZE the result"},
        "3288": {"synopsis":"INT of a number below 1 in size: 0 when positive, -1 when negative.","comment":"Positive?"},
        "3292": {"comment":"Then 0"},
        "3295": {"comment":"Make it 1"},
        "3298": {"comment":"and -1"},
        "3301": {"synopsis":"IO's entry in the built-in function chain: link INT_ENTRY, length 2, \"IO\", type $0A (bit 3:\nbuilt-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at IO_FN."},
        "3307": {"synopsis":"The IO function: pushes the word at RAM $1941 + 2n for the n in brackets (0-255): the table\nat $1941 that the SET statement's code ($E7A0) also addresses.","comment":"The number in brackets"},
        "3310": {"comment":"A = n, dropped"},
        "3316": {"comment":"The word table"},
        "3319": {"comment":"Two bytes an entry"},
        "3321": {"comment":"HL = word n"},
        "3325": {"comment":"Push it as a number"},
        "3328": {"synopsis":"LEN's entry in the built-in function chain: link IO_ENTRY, length 3, \"LEN\", type $0A (bit 3:\nbuilt-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at LEN_FN."},
        "3335": {"synopsis":"The LEN function: takes the string in brackets (PAREN_STRING), drops it and pushes its\nlength. LEN_PUSH is shared by ORD_FN, which enters one byte earlier to push the first\ncharacter.","comment":"The string in brackets"},
        "3338": {"comment":"Its address"},
        "3340": {"comment":"Drop the string"},
        "3344": {"synopsis":"Steps past the string's type byte (or, from ORD_FN, its length byte) and pushes the byte\nthere as a number.","comment":"The length (from ORD_FN: the first character)"},
        "3346": {"synopsis":"Pushes the byte in L as a number. Also the end of PEEK_FN.","comment":"HL = the byte"},
        "3348": {"comment":"Push it"},
        "3351": {"synopsis":"LOG's entry in the built-in function chain: link LEN_ENTRY, length 3, \"LOG\", type $0A (bit\n3: built-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at LOG_FN."},
        "3358": {"synopsis":"The LOG function, the natural logarithm. LOG_BODY does the work and is also used by\nPOWER_OP.","comment":"x = the number in brackets"},
        "3361": {"synopsis":"ln x for the number on the IY stack.\n1. x goes to memory A; zero or negative: error 4.\n2. Its exponent is replaced by $3F, so the mantissa m lies in [0.1, 1); below sqrt(0.1)\n   (constant 32) it is made ten times bigger, so m lies in [0.316, 3.16).\n3. With t = (m - 1) / (m + 1): log10 m = t (2 / ln 10 + t^2 P / Q), P and Q polynomials in\n   t^2 (constants 18-20 and 21-23, constant 24 = 2 / ln 10), a rational approximation\n   (LOG_SERIES).\n4. Adds the decimal exponent and multiplies by ln 10 (constant 17).\nExit: ln x on the IY stack.","comment":"Memory A = x, dropped"},
        "3364": {"comment":"The top BCD byte of x"},
        "3369": {"comment":"Zero?"},
        "3370": {"comment":"Error 4"},
        "3373": {"comment":"E = x's exponent"},
        "3376": {"comment":"Negative?"},
        "3380": {"comment":"m = the mantissa, 0.1 to 1"},
        "3382": {"comment":"Keep the exponent"},
        "3384": {"comment":"Push m and sqrt(0.1)"},
        "3388": {"comment":"m against sqrt(0.1), both dropped (NUM_COMPARE)"},
        "3392": {"comment":"Equal?"},
        "3394": {"comment":"Above?"},
        "3397": {"synopsis":"m is not above sqrt(0.1): multiplies it by 10 (exponent $40) and counts the exponent one\nlower.","comment":"The exponent one lower"},
        "3399": {"comment":"m * 10"},
        "3401": {"synopsis":"log10 m into the IY stack: t = (m - 1) / (m + 1), then t (2 / ln 10 + t^2 P / Q). Then adds\nthe exponent ($3F based) and multiplies by ln 10.","comment":"log10 m = t(2/ln 10 + t^2 P/Q), t = (m-1)/(m+1)"},
        "3451": {"comment":"HL = the exponent byte"},
        "3452": {"comment":"The decimal exponent"},
        "3458": {"comment":"Push it"},
        "3461": {"comment":"(log10 m + exponent) * ln 10"},
        "3467": {"synopsis":"Reduces y modulo x for the two numbers on the IY stack: y - x * INT(y / x), with x kept in\nmemory A; a result as big as x (a rounding) becomes 0. Called by SIN_BODY with x = 2 pi.\nEntry: y, then x on top.\nExit: the remainder (0 <= r < x for a positive x) on top.","comment":"Copy both"},
        "3470": {"comment":"Memory A = x"},
        "3473": {"comment":"y / x"},
        "3476": {"comment":"INT(y / x)"},
        "3479": {"comment":"r = y - x*INT(y/x), twice"},
        "3483": {"comment":"|r|"},
        "3487": {"comment":"Push x"},
        "3490": {"comment":"|r| against x, both dropped (NUM_COMPARE)"},
        "3493": {"comment":"Below: r stays"},
        "3494": {"comment":"A rounding: r = 0"},
        "3497": {"synopsis":"The ORD function, reached through its token $C0 by the operand code at $F1C0 (not through\nthe chain): pushes the code of the first character of the string in brackets; an empty\nstring is error 4.","comment":"Past the ORD token"},
        "3500": {"comment":"The string in brackets"},
        "3503": {"comment":"Its address"},
        "3505": {"comment":"Drop the string"},
        "3509": {"comment":"Its length"},
        "3511": {"comment":"Empty?"},
        "3512": {"comment":"Error 4"},
        "3515": {"comment":"Push the first character"},
        "3518": {"synopsis":"PEEK's entry in the built-in function chain: link LOG_ENTRY, length 4, \"PEEK\", type $0A (bit\n3: built-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at PEEK_FN."},
        "3526": {"synopsis":"The PEEK function: pushes the byte at the address in brackets (TOP_TO_ADDRESS takes -32768\nto 65535). An address from $C000 up is read with port 02h = $50 (video RAM in page 2) at the\naddress less $4000 (VIDEO_ADDRESS), with interrupts off, then port 02h is set back to $70.","comment":"The number in brackets"},
        "3529": {"comment":"HL = the address, dropped"},
        "3532": {"comment":"From $C000: A = $50, HL - $4000"},
        "3535": {"comment":"Below $C000: read it as it is"},
        "3537": {"comment":"Video RAM into page 2"},
        "3540": {"synopsis":"Reads the byte, sets port 02h to $70 and pushes the byte.","comment":"The byte"},
        "3541": {"comment":"The usual paging back"},
        "3546": {"comment":"Push it"},
        "3549": {"synopsis":"PI's entry in the built-in function chain: link PEEK_ENTRY, length 2, \"PI\", type $0A (bit 3:\nbuilt-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at PI_FN."},
        "3555": {"synopsis":"The PI function, without an argument: pushes constant 34, pi.","comment":"Push pi"},
        "3559": {"synopsis":"RND's entry in the built-in function chain: link PI_ENTRY, length 3, \"RND\", type $0A (bit 3:\nbuilt-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at RND_FN."},
        "3566": {"synopsis":"The RND function. Without brackets: the random generator at RANDOM_STEP gives 16 bits in HL,\npushed as -32768 to 32767, and the calculator makes (h + 32768) / 65536, from 0 up to below\n1. With a number n in brackets (1-32767, else error 4): RND_INT.","comment":"\"(\": an integer range"},
        "3570": {"comment":"HL = 16 random bits"},
        "3573": {"comment":"Push them, signed"},
        "3576": {"comment":"(h + 32768) / 65536"},
        "3586": {"synopsis":"RND(n): a random integer from 0 to n - 1. Counts the leading zero bits of n, then draws 16\nrandom bits, shifts them down by that count and draws again while the value is above n - 1.","comment":"n = the number in brackets"},
        "3589": {"comment":"HL = n, dropped"},
        "3592": {"comment":"Negative?"},
        "3597": {"comment":"Zero?"},
        "3603": {"comment":"A counts the shifts"},
        "3604": {"synopsis":"Counts the shifts until n's top bit leaves HL."},
        "3605": {"comment":"The top bit out?"},
        "3608": {"comment":"A = n's leading zero bits"},
        "3610": {"comment":"HL = n - 1, the largest result"},
        "3611": {"synopsis":"Draws 16 random bits, shifts them down by the leading zero count and tries again while the\nvalue is above n - 1; pushes the value.","comment":"Keep n - 1"},
        "3613": {"comment":"HL = 16 random bits"},
        "3617": {"comment":"B = the shift count"},
        "3618": {"synopsis":"Shifts the random value right B times.","comment":"HL / 2"},
        "3624": {"comment":"DE = the value"},
        "3625": {"comment":"HL = n - 1"},
        "3627": {"comment":"Above n - 1?"},
        "3630": {"comment":"Then draw again"},
        "3632": {"comment":"HL = the value"},
        "3633": {"comment":"Push it"},
        "3636": {"synopsis":"SGN's entry in the built-in function chain: link RND_ENTRY, length 3, \"SGN\", type $0A (bit\n3: built-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at SGN_FN."},
        "3643": {"synopsis":"The SGN function: 0 for 0, else 1 with the argument's sign.","comment":"x = the number in brackets"},
        "3646": {"comment":"The top BCD byte: zero?"},
        "3650": {"comment":"Then 0 is the result"},
        "3651": {"comment":"Keep the sign"},
        "3655": {"comment":"Make it 1"},
        "3659": {"comment":"with x's sign"},
        "3668": {"synopsis":"SIN's entry in the built-in function chain: link SGN_ENTRY, length 3, \"SIN\", type $0A (bit\n3: built-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at SIN_FN."},
        "3675": {"synopsis":"The SIN function. SIN_BODY does the work and is also used by COS_FN and TAN_FN.","comment":"x = the number in brackets"},
        "3678": {"synopsis":"sin x for the number on the IY stack.\n1. Reduces x modulo 2 pi (MOD_TOP), so r lies in [0, 2 pi).\n2. Folds r into [-pi/2, pi/2] (SIN_FOLD): r above pi/2 becomes r - pi, and the saved state\n   byte (bit 6) records that the result changes sign.\n3. sin t = t + t * t^2 (c25 + t^2 (c26 + t^2 (c27 + t^2 (c28 + t^2 c29)))), an odd series to\n   t^11 with constants 25-29 (SIN_SERIES).\n4. Negates the result when the fold says so.\nUses calculator memory A.\nExit: sin x on the IY stack.","comment":"Push 2 pi"},
        "3684": {"comment":"r = x mod 2 pi"},
        "3687": {"comment":"r's exponent byte"},
        "3691": {"comment":"Bit 6 = r's sign: the state byte"},
        "3693": {"comment":"Memory A = r"},
        "3695": {"synopsis":"Compares r (memory A) with pi/2, or with -pi/2 while bit 7 of the state byte is set; inside\nthe range goes to the series, above it subtracts pi and flips bit 6 of the state.","comment":"Push r and pi/2"},
        "3699": {"comment":"The state byte"},
        "3701": {"comment":"Bit 7 set: compare with -pi/2"},
        "3704": {"comment":"r against the limit, both dropped (NUM_COMPARE)"},
        "3707": {"comment":"B = the state"},
        "3709": {"comment":"On the limit: no fold"},
        "3711": {"comment":"Inside the range?"},
        "3715": {"comment":"Push r and pi"},
        "3719": {"comment":"Flip bit 6: the sign changes"},
        "3723": {"comment":"Subtract pi (add it when bit 7 is set)"},
        "3726": {"comment":"Memory A = r - pi"},
        "3729": {"comment":"Check again"},
        "3731": {"synopsis":"Flips bit 7 of the state byte; set, r is checked against -pi/2 next, clear, r is inside the\nrange.","comment":"The state byte"},
        "3732": {"comment":"The other limit"},
        "3735": {"comment":"Check against -pi/2"},
        "3738": {"synopsis":"sin t for t in memory A: t + t * t^2 (c25 + ... + c29 t^8), t^2 kept in memory A; then the\nsign from the state byte (bit 6 into bit 7 by ADD A,A).","comment":"t + t*t^2(c25 + t^2(c26 + ... t^2 c29))"},
        "3770": {"comment":"The state byte"},
        "3771": {"comment":"Bit 6 clear: the sign stays"},
        "3773": {"comment":"Negate the result"},
        "3776": {"synopsis":"SQR's entry in the built-in function chain: link SIN_ENTRY, length 3, \"SQR\", type $0A (bit\n3: built-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at SQR_FN."},
        "3783": {"synopsis":"The SQR function, the square root by Heron's (Newton's) method.\n1. A negative x is error 4; 0 gives 0.\n2. x goes to memory A with its exponent replaced by $3F, so the mantissa m lies in [0.1, 1);\n   the exponent less $3F is kept.\n3. A first guess g = 0.894427 m + 0.223607 (constants 31 and 30) in memory B, then four\n   rounds of g = g + (m / g - g) / 2 (SQR_STEP).\n4. An odd exponent multiplies g by sqrt(0.1) (constant 32) and counts one more; half the\n   exponent is added to g's exponent byte ($19CD) and memory B is pushed (SQR_SCALE).\nExit: sqrt x on the IY stack.","comment":"x = the number in brackets"},
        "3786": {"comment":"Negative?"},
        "3790": {"comment":"Error 4"},
        "3793": {"comment":"Zero?"},
        "3797": {"comment":"Then 0 is the result"},
        "3798": {"comment":"Memory A = x, dropped"},
        "3801": {"comment":"x's exponent"},
        "3805": {"comment":"The decimal exponent"},
        "3807": {"comment":"Keep it"},
        "3808": {"comment":"m = the mantissa, 0.1 to 1"},
        "3810": {"comment":"Memory B = 0.894427 m + 0.223607, the guess"},
        "3819": {"comment":"Four rounds"},
        "3821": {"synopsis":"One round of Heron's method: g = g + (m / g - g) / 2 in memory B.","comment":"Keep the count"},
        "3822": {"comment":"Memory B = g + (m/g - g) * 0.5"},
        "3834": {"comment":"The count"},
        "3835": {"comment":"Four times"},
        "3837": {"comment":"A = the exponent"},
        "3838": {"comment":"Odd?"},
        "3842": {"comment":"Keep it"},
        "3843": {"comment":"Memory B * sqrt(0.1)"},
        "3849": {"comment":"The exponent"},
        "3850": {"comment":"Plus 1, even now"},
        "3851": {"synopsis":"Adds half the exponent to memory B's exponent byte ($19CD, 7 bits) and pushes memory B as\nthe result (calculator operation 7's routine).","comment":"Half the exponent"},
        "3853": {"comment":"Memory B's exponent"},
        "3856": {"comment":"Times 10^(e/2)"},
        "3857": {"comment":"The sign bit stays clear"},
        "3860": {"comment":"Push memory B: the result"},
        "3863": {"synopsis":"Raises y to the power x for the two numbers on the IY stack (x on top); called by the\noperand code at $F1B4 (the ^ operator).\n1. x = 0 gives 1; y = 0 gives 0.\n2. An integer x below 1000 in size (INT and the rounded value agree) is done by squaring and\n   multiplying, the bits of |x| from the lowest (POWER_LOOP); a negative x then takes 1 /\n   the result.\n3. Any other x: e^(x ln y) through LOG_BODY and EXP_BODY (POWER_EXP), so y must be positive\n   (LOG's error 4).\nExit: y^x on the IY stack.","comment":"The exponent's top BCD byte"},
        "3867": {"comment":"x is not 0?"},
        "3869": {"comment":"Drop x"},
        "3872": {"comment":"y^0 = 1"},
        "3875": {"synopsis":"x is not 0: y = 0 gives 0; an x of 1000 or more in size goes to POWER_EXP; otherwise checks\nthat x is an integer.","comment":"The base's top BCD byte"},
        "3879": {"comment":"0: drop x, the result is 0"},
        "3882": {"comment":"x's exponent"},
        "3887": {"comment":"1000 or more in size?"},
        "3891": {"comment":"A copy of x"},
        "3894": {"comment":"HL = INT x, the copy dropped"},
        "3898": {"comment":"Another copy"},
        "3901": {"comment":"Rounded (ROUND_MANTISSA)"},
        "3906": {"comment":"Push INT x"},
        "3909": {"comment":"The two against each other, dropped"},
        "3913": {"comment":"Not an integer: the general way"},
        "3915": {"comment":"A negative exponent?"},
        "3917": {"comment":"Keep the answer (Z: positive)"},
        "3918": {"comment":"HL = |x|"},
        "3921": {"comment":"Keep |x|"},
        "3922": {"comment":"Memory A = y (x dropped), push 1"},
        "3927": {"synopsis":"Square and multiply: for each bit of HL from the lowest, a set bit multiplies the result by\nmemory A; memory A is squared between the bits.","comment":"HL = the bits left"},
        "3929": {"comment":"This bit set?"},
        "3933": {"comment":"Push the power of y"},
        "3936": {"comment":"Multiply it into the result"},
        "3939": {"synopsis":"Takes the next bit; when none are left, ends (POWER_DONE); else squares memory A."},
        "3940": {"comment":"The next bit"},
        "3944": {"comment":"None left?"},
        "3948": {"comment":"Keep the bits"},
        "3949": {"comment":"Memory A = its square"},
        "3954": {"comment":"The next bit"},
        "3956": {"synopsis":"A positive exponent returns the result; a negative one makes it 1 / the result.","comment":"The exponent's sign"},
        "3957": {"comment":"Positive: done"},
        "3958": {"comment":"1 / the result"},
        "3965": {"synopsis":"y^x = e^(x ln y): x goes to memory B, LOG_BODY makes ln y, x is multiplied in and EXP_BODY\nends the work.","comment":"Memory B = x, dropped"},
        "3968": {"comment":"ln y"},
        "3971": {"comment":"Push x"},
        "3974": {"comment":"x ln y"},
        "3977": {"comment":"e^(x ln y)"},
        "3980": {"synopsis":"STR$'s entry in the built-in function chain: link SQR_ENTRY, length 4, \"STR$\", type $08 (bit\n3: built-in code, bit 1 clear: a string result). The code follows at STRS_FN."},
        "3988": {"synopsis":"The STR$ function: NUM_TO_TEXT writes the number's text from $1931 and returns HL at its\nlength byte ($1930); the number is dropped and PUSH_STR_LEN pushes the text as a string.","comment":"x = the number in brackets"},
        "3991": {"comment":"Its text, HL = the length byte"},
        "3994": {"comment":"Drop x"},
        "3997": {"comment":"Push the text as a string"},
        "4000": {"synopsis":"STRING$'s entry in the built-in function chain: link STRS_ENTRY, length 7, \"STRING$\", type\n$08 (bit 3: built-in code, bit 1 clear: a string result). The code follows at STRINGS_FN."},
        "4011": {"synopsis":"The STRING$ function, STRING$(n, x): a string of n copies of a character. n must be 0-254\n(255: error $0D). The second argument is a string, whose first character is used, when the\ntoken after the comma is below 2; otherwise a number, the character's code.\nExit: the string (type 1, length n, n characters) on the IY stack.","comment":"\"(\""},
        "4016": {"comment":"A = n, a byte"},
        "4019": {"comment":"Keep n"},
        "4020": {"comment":"\",\""},
        "4025": {"comment":"A token below 2: a string"},
        "4029": {"comment":"The string"},
        "4032": {"comment":"Its length"},
        "4036": {"comment":"Empty?"},
        "4038": {"comment":"A = its first character"},
        "4041": {"comment":"Drop the string"},
        "4044": {"comment":"Skips the next two bytes; $EFCD (POP BC, PUSH AF) is one byte in"},
        "4047": {"comment":"Carry: no second evaluation"},
        "4048": {"synopsis":"A numeric second argument: its value as a byte is the character.","comment":"A = the character's code"},
        "4051": {"synopsis":"Writes the string below IY: n copies of the character, the length n and the type byte 1. An\nempty first-argument string arrives at $EFCD inside the LD BC at $EFCC: it replaces n with 0\nthere, without dropping that string. Observed: 20 such calls in one line left the free\nmemory (FREE) unchanged.","comment":"Keep the character"},
        "4052": {"comment":"\")\""},
        "4055": {"comment":"D = the character"},
        "4056": {"comment":"HL = the stack top"},
        "4060": {"comment":"A = n"},
        "4061": {"comment":"255?"},
        "4062": {"comment":"Error $0D"},
        "4066": {"comment":"None?"},
        "4068": {"comment":"B = n"},
        "4069": {"synopsis":"Writes the character n times downwards.","comment":"The character"},
        "4073": {"synopsis":"Writes the length and the type byte 1 and makes the string the new top.","comment":"The length"},
        "4075": {"comment":"Type 1: a string"},
        "4077": {"comment":"The new top"},
        "4081": {"synopsis":"TAN's entry in the built-in function chain: link STRINGS_ENTRY, length 3, \"TAN\", type $0A\n(bit 3: built-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at TAN_FN."},
        "4088": {"synopsis":"The TAN function: tan x = sin x / sin(x + pi/2), x kept in memory B.","comment":"x = the number in brackets"},
        "4091": {"comment":"Memory B = x"},
        "4094": {"comment":"sin x"},
        "4097": {"comment":"Push x + pi/2"},
        "4102": {"comment":"cos x"},
        "4105": {"comment":"sin x / cos x"},
        "4108": {"synopsis":"USR's entry in the built-in function chain: link TAN_ENTRY, length 3, \"USR\", type $0A (bit\n3: built-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at USR_FN."},
        "4115": {"synopsis":"The USR function, USR(address) or USR(address, value): calls the machine code at the address\n(TOP_TO_ADDRESS takes -32768 to 65535) with HL = the value (16 bits, error 4 out of range;\nwithout one HL is not set). The routine returns into PUSH_INT, so its HL comes back as the\nresult (-32768 to 32767).\nExit: the routine's HL on the IY stack.","comment":"\"(\""},
        "4120": {"comment":"The address"},
        "4123": {"comment":"\")\" at once: no value"},
        "4127": {"comment":"\",\""},
        "4132": {"comment":"HL = the value"},
        "4135": {"comment":"\")\""},
        "4137": {"synopsis":"Checks the token, plants PUSH_INT as the routine's return and jumps to the address with HL =\nthe value.","comment":"Check it"},
        "4140": {"comment":"The routine returns into PUSH_INT"},
        "4144": {"comment":"Keep the value"},
        "4145": {"comment":"HL = the address, dropped"},
        "4148": {"comment":"HL = the value, the address on the stack"},
        "4149": {"comment":"Jump to it"},
        "4150": {"synopsis":"VAL's entry in the built-in function chain: link USR_ENTRY, length 3, \"VAL\", type $0A (bit\n3: built-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at VAL_FN."},
        "4157": {"synopsis":"The VAL function: reads a number from the text of the string in brackets. A 0 is put after\nthe string's last character for READ_NUMBER (the byte there is saved and put back);\nNUM_CLAMP replaces an overflow with the largest number; the 9-byte number is then copied up\nover the string.\nExit: the number on the IY stack, where the string began.","comment":"The string in brackets"},
        "4160": {"comment":"HL = its length byte"},
        "4165": {"comment":"BC = the length"},
        "4168": {"comment":"Past the last character"},
        "4170": {"comment":"Save the byte there"},
        "4171": {"comment":"End the text with 0"},
        "4172": {"comment":"Keep its address"},
        "4174": {"comment":"The first character"},
        "4175": {"comment":"Read the number onto the stack"},
        "4178": {"comment":"An overflow: the largest number"},
        "4181": {"comment":"Put the saved byte back"},
        "4184": {"comment":"DE = the string's last byte"},
        "4186": {"comment":"HL = the number's last byte"},
        "4194": {"comment":"Copy the number up over the string"},
        "4197": {"comment":"The number is the new top"},
        "4201": {"synopsis":"VARPTR's entry in the built-in function chain: link VAL_ENTRY, length 6, \"VARPTR\", type $0A\n(bit 3: built-in code, bit 1: a numeric result). The code follows at VARPTR_FN."},
        "4211": {"synopsis":"The VARPTR function: pushes the address of the variable named in brackets. The token after\n\"(\" must be a name (1 or 3, by AND $FD) and its type byte (C') may not be a function (bits\n2-3), else error 4; the type byte is kept in $1708 and VAR_ADDR finds the variable (with its\nsubscripts).\nExit: the address on the IY stack.","comment":"\"(\""},
        "4216": {"comment":"A name token (1 or 3)?"},
        "4220": {"synopsis":"Error 4 for an argument that is not a variable.","comment":"Error 4"},
        "4224": {"comment":"C' = the name's type byte"},
        "4227": {"comment":"Kept in $1708"},
        "4230": {"comment":"A function (bits 2-3)?"},
        "4235": {"comment":"The variable's address in HL"},
        "4238": {"comment":"Push it"},
        "4241": {"comment":"\")\""},
        "4244": {"synopsis":"VERNUM's entry, the head of the built-in function chain: the SYS low chip's $DD0F puts its\naddress ($F094) into $1724, where the chain of names starts. Each entry is a link word (the\nrun address of the next entry, $0000 after ABS_ENTRY), the name's length, the name in ASCII\nand a type byte, and the function's code follows the type byte. New variables are put in\nfront of the chain (VAR_NEW_NAME stores the old head as the new entry's link), so the\nbuilt-in names are its tail. VAR_FIND walks it comparing names; on a match C = the type byte\nand HL = the code. The type byte's bits as the code uses them: bit 3 a built-in routine (the\noperand code at $F1EA jumps to it through the SYS low chip's $DBAD, JP (HL), with the token\nafter the name already fetched), bit 2 a DEF FN function (USER_FN_CALL), bit 1 a numeric\nvalue (clear: a string; VAR_CREATE makes $02 or $00 for a new variable), bit 0 subscripts\nfollow ($F430). So $0A is a built-in with a number as result, $08 one with a string. Here:\nlink VARPTR_ENTRY, length 6, \"VERNUM\", type $0A. The code follows at VERNUM_FN."},
        "4254": {"synopsis":"The VERNUM function, without an argument: pushes 12, the version number (BASIC 1.2).","comment":"Version 1.2"},
        "4257": {"comment":"Push it"},
        "4260": {"synopsis":"Fetches the next token (NEXT_ITEM) and falls into EVAL_NUM: evaluates the numeric expression\nafter the current token.","comment":"Past the token before the expression"},
        "4263": {"synopsis":"Evaluates a numeric expression onto the IY stack, starting at the current token (B'). This\nis the lowest level, OR and XOR, left to right; each level calls the one above it: EVAL_NUM\n(OR, XOR) -> EVAL_AND (AND) -> EVAL_NOT (NOT) -> EVAL_COMPARE (one of < = <= > <> >=) ->\nEVAL_SUM (a leading sign, + and -) -> EVAL_TERM (* and /) -> EVAL_POWER (^) -> EVAL_OPERAND.\nOR, XOR, AND and NOT work on 16-bit integers (TWO_INTS and TOP_TO_INT take them, PUSH_INT\npushes the result). XOR handles only the low bytes: the high bytes are ORed ($10CF), so 256\nXOR 256 gives 256 (observed).\nEntry: A = B' = the current token.\nExit: the value on the IY stack, A = the token after the expression (NZ). The SYS high\nchip's EXPR_TO_INT reaches it to read an integer argument; EVAL_TYPED for a numeric one.","comment":"A = the current token"},
        "4266": {"comment":"The AND level gives the left operand"},
        "4269": {"synopsis":"Looks for OR ($BF) or XOR ($B2) after an operand; returns at anything else.","comment":"The token after it"},
        "4272": {"comment":"OR?"},
        "4276": {"comment":"XOR? Neither: done"},
        "4279": {"comment":"Carry: XOR"},
        "4280": {"synopsis":"OR or XOR (carry set): evaluates the right operand at the AND level and combines the two\nintegers.","comment":"Keep which operator"},
        "4281": {"comment":"Past the operator"},
        "4284": {"comment":"The right operand, at the AND level"},
        "4287": {"comment":"HL = left, DE = right, as integers"},
        "4291": {"comment":"XOR?"},
        "4293": {"comment":"OR: low bytes"},
        "4296": {"comment":"OR: high bytes"},
        "4298": {"comment":"Store the high byte"},
        "4300": {"synopsis":"XOR: the low bytes are XORed, but the high bytes are ORed (the OR D at $10CF).","comment":"XOR: low bytes"},
        "4303": {"comment":"High bytes ORed, not XORed"},
        "4305": {"synopsis":"Pushes HL, the OR or XOR result, as a number and looks for another operator.","comment":"H = the high byte of the result"},
        "4306": {"comment":"Push the result as a number"},
        "4309": {"comment":"Another OR or XOR?"},
        "4311": {"synopsis":"The AND level of EVAL_NUM: a NOT-level operand, then any number of AND ($C9) operands, each\ncombined bit by bit as 16-bit integers.\nEntry: A = the current token.\nExit: the value on the IY stack, A = the next token.","comment":"The NOT level gives the left operand"},
        "4314": {"synopsis":"Looks for AND after an operand; returns at anything else.","comment":"The token after it"},
        "4317": {"comment":"AND? Else done"},
        "4320": {"comment":"Past AND"},
        "4323": {"comment":"The right operand, at the NOT level"},
        "4326": {"comment":"HL = left, DE = right, as integers"},
        "4329": {"comment":"AND the low bytes"},
        "4332": {"comment":"AND the high bytes"},
        "4335": {"comment":"Push the result as a number"},
        "4338": {"comment":"Another AND?"},
        "4340": {"synopsis":"The NOT level of EVAL_NUM: NOT ($C2) complements the 16-bit integer of the operand after it\n(NOT may repeat); without NOT it falls into EVAL_COMPARE.\nEntry: A = the current token.","comment":"NOT?"},
        "4342": {"comment":"No: a comparison"},
        "4344": {"comment":"Past NOT"},
        "4347": {"comment":"The operand: NOT may repeat"},
        "4350": {"comment":"HL = it as an integer"},
        "4353": {"comment":"Complement the low byte"},
        "4356": {"comment":"And the high byte"},
        "4359": {"comment":"Push the result as a number"},
        "4362": {"synopsis":"The comparison level of EVAL_NUM. Evaluates a sum and, when one of the operators $99-$9E (<\n= <= > <> >=) follows, a second one, compares them (NUM_COMPARE) and pushes -1 for true or 0\nfor false. Only one comparison is taken: what follows goes back to the levels below. A\nstring operand (token 0 or 1) is compared with a second string (EVAL_STR_COMPARE).\nEntry: A = the current token.\nExit: the value on the IY stack, A = the next token.","comment":"A string operand (token 0 or 1)?"},
        "4366": {"comment":"The left side: a numeric sum"},
        "4369": {"comment":"Below '<' ($99)? Not a comparison"},
        "4372": {"comment":"Above '>=' ($9E)? Not a comparison"},
        "4375": {"comment":"Keep the operator"},
        "4376": {"comment":"Past it"},
        "4379": {"comment":"The right side"},
        "4382": {"comment":"Compare the two numbers (NUM_COMPARE): flags"},
        "4385": {"comment":"Make the truth value"},
        "4387": {"synopsis":"A string on the left: evaluates it and a second string after the operator and compares them\n(STR_COMPARE). A string without a comparison operator after it is error 14 (Type mismatch).","comment":"The left side: a string expression"},
        "4390": {"comment":"No comparison after a string: type mismatch"},
        "4400": {"comment":"Keep the operator"},
        "4401": {"comment":"Past it"},
        "4404": {"comment":"The right side: a string expression"},
        "4407": {"comment":"Compare the two strings (STR_COMPARE): flags"},
        "4410": {"synopsis":"Turns the comparison's flags and the operator into -1 or 0 (CMP_TRUTH) and pushes it as a\nnumber.","comment":"H = the operator token"},
        "4412": {"comment":"HL = -1 or 0"},
        "4415": {"comment":"Push it as a number"},
        "4418": {"synopsis":"Turns the flags of NUM_COMPARE or STR_COMPARE into a truth value. The low three bits of the\noperator token say which outcomes are true: bit 0 less (sign set), bit 1 equal (Z), bit 2\ngreater. RRCA keeps S and Z.\nEntry: A = the operator token ($99-$9E), S and Z from the comparison.\nExit: HL = $FFFF (true) or 0.","comment":"True unless no condition holds"},
        "4421": {"comment":"Bit 0: '<' accepted?"},
        "4424": {"comment":"Less: true"},
        "4425": {"synopsis":"Tests the '=' bit.","comment":"Bit 1: '=' accepted?"},
        "4428": {"comment":"Equal: true"},
        "4429": {"synopsis":"Tests the '>' bit: greater is neither equal nor less.","comment":"Bit 2: '>' accepted?"},
        "4432": {"comment":"Equal is not greater"},
        "4434": {"comment":"Greater: true"},
        "4435": {"synopsis":"No accepted outcome: HL = 0.","comment":"False: 0"},
        "4437": {"synopsis":"The sum level of EVAL_NUM: an optional leading '+' or '-' before the first term (the sign\napplies to that term only, after its * / and ^), then any number of '+' or '-' terms, left\nto right, through CALC_ADD and CALC_SUBTRACT. A sign is taken only here: 4*-1 is error 1\n(observed).\nEntry: A = the current token.\nExit: the value on the IY stack, A = the next token (NZ).","comment":"A leading sign and the first term?"},
        "4440": {"comment":"No sign: just the first term"},
        "4443": {"comment":"Was the sign '-'?"},
        "4444": {"comment":"Yes: negate the first term (CALC_NEGATE)"},
        "4447": {"synopsis":"Adds or subtracts each further term.","comment":"Another '+' or '-' with its term?"},
        "4450": {"comment":"No: the sum is done"},
        "4451": {"comment":"Z: it was '+'"},
        "4452": {"comment":"Add (CALC_ADD)"},
        "4455": {"comment":"Else subtract (CALC_SUBTRACT)"},
        "4458": {"comment":"Next operator"},
        "4460": {"synopsis":"Looks for '+' ($98) or '-' ($A2) at the current token. When one is there, skips it and\nevaluates the term after it (EVAL_TERM).\nExit: Z and E = $FF after '+', Z and E = 0 after '-'; NZ with E = $FF when there is no sign\n(nothing evaluated).","comment":"A = the current token"},
        "4463": {"comment":"E = $FF: '+' (or no sign)"},
        "4465": {"comment":"'+'?"},
        "4469": {"comment":"'-'?"},
        "4471": {"comment":"Neither: NZ"},
        "4472": {"comment":"'-': E = 0"},
        "4473": {"synopsis":"A sign: the term after it, with Z set.","comment":"Past the sign"},
        "4476": {"comment":"The term after it"},
        "4479": {"comment":"Z: a sign was found"},
        "4481": {"synopsis":"The term level of EVAL_NUM: powers joined by '*' ($A8) and '/' ($A1), left to right, through\nCALC_MULTIPLY and CALC_DIVIDE.\nEntry: A = the current token.\nExit: the value on the IY stack, A = the next token.","comment":"The first factor"},
        "4484": {"synopsis":"Looks for '*' or '/' after a power; returns at anything else.","comment":"The token after it"},
        "4487": {"comment":"'*'?"},
        "4491": {"comment":"'/'? Neither: done"},
        "4494": {"synopsis":"'*' or '/': the next power, then the operation.","comment":"D = the operator"},
        "4495": {"comment":"Past it"},
        "4498": {"comment":"The next factor"},
        "4501": {"comment":"Keep the operator over the call"},
        "4502": {"comment":"'*'?"},
        "4505": {"comment":"Multiply (CALC_MULTIPLY)"},
        "4508": {"comment":"Else divide (CALC_DIVIDE)"},
        "4512": {"comment":"Next operator"},
        "4514": {"synopsis":"The power level of EVAL_NUM: operands joined by '^' ($9F), left to right (POWER_OP raises to\nthe power), so 2^3^2 is 64 (observed). Keeps DE (EVAL_SUM's sign in E).\nEntry: A = the current token.\nExit: the value on the IY stack, A = the next token.","comment":"Keep DE over the call"},
        "4515": {"comment":"The base: an operand"},
        "4518": {"synopsis":"Looks for another '^'."},
        "4519": {"comment":"The token after it"},
        "4522": {"comment":"'^'? Else done"},
        "4525": {"comment":"Keep DE over the calls"},
        "4526": {"comment":"Past '^'"},
        "4529": {"comment":"The exponent: an operand"},
        "4532": {"comment":"Raise to the power (POWER_OP)"},
        "4535": {"comment":"Another '^': left to right"},
        "4537": {"synopsis":"Raises error 3 (Argument missing).","comment":"Error 3: Argument missing"},
        "4539": {"synopsis":"Evaluates a numeric operand onto the IY stack, after checking the room (ROOM_256, error 6):\n1. ORD and ATN, which are tokens, run their code (ORD_FN, ATN_FN).\n2. A number (token 2) is stacked already by NEXT_ITEM: just fetches the next token.\n3. '(' evaluates the expression in it (EVAL_NEXT_NUM) and needs ')'.\n4. A numeric name (token 3) is looked up (VAR_ADDR): a built-in function (type bit 3) runs\n   its code at HL through the SYS low chip's JP (HL) at $DBAD, a DEF function (bit 2) is\n   called (USER_FN_CALL), a variable's number is pushed (PUSH_VAR_NUM). A string (token 0 or\n   1) is error 14 (Type mismatch), the end of the statement error 3, anything else error 1\n   (ERROR_1).\nEntry: A = the current token.\nExit: A = the token after the operand.","comment":"Keep the token"},
        "4540": {"comment":"Room on the stack? (error 6 if not)"},
        "4544": {"comment":"ORD?"},
        "4546": {"comment":"ORD's code (ORD_FN)"},
        "4549": {"comment":"ATN?"},
        "4551": {"comment":"ATN's code (ATN_FN)"},
        "4554": {"comment":"Token 0 or 1, a string: type mismatch"},
        "4559": {"comment":"Token 2: the number is stacked; next token"},
        "4562": {"comment":"End of the statement ($FD-$FF)?"},
        "4564": {"comment":"Yes: argument missing"},
        "4566": {"comment":"'('?"},
        "4570": {"comment":"Evaluate what is in the brackets"},
        "4573": {"comment":"Must end with ')'; then next token"},
        "4578": {"synopsis":"Token 3, a numeric name: a function or a variable.","comment":"Token 3: a numeric name"},
        "4580": {"comment":"Anything else: error 1"},
        "4583": {"comment":"HL = its value, C = its type byte"},
        "4586": {"comment":"Bit 3: a built-in function?"},
        "4588": {"comment":"Yes: run its code at HL"},
        "4591": {"comment":"Bit 2: a DEF function?"},
        "4593": {"comment":"No: push the variable's number"},
        "4596": {"synopsis":"Calls a function defined by DEF, for EVAL_OPERAND and EVAL_STR_OPND.\n1. Keeps $1701 (the expression type). If '(' follows the name, evaluates the argument with\n   the type of its first token's class (bit 1: numeric) and needs ')'.\n2. Keeps the free end $1726, the variable chain's head $1724, the text pointer HL', the line\n   pointer $170C and the token in BC', then runs in the DEF's line from its record's text\n   pointer (the record, after the type byte: the DEF's line pointer, then the text after its\n   name; DEF stores them at $E01E).\n3. The definition must have '(' when the call has one (error 1 otherwise). Its parameter\n   becomes a variable at the chain's head (a new one, VAR_CREATE, when a variable of that\n   name exists already), of the argument's type (error 14 otherwise), and gets the argument\n   (STORE_VALUE).\n4. After '=', evaluates the body with the function's type in $1701, then restores everything\n   kept: the parameter disappears with the old chain head and free end.\nObserved: 10 DEF Q(Z)=Z*2+1 and 20 POKE 30003,Q(3) poke 7; DEF R=7 works without a\nparameter.\nEntry: HL = the record's value part, C = its type byte, A = the token after the name.\nExit: the value on the IY stack.","comment":"Keep $1701 (the expression type)"},
        "4600": {"comment":"Keep the function's record"},
        "4601": {"comment":"Is the token after the name '('?"},
        "4605": {"comment":"A = $FF if so, else 0"},
        "4606": {"comment":"Keep the argument flag"},
        "4607": {"comment":"A = the function's type byte"},
        "4608": {"comment":"No argument: skip"},
        "4610": {"comment":"Keep the type in A'"},
        "4611": {"comment":"The argument's first token"},
        "4614": {"comment":"An operator or keyword: error 1"},
        "4618": {"comment":"Its class gives the argument's type"},
        "4621": {"comment":"Evaluate the argument"},
        "4624": {"comment":"Must end with ')'"},
        "4629": {"comment":"The type back"},
        "4630": {"synopsis":"Keeps the caller's state and moves into the DEF's text.","comment":"Type in A'"},
        "4631": {"comment":"A = the argument flag"},
        "4632": {"comment":"Keep the free end ($1726)"},
        "4636": {"comment":"And the variable chain's head ($1724)"},
        "4641": {"comment":"Keep the text pointer"},
        "4643": {"comment":"And the line pointer $170C"},
        "4647": {"comment":"And the current token"},
        "4649": {"comment":"DE = the DEF's line"},
        "4653": {"comment":"Run in it for now"},
        "4657": {"comment":"DE = the text after the DEF's name"},
        "4660": {"comment":"Text pointer = the DEF's text"},
        "4662": {"comment":"C = the argument flag"},
        "4663": {"comment":"The token after the DEF's name"},
        "4666": {"comment":"'(' in the definition?"},
        "4671": {"comment":"Same as in the call? Else error 1"},
        "4675": {"comment":"No argument: straight to the body"},
        "4678": {"comment":"The parameter's name"},
        "4681": {"comment":"A simple variable (token 1 or 3)?"},
        "4685": {"comment":"No: error 1"},
        "4688": {"comment":"C' = its type byte"},
        "4689": {"comment":"Found an old one: make a new one ahead"},
        "4694": {"comment":"The parameter's type"},
        "4695": {"comment":"Same as the argument's?"},
        "4700": {"comment":"No: type mismatch"},
        "4703": {"comment":"DE = the parameter's value"},
        "4706": {"comment":"Assign the argument to it (STORE_VALUE)"},
        "4709": {"comment":"Past the name"},
        "4712": {"comment":"Must be ')'"},
        "4717": {"synopsis":"After '=', evaluates the function's body and restores the caller's state.","comment":"Must be '='"},
        "4722": {"comment":"The function's type"},
        "4723": {"comment":"Is the expression type"},
        "4726": {"comment":"Evaluate the body"},
        "4729": {"comment":"The caller's token"},
        "4730": {"comment":"The caller's line pointer"},
        "4734": {"comment":"The caller's text pointer"},
        "4736": {"comment":"Restore the variable chain's head"},
        "4740": {"comment":"Free the parameter's space"},
        "4744": {"comment":"And the expression type"},
        "4748": {"comment":"The value is on the IY stack"},
        "4749": {"synopsis":"Evaluates an expression of the type in bit 1 of $1701: set, numeric (EVAL_NUM); clear, a\nstring (EVAL_STR).\nEntry: A = the current token.","comment":"Bit 1 of $1701: numeric?"},
        "4753": {"comment":"Yes: a numeric expression"},
        "4756": {"synopsis":"Evaluates a string expression onto the IY stack: operands (with their slices,\nEVAL_STR_SLICE) joined by '&' ($97). A string entry is a type byte 1, a length byte, then\nthe characters; joining moves the two strings into one entry in the left one's place. A\nresult over 254 characters is error 13 (Overflow, OVERFLOW_ERR).\nEntry: A = the current token.\nExit: A = the token after the expression.","comment":"A = the current token"},
        "4759": {"comment":"The first operand, with its slices"},
        "4762": {"synopsis":"Looks for '&' after an operand; returns at anything else.","comment":"The token after it"},
        "4765": {"comment":"'&'?"},
        "4768": {"comment":"Past it"},
        "4771": {"comment":"The next operand"},
        "4774": {"comment":"HL = the top entry"},
        "4777": {"comment":"Its length byte"},
        "4778": {"comment":"DE = it"},
        "4780": {"comment":"BC = the right string's length"},
        "4783": {"comment":"Past its characters"},
        "4784": {"comment":"The left string's entry"},
        "4785": {"comment":"Drop the right string for now"},
        "4788": {"comment":"HL = the left string's length byte"},
        "4789": {"comment":"C = its length"},
        "4790": {"comment":"Room for the result? (error 6 if not)"},
        "4793": {"comment":"Keep the length byte's address"},
        "4794": {"comment":"HL = its last character"},
        "4795": {"comment":"An empty left string?"},
        "4797": {"comment":"A = the right string's length"},
        "4798": {"comment":"Nothing to move"},
        "4800": {"comment":"Move the left characters under the right"},
        "4802": {"synopsis":"Stores the joined length and moves the joined string to the top of the left entry's place.","comment":"HL = the left length byte"},
        "4803": {"comment":"The total length"},
        "4804": {"comment":"Above the joined characters"},
        "4805": {"comment":"Over 255: overflow"},
        "4808": {"comment":"255 is overflow too"},
        "4813": {"comment":"C = the left length"},
        "4814": {"comment":"DE = below the left entry"},
        "4818": {"comment":"HL = the top of the left entry"},
        "4819": {"comment":"Copy from the joined text down"},
        "4820": {"comment":"The total plus the length byte"},
        "4822": {"comment":"Up to the top of the old entry"},
        "4824": {"comment":"HL = under the new length byte"},
        "4825": {"comment":"The string entry's type byte"},
        "4827": {"comment":"The result is the top entry"},
        "4830": {"comment":"Another '&'?"},
        "4832": {"synopsis":"A string operand (EVAL_STR_OPND) followed by any number of slices in brackets, (from:to),\n(from:), (:to) or (n) for one character (SLICE_ARGS reads them). Out-of-range bounds are\nclipped: 'to' to the length, 'from' 0 to 1; a 'from' beyond the end or after 'to' gives an\nempty string.\nObserved: A$=\"ABCDE\" gives A$(2:3) of length 2 starting with B, A$(4) of length 1.\nEntry: A = the current token.\nExit: the string on the IY stack, A = the next token.","comment":"A string operand"},
        "4835": {"synopsis":"Looks for '(' after the string: a slice.","comment":"The token after it"},
        "4838": {"comment":"'('? Else done"},
        "4841": {"comment":"E = from, D = to (SLICE_ARGS)"},
        "4844": {"comment":"HL = the top entry"},
        "4847": {"comment":"Its length byte"},
        "4848": {"comment":"A = the length"},
        "4849": {"comment":"BC = it too"},
        "4852": {"comment":"'to' beyond the end?"},
        "4855": {"comment":"Then 'to' = the length"},
        "4856": {"synopsis":"D = 'to', clipped; zero gives an empty string.","comment":"D = 'to'"},
        "4857": {"comment":"Zero: empty"},
        "4860": {"comment":"'from' beyond the end?"},
        "4862": {"comment":"Yes: empty"},
        "4864": {"comment":"'from' = 0?"},
        "4868": {"comment":"Is taken as 1"},
        "4869": {"synopsis":"The slice's length: 'to' - 'from'.","comment":"'to' - 'from'"},
        "4871": {"comment":"Negative: empty"},
        "4873": {"synopsis":"An empty slice: length 0 at the top of the entry.","comment":"HL = the last character"},
        "4874": {"comment":"Length 0"},
        "4875": {"comment":"Store it"},
        "4877": {"synopsis":"Moves the characters 'from' to 'to' to the top of the entry.","comment":"A = the count"},
        "4878": {"comment":"Keep the length byte's address"},
        "4879": {"comment":"HL = the last character"},
        "4880": {"comment":"DE = it; H = 'to'"},
        "4881": {"comment":"C = 'to'"},
        "4883": {"comment":"HL = the 'to' character"},
        "4884": {"comment":"C = the count"},
        "4885": {"comment":"Move the slice to the entry's top"},
        "4887": {"comment":"HL = under the slice"},
        "4888": {"synopsis":"Writes the new length and type byte under the slice and makes it the top entry.","comment":"Its length"},
        "4889": {"comment":"The type byte under it"},
        "4890": {"comment":"The string entry's type byte"},
        "4892": {"comment":"The slice is the top entry"},
        "4895": {"comment":"Another slice?"},
        "4897": {"comment":"No caller found: the same code as SLICE_BOUND"},
        "4900": {"comment":"0 when the value is negative"},
        "4902": {"comment":"Negative?"},
        "4904": {"comment":"D = $FF when above 255"},
        "4906": {"comment":"Above 255?"},
        "4907": {"comment":"D = the value"},
        "4909": {"synopsis":"Evaluates a string operand onto the IY stack, after checking the room (ROOM_256, error 6):\n1. A string in quotes (token 0) is stacked already by NEXT_ITEM: fetches the next token.\n2. A string name (token 1): INKEY$ runs its code (INKEYS_FN); otherwise VAR_ADDR finds it: a\n   built-in function (type bit 3) runs at HL, a DEF function (bit 2) is called\n   (USER_FN_CALL), a variable's string is pushed (PUSH_VAR_STR). A number (token 2 or 3) is\n   error 14 (Type mismatch), the end of the statement error 3, anything else error 1.\nEntry: B' = the current token.","comment":"Room on the stack? (error 6 if not)"},
        "4912": {"comment":"B = the token, C = C' (a type byte)"},
        "4916": {"comment":"A = the token"},
        "4917": {"comment":"Token 0: the string is stacked; next token"},
        "4922": {"comment":"Token 1: a string name"},
        "4924": {"comment":"Tokens 2 and 3 are numeric: type mismatch"},
        "4928": {"comment":"Not the end of the statement: error 1"},
        "4933": {"comment":"Error 3: Argument missing"},
        "4935": {"synopsis":"Token 1: INKEY$, a string function or a string variable.","comment":"INKEY$?"},
        "4938": {"comment":"Yes: its code (INKEYS_FN)"},
        "4941": {"comment":"HL = its value, C = its type byte"},
        "4944": {"comment":"Bit 3: a built-in function?"},
        "4946": {"comment":"Yes: run its code at HL"},
        "4949": {"comment":"Bit 2: a DEF function?"},
        "4951": {"comment":"Yes: call it"},
        "4954": {"comment":"Push the variable's string (PUSH_VAR_STR)"},
        "4957": {"synopsis":"Raises error 14 (Type mismatch).","comment":"Error 14: Type mismatch"},
        "4959": {"synopsis":"Starts a new variable record at the free end ($1726) and makes it the head of the variable\nchain ($1724): the record is a link word (the old head), a length byte and the name, copied\nfrom the text at $1728. The name runs while the characters are digits, '.', capitals and '?'\nto '_', control codes below ' ' or keyword tokens $A9-$FC (the tokeniser may have made part\nof a name a keyword); '$' is its last character; anything else ends it.\nEntry: ($1728) = the name in the text.\nExit: HL = ($1726) = the address of the record's type byte (not written yet), DE = the text\nafter the name, A = the name's last character.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"Room for the record? (error 6 if not)"},
        "4962": {"comment":"DE = the chain's head"},
        "4966": {"comment":"HL = the free end"},
        "4969": {"comment":"The new record is the head"},
        "4972": {"comment":"Its link: the old head"},
        "4976": {"comment":"C = the name's length"},
        "4978": {"comment":"Keep where the length goes"},
        "4979": {"comment":"DE = the name in the text"},
        "4983": {"synopsis":"Copies the name's characters.","comment":"The next character"},
        "4984": {"comment":"$FD-$FF ends the name"},
        "4988": {"comment":"Below ' ': kept"},
        "4992": {"comment":"'$'? Kept, and it is the last"},
        "4996": {"comment":"'.'? Kept"},
        "5000": {"comment":"Below '0': ends it"},
        "5004": {"comment":"A digit: kept"},
        "5008": {"comment":"':' to '>': ends it"},
        "5012": {"comment":"'?' to '_': kept"},
        "5016": {"comment":"Lower case and operators end it"},
        "5020": {"synopsis":"A character of the name: carry set, so more may follow.","comment":"Carry: more may follow"},
        "5021": {"synopsis":"Stores the character in the record.","comment":"Past it in the text"},
        "5022": {"comment":"One more character"},
        "5023": {"comment":"Store it"},
        "5025": {"comment":"More?"},
        "5027": {"synopsis":"Writes the length and sets the free end to the type byte's address.","comment":"A = the last character"},
        "5028": {"comment":"HL = where the type byte goes"},
        "5029": {"comment":"Is the free end for now"},
        "5032": {"comment":"HL = where the length goes"},
        "5033": {"comment":"Store the length"},
        "5034": {"comment":"HL = the type byte's address"},
        "5036": {"synopsis":"Appends a number of zero to the variable space: six zero bytes at the free end ($1726),\nwhich moves past them. A variable's number is 6 bytes: five BCD bytes and the exponent\n(PUSH_VAR_NUM pushes it with a zero lowest byte and carry byte).\nChanges: AF, B, HL.","comment":"Room for it? (error 6 if not)"},
        "5039": {"comment":"HL = the free end"},
        "5042": {"comment":"Six bytes"},
        "5044": {"comment":"Of zero"},
        "5045": {"synopsis":"Writes the six zeros."},
        "5049": {"comment":"New free end"},
        "5051": {"synopsis":"Appends an empty value of the type in bit 1 of $1701 to the variable space: a number\n(VAR_NEW_NUM) or a string of up to C characters (VAR_NEW_STR).","comment":"Bit 1 of $1701: numeric?"},
        "5055": {"comment":"Yes: a number"},
        "5057": {"synopsis":"Appends a string value of up to C characters at the free end ($1726): the longest length,\nthe current length (0), then room for C characters; the free end moves past them.\nEntry: C = the longest length.\nChanges: AF, B, HL.","comment":"Room for it? (error 6 if not)"},
        "5060": {"comment":"HL = the free end"},
        "5063": {"comment":"The longest length"},
        "5065": {"comment":"BC = it"},
        "5067": {"comment":"Current length 0"},
        "5068": {"comment":"Past the characters"},
        "5069": {"comment":"Past the last"},
        "5070": {"synopsis":"Sets the free end ($1726) to HL.","comment":"The new free end"},
        "5074": {"synopsis":"Looks up the name in the text in the variable chain, which starts at $1724 (reset to\nVERNUM_ENTRY, VERNUM's entry, so the built-in functions are found the same way). Each record\nis a link word, a length byte, the name and a type byte. A record matches when its name's\ncharacters are all there and the name in the text ends after them: at '$', a space, $FD-$FF\nor an operator token ($80-$A8). A found record's type bit 7 is cleared. The first pass\nenters with HL = $1725; the LD A,$E1 at $13D8 hides the POP HL ($13D9) where the loop\nresumes.\nEntry: HL = the name in the text.\nExit: carry set when found: C = the type byte, DE = the value (after the type byte), HL =\nthe text after the name. Not found: Z, carry clear, HL = the name, ($1728) = it. The SYS\nhigh chip's NEXT_ITEM calls it for each name, then VAR_CREATE when it fails.","comment":"Keep the name's address"},
        "5077": {"comment":"The head's high byte, at $1725"},
        "5080": {"comment":"POP HL at $13D9 is the loop's entry"},
        "5081": {"synopsis":"The next record, from the link at the address on the stack."},
        "5082": {"comment":"D = the link's high byte"},
        "5084": {"comment":"E = the low byte"},
        "5085": {"comment":"End of the chain?"},
        "5087": {"comment":"HL = the name"},
        "5090": {"comment":"Not found: Z"},
        "5091": {"comment":"HL = the record, DE = the name"},
        "5092": {"comment":"Its high link byte, for the next"},
        "5094": {"comment":"The length byte"},
        "5095": {"comment":"B = the length"},
        "5096": {"synopsis":"Compares the name's characters.","comment":"A character of the name"},
        "5098": {"comment":"The record's character"},
        "5099": {"comment":"Different: next record"},
        "5102": {"comment":"All of them?"},
        "5104": {"comment":"Ended by '$'? Found"},
        "5108": {"comment":"The character after the name"},
        "5109": {"comment":"A space? Found"},
        "5113": {"comment":"$FD-$FF? Found"},
        "5117": {"comment":"A keyword ($A9-$FC) may be part of a name"},
        "5121": {"comment":"Bit 7: an operator ends it: found"},
        "5124": {"synopsis":"Found: clears bit 7 of the type byte.","comment":"Drop the link address"},
        "5125": {"comment":"The type byte"},
        "5126": {"comment":"Found again: clear bit 7"},
        "5128": {"comment":"Carry: found"},
        "5131": {"synopsis":"Creates a variable for the name at ($1728) (VAR_NEW_NAME): a name ending in '$' gets type 0\nand a string of up to 18 characters (VAR_NEW_STR), any other type 2 and the number 0\n(VAR_NEW_NUM). Bit 7 of the type byte marks it as just created (VAR_FIND clears it), which\nDEF and DIM check (error 15, Variable declared twice).\nExit: C = the type byte, DE = the value, HL = the text after the name.","comment":"Store the name, A = its last character"},
        "5134": {"comment":"'$'?"},
        "5138": {"comment":"Numeric: type 2"},
        "5140": {"synopsis":"Writes the type byte and the value after it.","comment":"String: type 0"},
        "5141": {"comment":"Keep the type byte's address"},
        "5142": {"comment":"The value follows"},
        "5146": {"comment":"Numeric: skip"},
        "5148": {"comment":"A string of up to 18 characters"},
        "5153": {"comment":"Z: no number"},
        "5154": {"synopsis":"A number: zero. Then marks the record new.","comment":"A number, 0"},
        "5157": {"comment":"The type byte"},
        "5158": {"comment":"Bit 7: just created"},
        "5160": {"synopsis":"C = the type byte, DE = the value, HL = the text pointer; returns.","comment":"C = the type byte"},
        "5161": {"comment":"The value"},
        "5162": {"comment":"DE = the value, HL = the text"},
        "5164": {"synopsis":"Raises error 5 (Bad subscript).","comment":"Error 5: Bad subscript"},
        "5166": {"synopsis":"Gives the address of the value of the name just read by NEXT_ITEM (the value in DE', the\ntype byte in C'), then fetches the next token. Type bit 0 marks an array: '(' and one index\nper dimension follow, separated by ','; the value part is the number of dimensions, a size\nword for each, then the elements, row by row (the last index runs fastest). An index not\nbelow its size is error 5 (Bad subscript). A number element is 6 bytes, a string element its\nlongest length plus 2. The type byte's bits: 0 an array, 1 numeric, 2 a DEF function, 3 a\nbuilt-in function, 7 just created. Entry (VAR_ADDR_ALT): already in the alternate set.\nExit: HL = the value (or element), C = the type byte, A = the next token.","comment":"DE' = the variable's value"},
        "5167": {"synopsis":"VAR_ADDR for a caller already in the alternate registers ($E280)."},
        "5168": {"comment":"Bit 0: an array?"},
        "5170": {"comment":"A = the type byte"},
        "5172": {"comment":"HL = the value"},
        "5173": {"comment":"C = the type byte"},
        "5174": {"comment":"Not an array: next token"},
        "5177": {"comment":"Must be '('"},
        "5182": {"comment":"The index sum so far: 0"},
        "5185": {"comment":"A = the number of dimensions"},
        "5186": {"comment":"HL = the first size"},
        "5187": {"comment":"DE = it, HL = 0"},
        "5188": {"comment":"Keep the type byte"},
        "5189": {"synopsis":"Reads one index and checks it against its size.","comment":"Keep the sum"},
        "5190": {"comment":"Keep the count; Z on the first"},
        "5191": {"comment":"A ',' before each other index"},
        "5196": {"comment":"HL = the index"},
        "5199": {"comment":"DE = the size's address"},
        "5200": {"comment":"BC = the size"},
        "5203": {"comment":"DE = the next size"},
        "5205": {"comment":"The index below the size?"},
        "5208": {"comment":"No: bad subscript"},
        "5210": {"comment":"HL = the index again"},
        "5211": {"comment":"A = the dimensions left"},
        "5213": {"comment":"Keep the next size's address"},
        "5214": {"synopsis":"Multiplies the index by each later size (MUL16).","comment":"Any size after this one?"},
        "5215": {"comment":"No: add it in"},
        "5217": {"comment":"HL = the size's address"},
        "5218": {"comment":"BC = that size"},
        "5221": {"comment":"Keep the next size's address"},
        "5222": {"comment":"Keep the count"},
        "5223": {"comment":"HL = HL x BC"},
        "5228": {"comment":"DE = the next size's address"},
        "5229": {"comment":"More sizes?"},
        "5231": {"synopsis":"Adds the index's share to the sum; another index?","comment":"DE = past the sizes"},
        "5232": {"comment":"A = the dimensions left"},
        "5233": {"comment":"BC = the sum so far"},
        "5234": {"comment":"Add this index's share"},
        "5235": {"comment":"Another index?"},
        "5238": {"comment":"Must end with ')'"},
        "5243": {"comment":"HL = the elements, DE = the element number"},
        "5244": {"comment":"C = the type byte"},
        "5245": {"comment":"Numeric?"},
        "5247": {"comment":"A number is 6 bytes"},
        "5250": {"comment":"Numeric: go on"},
        "5252": {"comment":"A string: its longest length"},
        "5253": {"comment":"Plus the two length bytes"},
        "5255": {"synopsis":"HL = the first element + the element number x the element's size.","comment":"Keep the first element's address"},
        "5256": {"comment":"HL = the element number x its size"},
        "5259": {"comment":"The first element"},
        "5260": {"comment":"HL = the element"},
        "5262": {"synopsis":"Calculator operation 3 (and the statement word of token $CC, which no keyword produces):\nsubtracts the top number from the second, by negating it (CALC_NEGATE) and falling into\nCALC_ADD.\nEntry: IY = the top number.\nExit: IY = the result, in the second's place. AF is kept.","comment":"Keep the flags"},
        "5263": {"comment":"Negate the top number (CALC_NEGATE)"},
        "5266": {"comment":"Then add"},
        "5267": {"synopsis":"Calculator operation 0 (and the statement word of token $CF, which no keyword produces):\nadds the top number to the second and drops the top.\n1. Shifts the mantissa of the one with the smaller exponent right by the difference\n   (MANT_SHIFT_RIGHT), giving both the larger exponent.\n2. Same signs: adds the mantissas (BCD_ADD7). Different signs: puts the negative one in\n   ten's complement with its sign cleared, adds, and when the carry byte shows a negative\n   sum ($99), complements it back and makes the result negative.\n3. Normalises (NORMALIZE: error 13 past exponent $7E).\nEntry: IY = the top number (the second at IY + 9).\nExit: IY = the result, in the second's place. AF is kept.","comment":"Keep the flags: the operations keep AF"},
        "5268": {"comment":"Remember the top's address"},
        "5270": {"comment":"One entry up"},
        "5273": {"comment":"IY = the second number"},
        "5275": {"comment":"IY = the top, stack = the second"},
        "5277": {"comment":"The top's exponent"},
        "5280": {"comment":"Without the sign"},
        "5282": {"comment":"C = it"},
        "5283": {"comment":"The second's exponent"},
        "5286": {"comment":"Without the sign"},
        "5288": {"comment":"Second - top"},
        "5289": {"comment":"Second not smaller: shift the top"},
        "5292": {"comment":"IY = the second"},
        "5294": {"comment":"Keep the difference"},
        "5295": {"comment":"A = top - second"},
        "5297": {"comment":"Shift the second right that far (MANT_SHIFT_RIGHT)"},
        "5300": {"comment":"B = the difference"},
        "5301": {"comment":"IY = the top"},
        "5303": {"comment":"The second's exponent"},
        "5306": {"comment":"Plus the difference"},
        "5307": {"comment":"Now equal to the top's"},
        "5310": {"comment":"Z: no more shift"},
        "5311": {"synopsis":"Shifts the top right by A digits when the exponents differ.","comment":"Shift the top right A digits (MANT_SHIFT_RIGHT)"},
        "5314": {"comment":"The two signs"},
        "5316": {"comment":"The same?"},
        "5320": {"comment":"Carry: they differ"},
        "5321": {"comment":"Same: add the top into the second"},
        "5324": {"comment":"IY = the second"},
        "5326": {"comment":"Same signs: done"},
        "5328": {"comment":"Negative? Ten's complement, sign off"},
        "5331": {"comment":"IY = the top"},
        "5333": {"comment":"The same for it"},
        "5336": {"comment":"Add the top into the second"},
        "5339": {"comment":"IY = the second"},
        "5341": {"comment":"The overflow byte"},
        "5344": {"comment":"Zero: the sum is positive"},
        "5347": {"comment":"Else complement it back"},
        "5350": {"comment":"And flip its sign"},
        "5358": {"synopsis":"Drops the top's address and falls into CALC_NORM_EXIT.","comment":"Drop the top's address"},
        "5359": {"synopsis":"Normalises the result (NORMALIZE), restores the caller's AF and returns; the end of the\ncalculator's operations (CALC_MUL_EXIT jumps here).","comment":"Normalise the result (NORMALIZE)"},
        "5362": {"comment":"The caller's flags"},
        "5364": {"synopsis":"Adds the 7 mantissa bytes (+1 to +7, the carry byte included) of the number at IY into the\nnumber at IY + 9 in BCD, with the carry flag as the carry in (clear at both callers).\nChanges: B, DE, HL.","comment":"Keep the flags"},
        "5365": {"comment":"DE = the top"},
        "5368": {"comment":"One entry up"},
        "5371": {"comment":"HL = the second"},
        "5372": {"comment":"7 bytes, from the lowest"},
        "5374": {"synopsis":"Adds one byte pair.","comment":"Next byte up"},
        "5376": {"comment":"The top's byte"},
        "5377": {"comment":"Plus the second's, with carry"},
        "5378": {"comment":"Kept in BCD"},
        "5379": {"comment":"Into the second"},
        "5380": {"comment":"Next byte"},
        "5382": {"comment":"The caller's flags"},
        "5384": {"synopsis":"A zero product: drops the top and sets the second to 0 (CLEAR_TOP).","comment":"One entry up"},
        "5387": {"comment":"Drop the top"},
        "5389": {"comment":"The result is 0"},
        "5392": {"comment":"The caller's flags"},
        "5394": {"synopsis":"Calculator operation 2 (and the statement word of token $CD, which no keyword produces):\nmultiplies the second number by the top one and drops the top.\n1. A zero factor or an exponent sum below $40 gives 0 (CALC_MUL_ZERO); a result exponent of\n   $7F or more is error 13 (Overflow, OVERFLOW_ERR). The sign is the XOR of the signs.\n2. Keeps HL', DE' and BC' and works in both register sets. Unpacks both mantissas into one\n   digit per byte below the top (BCD_UNPACK14), the second's at IY down, the top's at IY -\n   14 down, which clears both entries.\n3. For each of the second's 12 digits, from the lowest, multiplies the top's 12 digits by it\n   through the SYS low chip's BCD_TIMES_TABLE ($C003 + 10a + b, read at $157C-$159A) and adds\n   the products, with their carries, into a 16-digit sum at IY + 1 up (one digit per byte);\n   A' counts the position, so digits beyond the top's are taken as 0 and products below the\n   kept 16 digits are dropped.\n4. Packs the sum's 14 highest digits into the second's place (BCD_PACK14), writes the type\n   and exponent and normalises (CALC_NORM_EXIT).\nEntry: IY = the top number.\nExit: IY = the product, in the second's place. AF is kept.","comment":"Keep the flags: the operations keep AF"},
        "5395": {"comment":"The second's highest digits"},
        "5398": {"comment":"Zero: the product is 0"},
        "5401": {"comment":"The top's highest digits"},
        "5404": {"comment":"Zero: the product is 0"},
        "5407": {"comment":"The second's exponent"},
        "5410": {"comment":"The top's"},
        "5413": {"comment":"Their signs"},
        "5414": {"comment":"H bit 7: the product's sign"},
        "5415": {"comment":"A = the second's exponent again"},
        "5416": {"comment":"Without the sign"},
        "5418": {"comment":"L = it"},
        "5419": {"comment":"The top's exponent"},
        "5422": {"comment":"Without the sign"},
        "5424": {"comment":"Their sum"},
        "5425": {"comment":"Less the bias $40"},
        "5427": {"comment":"Too small: the product is 0"},
        "5429": {"comment":"Too big: overflow"},
        "5432": {"comment":"$7F is too big as well"},
        "5436": {"comment":"A = the exponent"},
        "5437": {"comment":"L = it, H bit 7 = the sign"},
        "5438": {"comment":"Keep them"},
        "5439": {"comment":"Keep HL', the sign in HL'"},
        "5441": {"comment":"Keep DE' and BC'"},
        "5443": {"comment":"Sign and exponent again"},
        "5444": {"comment":"HL = the top"},
        "5447": {"comment":"DE = the top"},
        "5449": {"comment":"16 up"},
        "5452": {"comment":"HL = the second's highest byte"},
        "5453": {"comment":"Its 14 digits, one per byte, below IY"},
        "5456": {"comment":"Keep the end of them"},
        "5457": {"comment":"Clear the second's type byte"},
        "5458": {"comment":"One down"},
        "5459": {"comment":"Clear the top's exponent"},
        "5460": {"comment":"The top's highest byte"},
        "5461": {"comment":"Its 14 digits, one per byte, below"},
        "5464": {"comment":"Ten up from the end"},
        "5468": {"comment":"BC = the top's 12th digit from the top"},
        "5470": {"comment":"19 up"},
        "5473": {"comment":"HL = IY + 1: the 16-digit product"},
        "5474": {"comment":"DE = the second's digits"},
        "5475": {"comment":"From the lowest"},
        "5476": {"comment":"Keep the product's address"},
        "5477": {"comment":"A' = 2: the first digit's position"},
        "5480": {"comment":"Main C = 12 digits of the second"},
        "5484": {"synopsis":"One digit of the second number: its products with the top's digits.","comment":"HL = the product's lowest digit"},
        "5485": {"comment":"Keep it"},
        "5486": {"comment":"Keep the top's digit pointer"},
        "5487": {"comment":"A = the second's next digit"},
        "5488": {"comment":"A = the position counter"},
        "5490": {"comment":"Keep it for the next digit"},
        "5491": {"comment":"A = the digit"},
        "5492": {"comment":"Twice it"},
        "5493": {"comment":"Zero: nothing to add"},
        "5495": {"comment":"D = 2 x digit"},
        "5496": {"comment":"x 8"},
        "5498": {"comment":"10 x digit"},
        "5499": {"comment":"Its row in BCD_TIMES_TABLE"},
        "5500": {"comment":"BCD_TIMES_TABLE's page ($C000)"},
        "5502": {"comment":"No carry yet"},
        "5504": {"comment":"16 product digits"},
        "5506": {"comment":"A = the position counter"},
        "5507": {"synopsis":"One product digit position: the table's product and the carry.","comment":"Inside the top's digits?"},
        "5510": {"comment":"Below them: any carry left?"},
        "5512": {"comment":"No: this digit is done"},
        "5514": {"synopsis":"No top digit here: adds only the carry.","comment":"Only the carry"},
        "5515": {"comment":"Nothing from the table"},
        "5518": {"synopsis":"A top digit: digit x digit from BCD_TIMES_TABLE.","comment":"Past the top's digits?"},
        "5520": {"comment":"Yes: only the carry"},
        "5522": {"comment":"Counter back to A'"},
        "5523": {"comment":"The top's digit"},
        "5526": {"comment":"Plus the row"},
        "5527": {"comment":"BCD_TIMES_TABLE is at $C003 + 10a + b"},
        "5530": {"comment":"A = digit x digit in BCD"},
        "5531": {"synopsis":"Adds the product and carry into the sum's digit; the tens become the next carry.","comment":"Plus the carry"},
        "5532": {"comment":"Kept in BCD"},
        "5533": {"comment":"Plus the product digit"},
        "5535": {"comment":"Kept in BCD"},
        "5536": {"comment":"Clear it"},
        "5538": {"comment":"Low digit in, A = high digit"},
        "5540": {"comment":"Next product digit"},
        "5541": {"comment":"Next top digit"},
        "5543": {"comment":"The high digit"},
        "5547": {"comment":"Is the next carry"},
        "5548": {"comment":"A = the position counter"},
        "5549": {"comment":"One position on"},
        "5550": {"comment":"The next product digit"},
        "5552": {"synopsis":"Next digit of the second number, the top's digits one further down.","comment":"A = the counter at this digit's start"},
        "5553": {"comment":"One more for the next"},
        "5554": {"comment":"Back into A'"},
        "5555": {"comment":"One digit of the second fewer"},
        "5557": {"comment":"BC = the top's digit pointer"},
        "5558": {"comment":"One digit further down"},
        "5559": {"comment":"The second's next digit"},
        "5560": {"comment":"All 12 done?"},
        "5562": {"comment":"HL = the product's lowest digit"},
        "5563": {"comment":"15 up"},
        "5566": {"comment":"The product's highest digit"},
        "5567": {"comment":"Packed from the top down"},
        "5569": {"comment":"Pack 14 digits into 7 bytes"},
        "5572": {"comment":"IY = the second's place"},
        "5575": {"comment":"H = the sign, L = the exponent"},
        "5576": {"comment":"The sign"},
        "5579": {"comment":"Plus the exponent"},
        "5580": {"comment":"The result's exponent"},
        "5583": {"comment":"The type of a number entry"},
        "5587": {"synopsis":"Restores BC', DE' and HL' and normalises (CALC_NORM_EXIT). The division's end ($168B) jumps\nhere too.","comment":"Restore BC', DE', HL'"},
        "5591": {"comment":"Normalise and return"},
        "5594": {"synopsis":"Packs 14 digits, one per byte at DE going down (the highest first), into 7 BCD bytes at HL\ngoing down. HL may start at DE.\nExit: HL and DE below the bytes used.\nChanges: AF, B.","comment":"7 bytes"},
        "5596": {"synopsis":"Packs two digits into one byte.","comment":"The higher digit"},
        "5597": {"comment":"One down"},
        "5598": {"comment":"Into the low nibble"},
        "5600": {"comment":"The lower digit"},
        "5601": {"comment":"One down"},
        "5602": {"comment":"Higher digit up, lower in"},
        "5604": {"comment":"Next byte down"},
        "5605": {"comment":"All 7?"},
        "5608": {"synopsis":"Unpacks the 7 BCD bytes at HL going down (the highest first) into 14 digits, one per byte at\nDE going down, and clears the bytes.\nExit: A = 0, HL 7 and DE 14 below where they started.\nChanges: B.","comment":"7 bytes"},
        "5610": {"synopsis":"Unpacks one byte into two digits.","comment":"0 into the low nibble"},
        "5611": {"comment":"A = the high digit"},
        "5613": {"comment":"Store it"},
        "5614": {"comment":"One down"},
        "5615": {"comment":"0 again"},
        "5616": {"comment":"A = the low digit, byte cleared"},
        "5618": {"comment":"Store it"},
        "5619": {"comment":"One down"},
        "5620": {"comment":"Next byte down"},
        "5621": {"comment":"All 7?"},
        "5623": {"comment":"A = 0"},
        "5625": {"synopsis":"Raises error 11 (Cannot divide by 0).","comment":"Error 11: Cannot divide by 0"},
        "5627": {"synopsis":"Calculator operation 1 (and the statement word of token $CE, which no keyword produces):\ndivides the second number by the top one and drops the top.\n1. A zero divisor is error 11 (Cannot divide by 0); a zero dividend is the result as it is\n   (POP_DROP_NUMBER drops the divisor).\n2. Keeps HL', DE', BC' and both exponents, keeps the divisor's address in $171C and pushes a\n   zero number below it for the quotient.\n3. Long division (DIV_* at DIV_DIGIT on): subtracts the divisor's mantissa from the\n   dividend's while no borrow appears, counting the quotient digit up (GET_DIGIT,\n   SET_DIGIT), then shifts the dividend a digit left (MANT_SHIFT_LEFT), for 12 quotient\n   digits.\n4. Copies the quotient over the dividend, sets its exponent to the dividend's - the\n   divisor's + $40 with the XOR of the signs, and ends through CALC_MUL_EXIT. An exponent\n   outside $00-$7F gives 0, for an overflow as well as an underflow: X=1E60/1E-60 left X = 0\n   (observed).\nEntry: IY = the divisor (the dividend at IY + 9).\nExit: IY = the quotient, in the dividend's place. AF is kept.","comment":"Keep the flags: the operations keep AF"},
        "5628": {"comment":"The divisor's highest digits"},
        "5631": {"comment":"Zero?"},
        "5632": {"comment":"Divisor 0: error $0B (divide by 0)"},
        "5634": {"comment":"Dividend's leading byte"},
        "5638": {"comment":"Dividend 0: drop the divisor, the result is 0"},
        "5641": {"comment":"Save the text pointer and the other alternate registers"},
        "5645": {"comment":"The divisor's address"},
        "5650": {"comment":"IY = the dividend"},
        "5652": {"comment":"L = the divisor's exponent"},
        "5655": {"comment":"H = the dividend's exponent"},
        "5658": {"comment":"Keep both exponents; HL = the divisor"},
        "5659": {"comment":"$171C = the divisor's address"},
        "5663": {"comment":"IY = the divisor; the dividend's address kept"},
        "5665": {"comment":"Push 0, the quotient, with a room check"},
        "5668": {"comment":"First quotient digit: position 4"},
        "5670": {"synopsis":"Finds the next quotient digit (position B, kept in the main register set): sets up the\ndivisor, dividend and scratch pointers in the alternate set.","comment":"Divisor pointer"},
        "5674": {"comment":"-9"},
        "5683": {"comment":"HL = the dividend"},
        "5685": {"comment":"DE = the dividend"},
        "5686": {"comment":"HL = scratch space 18 bytes below the divisor"},
        "5688": {"synopsis":"Subtracts the divisor from the dividend into the scratch entry; while no borrow comes out,\nadds 1 to the quotient digit, copies the difference over the dividend and tries again.","comment":"7 bytes: the carry byte and six BCD bytes"},
        "5691": {"synopsis":"One BCD byte of the trial subtraction."},
        "5692": {"comment":"Dividend byte"},
        "5695": {"comment":"Minus the divisor byte"},
        "5697": {"comment":"BCD adjust"},
        "5699": {"comment":"Into the scratch entry"},
        "5702": {"comment":"Borrowed out of the top byte?"},
        "5707": {"comment":"This quotient digit"},
        "5710": {"comment":"Plus 1"},
        "5714": {"comment":"Divisor pointer again"},
        "5718": {"comment":"Difference over the dividend"},
        "5723": {"comment":"Subtract again"},
        "5725": {"synopsis":"The divisor no longer fits: multiplies the dividend by 10 and goes on with the next quotient\ndigit until position 15 is done; then copies the quotient over the dividend and works out\nits exponent and sign.","comment":"IY = the dividend"},
        "5727": {"comment":"Dividend times 10"},
        "5733": {"comment":"Next quotient digit"},
        "5735": {"comment":"All 12 digits (positions 4-15) done?"},
        "5740": {"comment":"The quotient's carry byte (divisor - 2)"},
        "5745": {"comment":"8 bytes: the quotient over the dividend"},
        "5750": {"comment":"IY = the result"},
        "5752": {"comment":"The two exponents"},
        "5753": {"comment":"Result sign: the signs differ?"},
        "5758": {"comment":"Exponents without their signs"},
        "5762": {"comment":"Dividend's minus the divisor's"},
        "5764": {"comment":"Plus the bias"},
        "5766": {"comment":"With the sign"},
        "5770": {"comment":"Unsigned exponent again"},
        "5771": {"synopsis":"A result exponent within $00-$7F goes to the end of addition ($15D3: restores the alternate\nregisters, normalises, returns); one outside it makes the result 0.","comment":"Within $00-$7F: restore, normalise, return"},
        "5774": {"comment":"Out of range: the result is 0"},
        "5779": {"synopsis":"Compares the second number on the expression stack with the top one and drops both. Called\nafter the operands of a comparison are evaluated.\nEntry: two numbers on the IY stack.\nExit: IY above both; flags and A from the difference: P (non-zero) when the second number is\ngreater, M when it is smaller, Z when they are equal.\n1. Different signs decide at once.\n2. For two negative numbers the operands swap, so the larger magnitude counts as smaller.\n3. Then the exponents, then the digits from the most significant down (the carry byte at +7\n   first).","comment":"The top number's address"},
        "5785": {"comment":"The second number"},
        "5788": {"comment":"DE = the top number's exponent"},
        "5789": {"comment":"Past both numbers"},
        "5790": {"comment":"Drop both"},
        "5793": {"comment":"HL = the second number's exponent"},
        "5794": {"comment":"Second number's sign as $40 or 0"},
        "5799": {"comment":"Top number's sign as $40 or 0"},
        "5803": {"comment":"Top's sign minus the second's"},
        "5804": {"comment":"Signs differ: done"},
        "5805": {"comment":"Both negative?"},
        "5808": {"comment":"Negative: compare the other way round"},
        "5809": {"synopsis":"Same sign: compares the exponents.","comment":"Exponent without the sign"},
        "5814": {"comment":"The other exponent"},
        "5818": {"comment":"Exponents differ: done"},
        "5820": {"comment":"7 bytes, the carry byte first"},
        "5822": {"synopsis":"Compares the digits byte by byte from +7 down, high digit first.","comment":"High digit, halved"},
        "5827": {"comment":"The other high digit"},
        "5831": {"comment":"Differ: done"},
        "5833": {"comment":"Low digit"},
        "5837": {"comment":"The other low digit"},
        "5840": {"comment":"Differ: done"},
        "5842": {"comment":"Next lower byte"},
        "5846": {"comment":"Equal: Z set"},
        "5847": {"synopsis":"Compares the second string on the expression stack with the top one and drops both (string\nentries: type $01, a length byte, the text).\nEntry: two strings on the IY stack.\nExit: IY above both; P with A = 1 when the second string is greater, M with A = $FF when it\nis smaller, Z with A = 0 when they are equal. The length bytes are compared first, so a\nshorter string is always the smaller one; strings of one length compare by their character\ncodes. Observed: \"B\"<\"AA\" is true, \"AB\"<\"B\" false.","comment":"The top string"},
        "5850": {"comment":"Its length byte"},
        "5852": {"comment":"C = the top string's length"},
        "5855": {"comment":"Past the top string"},
        "5858": {"comment":"The second string's length byte"},
        "5859": {"comment":"Second length shorter?"},
        "5863": {"comment":"No: take the top one's"},
        "5864": {"synopsis":"Drops both strings and counts the shorter length plus the length byte.","comment":"Past the second string"},
        "5867": {"comment":"Drop both strings"},
        "5870": {"comment":"Count = the shorter length + 1"},
        "5872": {"comment":"DE = the second string, HL = the top one"},
        "5874": {"synopsis":"Compares the length bytes, then the characters, until a pair differs or the count runs out.","comment":"Next byte of the second string"},
        "5876": {"comment":"Compare with the top string's"},
        "5878": {"comment":"All compared?"},
        "5881": {"comment":"Equal: go on"},
        "5883": {"synopsis":"Forms the result from the last pair compared.","comment":"The top string's byte"},
        "5885": {"comment":"Minus it from the second string's"},
        "5890": {"comment":"Equal"},
        "5891": {"comment":"Second smaller: A = $FF"},
        "5892": {"comment":"Second greater: A = 1"},
        "5895": {"synopsis":"Takes the ten's complement of the top number's digits (+1 to +7) when the number is\nnegative; the sign bit stays. INT_FN calls it before and after cutting off the fraction.","comment":"Negative?"},
        "5899": {"comment":"No: nothing to do"},
        "5900": {"synopsis":"Takes the ten's complement of the top number's seven digit bytes (+1 to +7, 0 minus them in\nBCD).\nChanges: A, DE, HL."},
        "5903": {"comment":"7 bytes; D = 0"},
        "5906": {"comment":"No borrow yet"},
        "5907": {"synopsis":"One BCD byte of the complement, the borrow running upwards."},
        "5908": {"comment":"0 minus the byte"},
        "5910": {"comment":"BCD adjust"},
        "5912": {"comment":"Next byte"},
        "5916": {"synopsis":"Turns a negative top number into its ten's complement and clears its sign bit, so that\naddition can add signed operands; a positive number is left alone.","comment":"Negative?"},
        "5921": {"comment":"Ten's complement"},
        "5924": {"comment":"Then clear the sign"},
        "5926": {"synopsis":"Calculator operation 4: negates the top number (flips the exponent's sign bit) unless it is\n0.\nChanges: A.","comment":"Zero?"},
        "5930": {"comment":"Zero stays positive"},
        "5931": {"synopsis":"Flips the sign bit of the top number's exponent.","comment":"Flip the exponent's sign bit"},
        "5940": {"synopsis":"Normalises the top number: the leading digit goes to the high digit of +6 and the carry byte\n+7 is emptied.\n1. All digits zero: the exponent becomes 0 (ZERO_IF_EMPTY).\n2. A digit in the carry byte: shifts one digit right and raises the exponent; error $0D\n   (overflow) when the exponent is $7E already.\n3. Otherwise shifts left one digit at a time, lowering the exponent; going below exponent 0\n   makes the number 0.","comment":"All digits 0: exponent 0, done"},
        "5944": {"comment":"Anything in the carry byte?"},
        "5950": {"comment":"Yes: one digit right"},
        "5955": {"comment":"The exponent without the sign"},
        "5960": {"comment":"Already $7E: error $0D (overflow)"},
        "5965": {"comment":"One power of ten up"},
        "5969": {"synopsis":"Shifts the digits left until the leading digit is not 0.","comment":"Leading digit (+6's high digit)"},
        "5974": {"comment":"Non-zero: normalised"},
        "5975": {"comment":"One digit left"},
        "5978": {"comment":"Old exponent"},
        "5981": {"comment":"One power of ten down"},
        "5986": {"comment":"Not below $00: check again"},
        "5988": {"comment":"Underflow: the result is 0"},
        "5991": {"synopsis":"Rounds the top number to 10 digits: clears the lowest digit byte (+1) and, when it held 50\nor more, adds 1 from +2 up, then normalises. NUM_TO_TEXT and calculator operation 13 call\nit.\nChanges: A, B, flags; HL kept.","comment":"Keep the caller's HL"},
        "5995": {"comment":"The lowest digit byte, +1"},
        "5997": {"comment":"Two lowest digits 50 or more?"},
        "5999": {"comment":"Cleared either way"},
        "6001": {"comment":"Below 50: done"},
        "6003": {"comment":"Up to six bytes, +2 to +7"},
        "6005": {"synopsis":"Adds 1 to the next byte up while the carry runs on."},
        "6007": {"comment":"Plus 1"},
        "6009": {"comment":"BCD adjust"},
        "6011": {"comment":"No carry: stop"},
        "6015": {"synopsis":"Normalises after the carry.","comment":"A carry into +7 is shifted back"},
        "6018": {"synopsis":"Restores HL and returns."},
        "6020": {"synopsis":"Shifts the top number's digits (+1 to +7) one digit left, multiplying them by 10; the\nexponent is unchanged.\nExit: A = the digit shifted out of +7.\nChanges: A, B, HL.","comment":"The top number"},
        "6023": {"comment":"7 bytes, +1 to +7"},
        "6025": {"comment":"A 0 digit comes in at the bottom"},
        "6026": {"synopsis":"One byte of the left shift."},
        "6027": {"comment":"Digit up through the byte"},
        "6032": {"synopsis":"Shifts the top number's digits (+1 to +7) right by A digits, dividing them by 10^A; digits\nshifted below +1 are lost and the exponent is unchanged. A of 14 or more clears the digits.\nEntry: A = the number of digits.\nChanges: A, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"14 or more digits?"},
        "6036": {"comment":"B = 0 (fill), C counts whole bytes"},
        "6042": {"comment":"Keep the entry's address"},
        "6045": {"synopsis":"Counts the whole bytes in A (A - 2 each time) into C, moving HL up.","comment":"Next byte up"},
        "6046": {"comment":"Two digits a byte"},
        "6051": {"comment":"Even count: no whole byte left over?"},
        "6053": {"comment":"Destination: +1"},
        "6055": {"comment":"Bytes to keep: 7 minus the bytes shifted"},
        "6059": {"comment":"Move them down"},
        "6061": {"comment":"HL = the first byte to clear"},
        "6062": {"comment":"Number of bytes to clear"},
        "6065": {"synopsis":"Clears the bytes freed at the top.","comment":"0"},
        "6070": {"comment":"Count's remainder"},
        "6071": {"synopsis":"An odd count still needs one digit shift."},
        "6072": {"comment":"An odd digit left?"},
        "6074": {"comment":"From the carry byte, +7"},
        "6078": {"synopsis":"One byte of the one-digit right shift, from +7 down.","comment":"Digit down through the byte"},
        "6084": {"synopsis":"14 digits or more: clears the number (CLEAR_TOP) but keeps its exponent byte.","comment":"Keep the exponent"},
        "6087": {"comment":"Clear the number"},
        "6090": {"comment":"Put the exponent back"},
        "6094": {"synopsis":"GET_DIGIT for code running in EXTH: returns through EXTH_RETURN, which takes the return\naddress into HL and jumps to it through EXTH_JUMP with port 02h = $F0 (EXT, the EXTH ROM,\npaged in at $C000-$FFFF). No reference to $F7CE was found in either SYS chip.","comment":"Return through EXTH_RETURN"},
        "6098": {"synopsis":"Reads digit B of the top number (see DIGIT_ADDR for the positions).\nEntry: B = the digit position.\nExit: A = the digit (0-9), Z when it is 0.\nChanges: A, flags; BC and HL kept.","comment":"Keep the caller's HL"},
        "6100": {"comment":"HL = the byte; carry: low digit"},
        "6105": {"comment":"High digit: rotate it down"},
        "6110": {"synopsis":"Keeps the low digit.","comment":"The digit"},
        "6115": {"synopsis":"Writes digit B of the top number (see DIGIT_ADDR for the positions).\nEntry: B = the digit position, A = the digit (0-15).\nChanges: A, flags; BC and HL kept."},
        "6117": {"comment":"HL = the byte; carry: low digit"},
        "6120": {"comment":"Low digit: keep the high one"},
        "6124": {"comment":"High digit: value to the high half"},
        "6133": {"comment":"Keep the low digit"},
        "6135": {"synopsis":"Masks the other digit and merges the new one.","comment":"Merge the digit"},
        "6141": {"synopsis":"Finds the byte holding digit B of the top number: positions 2-3 are the carry byte +7, 4-15\nthe digits from the most significant (4, the high digit of +6) to the least (15, the low\ndigit of +1).\nEntry: B = the position, A = a value.\nExit: HL = IY + 8 - B/2, A = the byte, C = the value from A, carry when B is odd (the low\ndigit); B halved.","comment":"The top number"},
        "6144": {"comment":"Byte number; carry: the low digit"},
        "6147": {"comment":"Offset 8 - B/2"},
        "6155": {"comment":"C = the value passed in A"},
        "6156": {"comment":"The byte"},
        "6158": {"synopsis":"Writes the top number as text into the buffer at $1930: the length at $1930, the characters\nfrom $1931. STR$ and PRINT's number output (PRINT_NUM_BARE) call it.\nExit: HL = $1930; E = the length.\nChanges: the top number is rounded and made positive; A, BC, DE.\n1. 0 gives \"0\"; a negative number starts with '-'.\n2. Rounds to 10 digits and finds the last non-zero digit.\n3. From 1 up to 10^10 the number is written plainly, the point only when digits follow it;\n   below 1 likewise, with the point first and no 0 before it (\".05\"), while at most 10\n   digits follow the point.\n4. Otherwise one digit, the point (when digits follow) and an exponent part: 'E', a sign and\n   two digits.","comment":"Zero?"},
        "6162": {"comment":"The text buffer, after its length byte"},
        "6165": {"comment":"'0' in case it is"},
        "6169": {"comment":"Negative?"},
        "6175": {"comment":"'-'"},
        "6178": {"comment":"Make it positive"},
        "6181": {"synopsis":"Rounds the number and finds its last non-zero digit.","comment":"Round to 10 digits"},
        "6184": {"comment":"Search from position 13 down"},
        "6186": {"synopsis":"Steps down from position 13 to the last non-zero digit."},
        "6188": {"comment":"A digit position?"},
        "6190": {"comment":"Read the digit"},
        "6193": {"comment":"Zero: look further up"},
        "6195": {"comment":"C = the last non-zero digit's position"},
        "6196": {"comment":"Start with the leading digit"},
        "6198": {"comment":"The exponent"},
        "6201": {"comment":"D = where the point goes"},
        "6206": {"comment":"10^10 or more?"},
        "6210": {"comment":"1 or more?"},
        "6214": {"comment":"Below 1: exponent minus the last position"},
        "6217": {"comment":"More than 10 digits after the point?"},
        "6221": {"comment":"No: start at the point, zeros first"},
        "6224": {"synopsis":"1 to 10^10: prints at least the digits before the point.","comment":"Last digit before the point?"},
        "6228": {"comment":"Print up to the point, no point"},
        "6229": {"comment":"No exponent part"},
        "6232": {"synopsis":"10^10 or more: the exponent form.","comment":"Carry: exponent part follows"},
        "6233": {"synopsis":"Exponent form (carry set): the point after the first digit.","comment":"Point after the first digit"},
        "6235": {"synopsis":"Ends the digits after the last one to print.","comment":"End after the last digit"},
        "6236": {"synopsis":"Writes the digits from position B up to C, the point at position D.","comment":"Keep the exponent-form flag"},
        "6238": {"synopsis":"One position: the point when it is reached, then the digit.","comment":"Point position reached?"},
        "6241": {"comment":"'.'"},
        "6244": {"synopsis":"Writes the digit; positions before 4 (leading zeros) write '0'.","comment":"A digit of the number?"},
        "6247": {"comment":"Before it: '0'"},
        "6249": {"comment":"Read the digit"},
        "6252": {"comment":"As a character"},
        "6256": {"comment":"Next position"},
        "6258": {"comment":"All written?"},
        "6261": {"comment":"Exponent form?"},
        "6264": {"comment":"'E'"},
        "6267": {"comment":"'+'"},
        "6269": {"comment":"Power of ten"},
        "6272": {"comment":"Not negative?"},
        "6274": {"comment":"'-' and the size"},
        "6278": {"synopsis":"Writes the power of ten as two digits.","comment":"B counts the tens"},
        "6281": {"synopsis":"Counts the tens by subtracting 10."},
        "6282": {"comment":"C = the remainder"},
        "6283": {"comment":"Another ten?"},
        "6287": {"comment":"Tens digit"},
        "6292": {"comment":"'0' plus the units"},
        "6295": {"synopsis":"Steps past the last character written.","comment":"Past the last character"},
        "6296": {"synopsis":"Stores the text's length at $1930.","comment":"Length = end - $1931"},
        "6302": {"comment":"HL = the buffer"},
        "6303": {"comment":"Store the length"},
        "6305": {"synopsis":"Reads one text item from the text at HL and pushes it on the expression stack as a string\n(type $01, the length, the characters). Reached when READ or INPUT reads into a string\n($0289) and from $1908 for an item starting with a quote.\nEntry: HL = the text.\nExit: HL = the byte ending the item; IY = the new string; BC, DE kept.\n1. Skips spaces. A quoted item runs to its closing quote or the end of the line ($FF); a\n   doubled quote stands for one quote.\n2. An unquoted item runs to ',', '!' or a byte $FD-$FF, keeping its spaces.\n3. Checks for room (ROOM_256) and copies the characters from the end down; a control\n   character is stored with bit 7 set."},
        "6307": {"comment":"Character count, -1"},
        "6309": {"comment":"Not quoted yet"},
        "6311": {"synopsis":"Skips spaces before the item.","comment":"Next text byte"},
        "6313": {"comment":"Skip spaces"},
        "6317": {"comment":"A quote?"},
        "6321": {"comment":"Quoted"},
        "6322": {"synopsis":"Takes the next character of the item.","comment":"Next character"},
        "6324": {"synopsis":"Counts the character and checks for the item's end.","comment":"Count it"},
        "6325": {"comment":"Quoted?"},
        "6329": {"comment":"End of the line?"},
        "6333": {"comment":"A quote?"},
        "6337": {"comment":"Doubled quote?"},
        "6339": {"comment":"Yes: one quote in the text"},
        "6341": {"comment":"Closing quote: the end"},
        "6343": {"synopsis":"An unquoted item ends at ',', '!' or a byte $FD-$FF.","comment":"','?"},
        "6347": {"comment":"'!'?"},
        "6351": {"comment":"Not $FD-$FF (':', '!' token, end of line)?"},
        "6355": {"synopsis":"The item's end: copies it onto the expression stack.","comment":"Back onto the end"},
        "6356": {"comment":"Text pointer to return"},
        "6358": {"comment":"B = the length"},
        "6359": {"comment":"DE = the expression stack"},
        "6362": {"comment":"Room for 256 bytes?"},
        "6365": {"comment":"Any characters left?"},
        "6366": {"synopsis":"Copies the next character down, skipping the closing quote and one of each doubled pair."},
        "6367": {"comment":"Next stack byte down"},
        "6370": {"comment":"Copy backwards from the end"},
        "6372": {"comment":"A quote?"},
        "6376": {"comment":"Quoted?"},
        "6380": {"comment":"Yes: skip one quote"},
        "6382": {"synopsis":"Stores the character.","comment":"Onto the stack"},
        "6383": {"comment":"A control character?"},
        "6387": {"comment":"Stored with bit 7 set"},
        "6392": {"synopsis":"Writes the length and the type byte and makes the string the stack top.","comment":"HL = the length byte's place"},
        "6394": {"comment":"Length"},
        "6396": {"comment":"Type $01: a string"},
        "6398": {"comment":"New stack top"},
        "6401": {"comment":"Text pointer at the end"},
        "6405": {"synopsis":"Reads a value from the text at HL for NEXT_ITEM: a string in quotes (READ_STR_ITEM) or a\nnumber onto the IY stack (READ_NUMBER). Anything else is error 1 (\"Not understood\"); a\nnumber too large is error $0D (\"Overflow\"), one too small becomes 0.","comment":"A string in quotes?"},
        "6408": {"comment":"Read the string (READ_STR_ITEM)"},
        "6410": {"comment":"A number onto the IY stack"},
        "6413": {"comment":"Nothing read: error 1, Not understood"},
        "6416": {"comment":"Carry = the exponent out of range"},
        "6417": {"comment":"In range"},
        "6418": {"synopsis":"Raises error $0D, \"Overflow\". Code all over this chip jumps here.","comment":"Error $0D, Overflow"},
        "6420": {"synopsis":"Reads a decimal number from the text at HL onto the IY stack.\n1. Pushes a zero entry (PUSH_ZERO) and keeps a pointer to its digits on the stack; skips\n   spaces and a sign ('-' sets bit 1 of D).\n2. Reads digits and decimal points. Leading zeros are skipped (after the point each lowers\n   the exponent); up to 10 significant digits go into the entry from the high nibble of IY+6\n   down (IY+1, the lowest digit byte, stays 0), later ones only count. E starts at $3F and\n   rises by one for each significant digit before the point.\n3. An 'E' (either case) takes an exponent: an optional sign ('+'/'-' or their tokens\n   $98/$A2) and digits, added to E.\n4. Stores E at IY+8, negates for a '-' (operation 4, CALC_NEGATE) and gives a zero exponent\n   0 (ZERO_IF_EMPTY); an out-of-range exponent with a '-' in it makes the number 0\n   (CLEAR_TOP). The flags in D: bit 0 a point, bit 1 a minus sign, bit 2 a significant\n   digit, bit 5 a negative exponent, bit 6 the exponent out of range, bit 7 a digit, point\n   or 'E' read.\nEntry: HL = the text.\nExit: HL = the first character after the number; carry set when something was read; bit 7 of\nA set when the exponent is out of range (not on an underflow).\nChanges: AF, HL, IY; BC and DE are kept.","comment":"Keep BC and DE"},
        "6422": {"comment":"A zero entry on the IY stack"},
        "6425": {"comment":"Save the text pointer"},
        "6426": {"comment":"HL = IY+7, the carry byte above the digits"},
        "6433": {"comment":"Digit pointer on the stack, text in HL"},
        "6434": {"comment":"E = the exponent, $3F before any digit"},
        "6436": {"comment":"B counts down the room for 10 digits"},
        "6438": {"synopsis":"Skips spaces before the number.","comment":"Next character"},
        "6440": {"comment":"A space?"},
        "6444": {"comment":"A plus sign?"},
        "6448": {"comment":"A minus sign?"},
        "6452": {"comment":"Bit 1 of D: negative"},
        "6454": {"synopsis":"Takes the next character of the mantissa.","comment":"Next character"},
        "6456": {"synopsis":"Handles one mantissa character: a point, a digit, or anything else, which ends it.","comment":"A decimal point?"},
        "6460": {"comment":"Above '9'?"},
        "6464": {"comment":"Below '0'?"},
        "6468": {"comment":"C = the digit"},
        "6469": {"comment":"Bit 7 of D: something read"},
        "6471": {"comment":"Carry = a point read (bit 0)"},
        "6473": {"comment":"Not a zero: a significant digit"},
        "6475": {"comment":"A zero after a significant digit?"},
        "6479": {"comment":"Leading zero before the point: ignore it"},
        "6481": {"comment":"Leading zero after the point: one lower"},
        "6484": {"synopsis":"A nonzero digit: from here on every digit is significant.","comment":"Bit 2 of D: significant digits started"},
        "6486": {"synopsis":"A significant digit before the point raises the exponent.","comment":"After the point?"},
        "6488": {"comment":"One more digit before the point"},
        "6489": {"synopsis":"Stores a significant digit while B leaves room for it; later digits are dropped.","comment":"Room for another digit?"},
        "6491": {"comment":"No: drop it"},
        "6494": {"comment":"HL = the digit pointer"},
        "6495": {"comment":"Odd count: the low nibble of this byte"},
        "6498": {"comment":"Even count: the next byte down"},
        "6499": {"synopsis":"Shifts the digit into the byte at HL.","comment":"The digit"},
        "6500": {"comment":"Into the low nibble, the old one up"},
        "6502": {"comment":"Text pointer back in HL"},
        "6505": {"synopsis":"A decimal point: the first one is noted, a second one ends the number.","comment":"A point counts as something read"},
        "6507": {"comment":"A point already?"},
        "6509": {"comment":"Bit 0 of D: a point read"},
        "6511": {"comment":"The first point: go on"},
        "6513": {"synopsis":"The mantissa ends: a half-filled digit byte gets a zero digit.","comment":"HL = the digit pointer"},
        "6514": {"comment":"Half a byte filled?"},
        "6519": {"comment":"Shift in a zero digit"},
        "6522": {"synopsis":"Back in the text: an 'E' starts an exponent, anything else ends the number.","comment":"The text pointer"},
        "6523": {"comment":"Back to the character that ended it"},
        "6525": {"comment":"In capitals"},
        "6528": {"comment":"An exponent?"},
        "6530": {"comment":"A = the exponent so far"},
        "6531": {"comment":"No: store it"},
        "6533": {"comment":"'E' counts as something read"},
        "6536": {"comment":"The exponent's sign"},
        "6537": {"comment":"Token $98, '+'?"},
        "6541": {"comment":"'+'?"},
        "6545": {"comment":"Token $A2, '-'?"},
        "6549": {"comment":"'-'?"},
        "6553": {"synopsis":"A negative exponent: bit 5 of D.","comment":"Bit 5 of D: a negative exponent"},
        "6555": {"synopsis":"Past the exponent's sign.","comment":"Past the sign"},
        "6556": {"synopsis":"Reads the exponent's digits into B.","comment":"B = the exponent's value"},
        "6558": {"comment":"Back one: the loop steps first"},
        "6559": {"synopsis":"Takes one exponent digit: B = 10B + the digit, while B stays below 128.","comment":"Next character"},
        "6561": {"comment":"Above '9'?"},
        "6565": {"comment":"Below '0'?"},
        "6569": {"comment":"C = the digit"},
        "6570": {"comment":"B x 2"},
        "6573": {"comment":"B x 8 + B x 2"},
        "6576": {"comment":"Plus the digit"},
        "6578": {"comment":"Below 128: next digit"},
        "6581": {"comment":"Bit 6 of D: out of range"},
        "6583": {"synopsis":"The exponent read, negated when negative, is added to E.","comment":"A = the exponent read"},
        "6584": {"comment":"A negative exponent?"},
        "6590": {"synopsis":"Forms the exponent byte; $7F or more (also a negative result) is out of range.","comment":"Plus the mantissa's exponent"},
        "6591": {"comment":"In range?"},
        "6595": {"comment":"No: bit 6 of D"},
        "6597": {"synopsis":"Stores the exponent and finishes the entry (sign, zero, underflow), then sets the exit\nflags.","comment":"Keep the flags in D"},
        "6598": {"comment":"The exponent byte"},
        "6601": {"comment":"A '-' before the number?"},
        "6603": {"comment":"Yes: negate (operation 4)"},
        "6606": {"comment":"A zero mantissa gets exponent 0"},
        "6610": {"comment":"Out of range (bit 6) with a negative exponent (bit 5)?"},
        "6615": {"comment":"Carry = something read (bit 7)"},
        "6617": {"comment":"Underflow: the number is 0"},
        "6620": {"comment":"Restore DE and BC"},
        "6623": {"synopsis":"Gives a number whose bytes IY+1 to IY+7 (the six digit bytes and the carry byte) are all\nzero the exponent 0, so zero has one form.\nExit: Z set when the number is zero.\nChanges: AF, B.","comment":"Keep HL"},
        "6624": {"comment":"HL = the entry"},
        "6627": {"comment":"7 bytes, IY+1 to IY+7"},
        "6630": {"synopsis":"ORs the next byte into A.","comment":"Any nonzero byte?"},
        "6632": {"comment":"Yes: done"},
        "6636": {"comment":"All zero: exponent 0"},
        "6639": {"synopsis":"Restores HL."},
        "6641": {"synopsis":"Checks that the IY stack has room for 256 bytes (ROOM_256) and falls into PUSH_ZERO.","comment":"Room for 256 bytes on the IY stack?"},
        "6644": {"synopsis":"Pushes a new number entry onto the IY stack and falls into CLEAR_TOP, which makes it 0.","comment":"Nine bytes down"},
        "6649": {"synopsis":"Makes the top entry of the IY stack the number 0: the type byte 9, then eight zero bytes.\nExit: carry set, A = 0; HL is kept.\nChanges: AF, B.","comment":"HL = the entry, old HL saved"},
        "6652": {"comment":"Type 9: a number"},
        "6654": {"comment":"Eight bytes to clear"},
        "6657": {"synopsis":"Clears the next byte.","comment":"Next byte"},
        "6661": {"comment":"Restore HL"},
        "6662": {"comment":"Carry set on exit"},
        "6664": {"synopsis":"Makes the top entry of the IY stack the number 1 (exponent $40, the digit 1 in the high\nnibble of IY+6).","comment":"Make it 0 first"},
        "6667": {"comment":"Exponent: 10^0"},
        "6671": {"comment":"The digit 1"},
        "6676": {"synopsis":"Reached from EXTH: its $FF96 jumps here through EXTH_JUMP with HL = $FA14 (nothing in SYS\nrefers to it). Makes the top entry 0 (CLEAR_TOP) and returns through EXTH_RETURN, which\npages EXTH back in and jumps to the address on the stack.","comment":"Return through EXTH_RETURN, back to EXTH"},
        "6680": {"comment":"Make the top entry 0"},
        "6682": {"synopsis":"Discards the word on top of the stack and falls into DROP_NUMBER.","comment":"Discard a word"},
        "6683": {"synopsis":"Drops the top number, 9 bytes, from the IY stack.\nChanges: DE.","comment":"One entry up"},
        "6689": {"synopsis":"Drops the top string entry from the IY stack: the length at IY+1 plus the type and length\nbytes.\nChanges: DE.","comment":"E = the string's length"},
        "6694": {"comment":"Plus its type and length bytes"},
        "6699": {"synopsis":"Pushes the signed 16-bit integer in HL onto the IY stack as a number.\n1. Pushes a 0 (PUSH_ZERO_ROOM) and keeps IY+1, the byte below the digits, in $171C.\n2. Takes HL's magnitude (ABS_HL) and shifts it out bit by bit from its highest 1, each time\n   doubling the BCD digits from IY+2 up and adding the bit; the number of digit bytes in use\n   grows as carries come out.\n3. Sets the exponent to $49 (the units in the low nibble of IY+2) with H's sign and\n   normalises (NORMALIZE).\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"A 0 on the IY stack"},
        "6702": {"comment":"$171C = IY+1, below the digits"},
        "6710": {"comment":"Keep the sign (H)"},
        "6711": {"comment":"HL = the magnitude"},
        "6714": {"comment":"A marker bit behind the number"},
        "6715": {"synopsis":"Shifts HL left until its highest 1 comes out into carry.","comment":"Until the first 1 comes out"},
        "6719": {"comment":"One digit byte in use"},
        "6721": {"comment":"Add that bit"},
        "6723": {"synopsis":"A carry out of the top digit byte: one more byte is in use and takes the carry.","comment":"One byte more for this carry"},
        "6724": {"comment":"And from now on"},
        "6725": {"comment":"Its operand is LD B,C: PI_DOUBLE enters there"},
        "6726": {"synopsis":"Entered on the operand of the LD A,$41 at $1A45, which is LD B,C: B = the digit bytes in\nuse."},
        "6727": {"synopsis":"Doubles the BCD digits and adds the carry, one byte at a time.","comment":"Next digit byte"},
        "6729": {"comment":"Twice it plus the carry"},
        "6730": {"comment":"In BCD"},
        "6732": {"comment":"Every byte in use"},
        "6734": {"comment":"A carry out: one byte more"},
        "6736": {"comment":"Next bit into carry"},
        "6738": {"synopsis":"Starts the next doubling until only the marker bit is left to shift out.","comment":"Back to the digits"},
        "6742": {"comment":"Bits left?"},
        "6744": {"comment":"A = the original H"},
        "6745": {"comment":"Its sign; exponent $49 (units at IY+2)"},
        "6752": {"comment":"Normalise (NORMALIZE)"},
        "6755": {"synopsis":"Pushes a numeric variable's value onto the IY stack: the six bytes at HL (five BCD bytes,\nthen the exponent) become IY+2 to IY+6 and IY+8; IY+1 (the lowest digit byte) and IY+7 (the\ncarry byte) are 0.\nEntry: HL = the value.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"Room on the IY stack?"},
        "6758": {"comment":"HL = the exponent byte"},
        "6762": {"comment":"DE = the new entry's IY+8"},
        "6766": {"comment":"The exponent"},
        "6768": {"comment":"IY+7, the carry byte, = 0"},
        "6771": {"comment":"Five digit bytes"},
        "6774": {"comment":"IY+1, the lowest digit byte, = 0"},
        "6775": {"comment":"Type 9: a number"},
        "6780": {"synopsis":"Pushes a string variable's value onto the IY stack as a string entry (type 1, then the\nlength and the characters). A string variable is its maximum length, the length, then the\ncharacters.\nEntry: HL = the variable (its maximum length byte).\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"Past the maximum length"},
        "6781": {"synopsis":"Pushes the length-prefixed string at HL onto the IY stack as a string entry.","comment":"C = the length"},
        "6784": {"comment":"Room on the IY stack?"},
        "6787": {"comment":"HL = the last character"},
        "6788": {"comment":"The length byte too"},
        "6789": {"comment":"DE = just below the stack"},
        "6793": {"comment":"Copy it down"},
        "6795": {"comment":"Type 1: a string"},
        "6797": {"synopsis":"Stores the type byte A at DE and makes the new entry the top of the IY stack.","comment":"The type byte"},
        "6798": {"comment":"IY = the new entry"},
        "6802": {"synopsis":"Calculator operation 12: pushes a copy of the top number onto the IY stack.\nChanges: BC, DE, HL.","comment":"Room on the IY stack?"},
        "6805": {"comment":"DE = just below the top entry"},
        "6809": {"comment":"Nine bytes"},
        "6814": {"comment":"HL = the top entry's last byte"},
        "6815": {"comment":"Copy it down"},
        "6817": {"comment":"IY = the copy"},
        "6822": {"synopsis":"Pushes a copy of the top two numbers onto the IY stack, keeping their order.\nChanges: BC, DE, HL.","comment":"Room on the IY stack?"},
        "6825": {"comment":"HL = just below the top entry"},
        "6831": {"comment":"18 bytes: two entries"},
        "6835": {"comment":"Copy them down"},
        "6837": {"comment":"IY = the copies"},
        "6842": {"synopsis":"Moves to the next item of the statement (NEXT_ITEM) and evaluates an integer expression\n(EXPR_TO_INT).","comment":"Next item"},
        "6845": {"comment":"Evaluate it"},
        "6847": {"synopsis":"Takes two numbers off the IY stack as integers: DE = the top one, HL = the one below it.\nCalls TOP_TO_INT and falls into it.","comment":"The top number"},
        "6850": {"comment":"DE = it; HL = the next one"},
        "6851": {"synopsis":"Takes the top number off the IY stack as an integer in HL without evaluating anything: its\nOR $37 clears carry and hides EXPR_TO_INT's SCF. Falls into TO_INT.","comment":"No carry: nothing to evaluate"},
        "6852": {"synopsis":"Evaluates an expression and takes it off the IY stack as an integer in HL: the SCF inside\nTOP_TO_INT's OR $37."},
        "6853": {"synopsis":"Converts the top number of the IY stack to a signed 16-bit integer and drops it.\n1. With carry set evaluates an expression first (EVAL_NUM).\n2. 10^5 or more is error 4 (\"Bad argument\"); below 1 gives 0.\n3. Otherwise builds HL from the integer digits (GET_DIGIT gives digit B): ten times plus the\n   digit, with an overflow being error 4. The fraction is dropped.\n4. Negates for a negative number; a result outside -32768 to 32767 is error 4.\nEntry: carry set to evaluate an expression first.\nExit: HL = the integer, A = the current token (B'), Z set when HL is 0-255.\nChanges: AF, HL, IY; BC and DE are kept.","comment":"Keep BC and DE"},
        "6855": {"comment":"Evaluate an expression first?"},
        "6858": {"comment":"The exponent"},
        "6861": {"comment":"Keep the sign"},
        "6862": {"comment":"Without the sign"},
        "6864": {"comment":"HL = 0"},
        "6866": {"comment":"10^5 or more?"},
        "6870": {"comment":"Below 1?"},
        "6874": {"comment":"C = 4 + the integer digits"},
        "6877": {"comment":"The first digit is digit 4"},
        "6879": {"synopsis":"Adds one integer digit: HL = 10HL + the digit.","comment":"HL 8192 or more?"},
        "6882": {"comment":"Then x 10 overflows"},
        "6884": {"comment":"HL x 2"},
        "6887": {"comment":"HL x 8"},
        "6889": {"comment":"x 10"},
        "6890": {"comment":"Too big?"},
        "6892": {"comment":"A = digit B"},
        "6898": {"comment":"Plus the digit"},
        "6901": {"comment":"Next digit"},
        "6902": {"comment":"All integer digits?"},
        "6906": {"comment":"The exponent byte again"},
        "6908": {"comment":"Negative?"},
        "6909": {"comment":"Yes: negate HL"},
        "6913": {"comment":"Is HL's sign the number's?"},
        "6914": {"comment":"No: out of range"},
        "6917": {"comment":"Its operand is POP AF: TI_RESULT enters there"},
        "6918": {"synopsis":"Drops the number and returns the integer; entered on the operand of the LD A,$F1 at $1B05\n(POP AF), with HL = 0 for a number below 1."},
        "6919": {"comment":"Drop the number"},
        "6924": {"comment":"Z: HL is 0-255"},
        "6926": {"comment":"A = the current token"},
        "6932": {"synopsis":"Raises error 4, \"Bad argument\". Code all over this chip jumps here.","comment":"Error 4, Bad argument"},
        "6934": {"synopsis":"Moves to the next item of the statement (NEXT_ITEM) and evaluates a byte expression\n(EXPR_TO_BYTE).","comment":"Next item"},
        "6937": {"comment":"Its operand is OR $37: TOP_TO_BYTE enters there"},
        "6938": {"synopsis":"Takes the top number off the IY stack as a byte in A without evaluating anything: entered on\nthe operand of the LD A,$F6 at $1B19, whose OR $37 clears carry and hides EXPR_TO_BYTE's\nSCF."},
        "6939": {"synopsis":"Evaluates an expression and takes it off the IY stack as a byte.\nExit: A = the value, 0-255; anything else is error 4 (\"Bad argument\"). Z set.\nChanges: AF, IY; BC, DE and HL are kept.","comment":"Carry: evaluate an expression"},
        "6940": {"comment":"Keep HL"},
        "6941": {"comment":"HL = the integer"},
        "6944": {"comment":"Above 255 or negative?"},
        "6949": {"comment":"A = the byte"},
        "6952": {"synopsis":"Calculator operation 13: rounds the top number on its lowest digit byte (ROUND_MANTISSA),\nstores its digit bytes IY+2 to IY+6 and its exponent in the numeric variable at HL, and\ndrops it.\nEntry: HL = the variable's value.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY.","comment":"Round to the stored digits"},
        "6955": {"comment":"DE = the variable"},
        "6956": {"comment":"HL = the number's digits"},
        "6961": {"comment":"Five digit bytes"},
        "6966": {"comment":"Past IY+7, the carry byte"},
        "6967": {"comment":"The exponent"},
        "6969": {"comment":"Drop the number"},
        "6971": {"synopsis":"Assigns the top of the IY stack to the variable at HL: a number (STORE_NUMBER) when bit 1 of\n$1701 is set (the variable's type as NEXT_ITEM found it), else a string (falls into\nSTORE_STRING).","comment":"A numeric variable?"},
        "6977": {"synopsis":"Stores the top string entry in the string variable at HL and drops it. A string longer than\nthe variable's maximum length (the byte at HL; at most 254) is error $0D (\"Overflow\").\nEntry: HL = the variable.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY.","comment":"C = the maximum length"},
        "6978": {"comment":"HL = the variable's length byte"},
        "6979": {"comment":"DE = the string's length byte"},
        "6983": {"comment":"A = its length"},
        "6984": {"comment":"Maximum + 1, unless that wraps to 0"},
        "6987": {"comment":"A maximum of $FF allows 254"},
        "6988": {"synopsis":"Checks the string's length against the maximum.","comment":"Too long?"},
        "6989": {"comment":"Error $0D, Overflow"},
        "6992": {"comment":"Length byte and characters"},
        "6996": {"comment":"Copy them"},
        "6999": {"synopsis":"Makes HL, just past the stored entry, the top of the IY stack: the value is dropped.","comment":"IY = past the entry"},
        "7003": {"synopsis":"The restart code: BASIC's start (the SYS low chip's BASIC_START) copies $1B5B-$1B81 to RAM\n$0008-$002E, and it runs there, so its addresses are RAM addresses (this line runs at\n$0008).\n- RST $08 ($0008): takes the error code byte after the RST and goes on into RST $10's code\n  with it; code 0 returns.\n- RST $10 ($0010, RST10_CODE): returns when Z is set, else drops the return address and\n  raises error A.\n- $0015 (CALC_OP_JUMP): a JP whose address CALCULATOR patches.\n- RST $18 ($0018, RST18_CODE): JP CALCULATOR.\n- $001B (SYSCALL_A_CODE): system call function A.\n- $0021-$002E: zeros.","comment":"$0008: HL = the code byte's address"},
        "7004": {"comment":"A = the error code"},
        "7005": {"comment":"Code 0: no error"},
        "7006": {"comment":"Return address back"},
        "7007": {"comment":"$000C-$000F: on into RST $10's code"},
        "7011": {"synopsis":"RST $10, run at RAM $0010: returns when Z is set, else raises the error in A (ERROR_ENTRY)\nin place of returning.","comment":"$0010: Z: no error"},
        "7012": {"comment":"Drop the return address"},
        "7013": {"comment":"Raise error A"},
        "7016": {"synopsis":"Run at RAM $0015: CALCULATOR writes the operation routine's address into $0016 and calls\n$0015.","comment":"$0015: JP to the operation, patched"},
        "7019": {"synopsis":"RST $18, run at RAM $0018: the calculator (CALCULATOR).","comment":"$0018: the calculator"},
        "7022": {"synopsis":"Run at RAM $001B: calls system function A by writing A into the function code byte ($001F)\nof the RST $30 after it. The SYS high chip's $FEB0 jumps here.","comment":"$001B: the function code into $001F"},
        "7025": {"comment":"$001E: system call function A"},
        "7027": {"comment":"$0020"},
        "7028": {"comment":"$0021-$002E: zeros, copied too"},
        "7042": {"synopsis":"RST $18, the calculator (RAM $0018 jumps here): runs the operation bytes after the RST. Each\nbyte is a calculator operation n (bits 6-0), the word at the SYS low chip's CALC_OP_TABLE +\n2n; bit 7 is set on the last one.\n1. Saves HL and A in $171E and $1704 and puts the previous stream pointer ($1718) on the\n   stack in place of the return address, so an operation can run a stream of its own.\n2. For each byte moves $1718 past it, patches the operation's address into RAM $0016 and\n   calls $0015 (CALC_OP_JUMP) with HL and A as the previous operation left them. An\n   operation that takes more bytes reads them through $1718 (operation 5 its constant\n   index).\n3. After the last one puts the address past the stream back as the return address, restores\n   $1718 and returns with the last operation's HL and A.\nChanges: whatever the operations change.","comment":"Keep HL and A for the first operation"},
        "7048": {"comment":"The previous stream pointer"},
        "7051": {"comment":"Saved; HL = the stream"},
        "7052": {"synopsis":"Runs the next operation of the stream.","comment":"The operation byte"},
        "7053": {"comment":"Past it"},
        "7057": {"comment":"x 2; carry = the last one?"},
        "7058": {"comment":"Keep that"},
        "7059": {"comment":"HL = CALC_OP_TABLE + 2n"},
        "7064": {"comment":"The operation's address"},
        "7068": {"comment":"Patch RAM $0016"},
        "7071": {"comment":"HL and A as the last operation left them"},
        "7077": {"comment":"Run the operation"},
        "7080": {"comment":"Keep its HL and A"},
        "7086": {"comment":"Where the stream is now"},
        "7089": {"comment":"The last operation?"},
        "7090": {"comment":"No: the next one"},
        "7092": {"comment":"Return past the stream; HL = old pointer"},
        "7093": {"comment":"Restore it"},
        "7096": {"comment":"The last operation's HL and A"},
        "7103": {"synopsis":"Reads the character at DE for the tokeniser (the SYS low chip's TOK_MATCH and TOK_STORE): as\nit is while B is nonzero (B is the closing quote inside quotes) or when it is the end marker\n$FF (Z); otherwise without bit 7 and in capitals (falls into TO_CAPITALS).","comment":"The character"},
        "7104": {"comment":"Inside quotes?"},
        "7106": {"comment":"Yes: as it is"},
        "7107": {"comment":"The end marker?"},
        "7110": {"comment":"Without bit 7"},
        "7112": {"synopsis":"Turns a small letter in A into a capital: a-z to A-Z, and codes $10-$18 to $00-$08 (the\nsmall Hungarian accented letters, which BASIC stores at $10-$18, to their capitals at\n$00-$08, as Klive's earlier TVC work observed); any other code is returned unchanged.","comment":"A control code?"},
        "7116": {"comment":"Below 'a'?"},
        "7119": {"comment":"Above 'z'?"},
        "7122": {"comment":"To the capital"},
        "7125": {"synopsis":"Codes $10-$18 become $00-$08.","comment":"Below $10?"},
        "7128": {"comment":"Above $18?"},
        "7131": {"comment":"Clear bit 4"},
        "7134": {"synopsis":"Takes the line number that follows and finds that line: the current token must be 2 (a\nnumber follows), else error 1 (\"Not understood\"); a line that is not in the program is error\n2 (\"Line missing\").\nExit: HL = the line (the SYS low chip's FIND_LINE_HL).","comment":"A number follows (token 2)?"},
        "7136": {"comment":"No: error 1, Not understood"},
        "7139": {"comment":"HL = the line number"},
        "7142": {"comment":"Find the line"},
        "7145": {"comment":"Found"},
        "7146": {"comment":"Error 2, Line missing"},
        "7148": {"synopsis":"Takes an optional channel for TRACE: '#n' or the default $20. After '#n' comes ':' (skipped\nby moving to the next item, NEXT_ITEM) or the end of the statement ($FE or above); anything\nelse is error 1 (\"Not understood\").","comment":"The default channel"},
        "7150": {"synopsis":"CHANNEL_END with the default channel in C.","comment":"'#n' given?"},
        "7153": {"comment":"No: done"},
        "7154": {"comment":"':' after it?"},
        "7156": {"comment":"Yes: past it"},
        "7158": {"comment":"The end of the statement?"},
        "7161": {"comment":"Error 1, Not understood"},
        "7163": {"synopsis":"SET_CHANNEL with the default channel $20 (device 2, the screen editor).","comment":"The default channel"},
        "7165": {"synopsis":"Sets the output channel in $1705 to C, or, when the current token is '#' ($A7), to 16 x n\nfor the '#n' that follows (n from NEXT_BYTE_EXPR, bits 2-0 kept): system call entry n, whose\nfunction 1 the output uses.\nExit: Z set when '#n' was given (C = the channel, A = the token after it); NZ otherwise (A =\nthe current token).","comment":"The channel, unless '#n' follows"},
        "7168": {"synopsis":"Reads an optional \"#n\" channel: when the current token is '#' ($A7), evaluates n as a byte\n(NEXT_BYTE_EXPR, which steps to it first) and makes channel n the output channel: $1705 = (n\nAND 7) x 16 (the entry in bits 6-4 of a system call code). The routine at $1BFB sets $1705\nto $20 (the screen) and falls in here; PRINT calls it at PRINT_CHANNEL.\nExit: NZ, A = the current token: no '#'. Z: C = $1705, A = the current token after n.\nChanges: AF, C, the alternate registers through NEXT_BYTE_EXPR.","comment":"Current token"},
        "7171": {"comment":"'#'?"},
        "7174": {"comment":"n, a byte"},
        "7177": {"comment":"Channel 0-7 to bits 6-4"},
        "7183": {"comment":"The output channel"},
        "7187": {"comment":"Z: a channel was read"},
        "7188": {"comment":"A = the current token (flags kept)"},
        "7192": {"synopsis":"Sends output to the screen editor again ($1705 = $20, device 2) and makes sure the ink can\nbe seen: when the ink colour $0B4D equals the paper colour $0B4E, the ink becomes paper + 1,\nor in 16-colour mode (bit 1 of $0B13, the copy of port 06h) the complement of the paper.\nUsed before the error and STOP messages and by the SYS low chip (TITLE_DELAY's area at\n$DA7E, PROMPT_OK).\nChanges: AF, C.","comment":"Output channel $20: the screen editor"},
        "7196": {"comment":"Paper colour"},
        "7200": {"comment":"Ink colour"},
        "7203": {"comment":"Ink differs from paper?"},
        "7205": {"comment":"Ink = paper + 1"},
        "7209": {"comment":"16-colour mode?"},
        "7215": {"comment":"Ink = NOT paper"},
        "7221": {"synopsis":"Closes the cassette channel when bit 3 of $1700 says one is open (set at OPEN_FILE, cleared\nat $EA63 after function 4 of the channel): clears the bit and falls into CLOSE_CASSETTE. The\nerror and STOP code call it.","comment":"A channel open?"},
        "7226": {"comment":"Not any more"},
        "7230": {"synopsis":"Calls function 4 of device 5 (the cassette) and function 4 of entry 5 of the RAM table $0B00\n(codes $54 and $D4); the results are ignored. The SYS low chip's RUN_CMD calls it here.","comment":"Device 5 function 4"},
        "7232": {"comment":"Entry 5 of the $0B00 table, function 4"},
        "7235": {"synopsis":"Steps the program text to the next item and makes it the current token (B'), skipping\nspaces.\n1. The end of the line ($FF) or '!' ($FE) is not passed: B' = that byte.\n2. A keyword or operator token ($80-$FD) is passed: B' = the token.\n3. A quote starts a string constant and anything else that is not a letter a number\n   constant: both are read by READ_VALUE (B' = 0 for a string, 2 for a number).\n4. A letter is a variable, found by VAR_FIND (made by VAR_CREATE when not found): B' = 1 or\n   3, bit 1 from C after the lookup (1 a string variable, 3 a numeric one: bit 1 is the\n   variable's numeric bit). INKEY$ ($C5) also gives B' = 1, with C = $C5.\nEntry: HL' = the text pointer.\nExit: A = B'; Z: the item is ':'; NC: the statement ends there (':', '!' or the end of the\nline); C set otherwise.\nChanges: AF, the alternate registers.","comment":"Text pointer to HL"},
        "7236": {"synopsis":"Skips spaces."},
        "7238": {"comment":"The end of the line or '!'?"},
        "7243": {"comment":"INKEY$?"},
        "7247": {"comment":"A token?"},
        "7251": {"comment":"A space?"},
        "7255": {"comment":"Back to the item's first byte"},
        "7258": {"comment":"A string constant: B = 0"},
        "7263": {"comment":"B = 2: numeric"},
        "7265": {"comment":"Below 'A': a number"},
        "7269": {"comment":"Above 'Z': a number too"},
        "7274": {"comment":"A variable: find it"},
        "7277": {"comment":"Not found: make it"},
        "7280": {"comment":"B = 1 or 3 from the variable's type"},
        "7286": {"synopsis":"Reads a constant (string or number) when carry is set."},
        "7289": {"synopsis":"Returns with A = B = the item's code and the flags of a compare with ':'."},
        "7290": {"synopsis":"A token, or the end of the line: B already holds it."},
        "7291": {"comment":"Z on ':', NC at the statement's end"},
        "7294": {"synopsis":"INKEY$: B = 1, C = its token."},
        "7299": {"synopsis":"Makes HL its absolute value as a signed number, through NEG_HL.\nChanges: AF, HL.","comment":"Positive?"},
        "7302": {"synopsis":"Negates HL (two's complement: HL - 1, then each bit inverted).\nChanges: A, HL."},
        "7310": {"synopsis":"Checks there is room for 256 more bytes (ROOM_CHECK with DE = $0100); keeps HL and DE. Error\n6, \"No memory\", when there is not."},
        "7321": {"synopsis":"Checks there is room for DE more bytes above the program: ($1726), just past the program's\nend, + DE must not pass 65535 or the expression stack (IY), and SP must not be below $0B17,\nthe lowest address the stack may use.\nEntry: DE = the bytes needed.\nExit: returns when there is room; otherwise error 6, \"No memory\".\nChanges: AF, DE, HL.","comment":"Past the program's end"},
        "7325": {"comment":"Past 65535?"},
        "7328": {"comment":"Above the expression stack?"},
        "7335": {"comment":"HL = SP"},
        "7338": {"comment":"SP below the stack limit?"},
        "7345": {"synopsis":"Error 6, \"No memory\".","comment":"Error 6: No memory"},
        "7347": {"synopsis":"Multiplies BC by DE, shifting BC left one bit at a time over 16 steps.\nEntry: BC, DE.\nExit: HL = the product's low 16 bits.\nChanges: AF, BC, HL.","comment":"HL = 0"},
        "7350": {"comment":"16 bits"},
        "7352": {"synopsis":"One bit of BC, from the top: HL doubled, DE added when the bit is set."},
        "7356": {"comment":"Next bit of BC into carry"},
        "7365": {"synopsis":"Finds the next statement in the line that starts with a statement keyword ($CA-$FD), from\nthe text pointer: statements that do not (assignments without LET) are skipped.\nEntry: HL' = the text pointer.\nExit: NC: A = the keyword, HL at it; or A = $FE/$FF, the end of the line. Carry is never\nreturned.\nChanges: AF, HL.","comment":"The next statement"},
        "7368": {"synopsis":"The end of the line, or a statement keyword, ends the search."},
        "7369": {"comment":"A statement keyword?"},
        "7372": {"comment":"No: on to the next ':'"},
        "7377": {"synopsis":"Finds the next ':' after the text pointer, without stepping the pointer itself.\nEntry: HL' = the text pointer.\nExit: carry set: HL and A = the first byte after the ':' and its spaces; NC: A = $FE or $FF,\nthe end of the line, HL at it.\nChanges: AF, HL.","comment":"HL = the text pointer"},
        "7381": {"synopsis":"Steps byte by byte to a ':' token or the end of the line. Quotes are not looked at (TOKENISE\nnever makes $FD inside them)."},
        "7382": {"comment":"The end of the line or '!'?"},
        "7386": {"comment":"':'?"},
        "7390": {"synopsis":"Skips the spaces after the ':'."},
        "7396": {"comment":"Back to the first byte that is not a space"},
        "7399": {"synopsis":"Drops entries off the expression stack until one of type A: a FOR entry ($2B) for NEXT, a\nGOSUB entry ($06) for RETURN. Each entry's type byte is its length; type 1 has its length in\nthe next byte (2 + that many bytes in all); type 0 is the stack's base.\nEntry: A = the type wanted.\nExit: Z, NC: found, IY = HL = the entry. Z, carry: the base was reached (none).\nChanges: BC, HL, IY.","comment":"The top entry"},
        "7404": {"synopsis":"The entry at HL: its type?"},
        "7405": {"comment":"The type wanted?"},
        "7407": {"comment":"Type 0: the base"},
        "7411": {"comment":"Type 1: the length is in the next byte"},
        "7416": {"synopsis":"Steps past the entry and drops it (IY = the next one)."},
        "7423": {"synopsis":"Evaluates an expression (EVAL_NUM) as an address and falls into TOP_TO_ADDRESS. POKE starts\nwith it.","comment":"Evaluate it"},
        "7426": {"synopsis":"Takes the number on top of the expression stack as an address 0-65535: offsets it by 32768\n(PUSH_INT, CALC_ADD), makes it a signed integer (TOP_TO_INT, error otherwise) and adds $8000\nback.\nObserved: POKE -1 and POKE 70000 give error 4, \"Bad argument\"; 0-65535 work.\nExit: HL = the address.\nChanges: AF, DE, HL, the stack's top.","comment":"32768"},
        "7436": {"comment":"The signed integer"},
        "7439": {"comment":"Plus $8000"},
        "7442": {"synopsis":"Looks a keyword up in a table and runs its handler. The table holds pairs [key byte,\noffset], ended by a 0 key; the handler is the address after the pair plus the offset. SET\n($07E4 table) and SOUND ($087B) use it. The handler is called with the next item fetched\n(NEXT_ITEM), through the SYS low chip's JP (HL) at $DBAD.\nEntry: A = the keyword, HL = the table.\nExit: Z, NC, A kept: not in the table. Carry: the handler ran.","comment":"Keep the keyword"},
        "7443": {"synopsis":"One pair: the end of the table, or the key?"},
        "7447": {"comment":"Zero when the key matches"},
        "7449": {"comment":"The offset"},
        "7453": {"comment":"D = 0"},
        "7454": {"comment":"The handler"},
        "7458": {"comment":"Call it (JP (HL) at $DBAD)"},
        "7463": {"synopsis":"Reads \"(a:b)\", \"(a:)\", \"(:b)\" or \"(a)\" after the opening bracket, each bound an integer\nclipped to 0-255 (SLICE_BOUND), and the closing ')'.\nExit: E = the first bound (1 when left out), D = the last (255 when left out; equal to the\nfirst for \"(a)\").\nChanges: AF, DE, HL, the alternate registers.","comment":"The first item"},
        "7466": {"comment":"First bound defaults to 1"},
        "7468": {"comment":"':' at once: no first bound"},
        "7474": {"comment":"':'?"},
        "7478": {"synopsis":"After the ':': the last bound, 255 when ')' follows at once."},
        "7483": {"comment":"')'?"},
        "7488": {"synopsis":"Expects the closing ')'."},
        "7493": {"synopsis":"Evaluates an integer (EXPR_TO_INT) and clips it to 0-255 into D: a negative number gives 0,\none above 255 gives 255.\nExit: D = the bound; HL = the integer.","comment":"Evaluate the integer"},
        "7498": {"comment":"Negative: D = 0"},
        "7500": {"comment":"Above 255: D = 255"},
        "7505": {"synopsis":"Steps to the next item, then falls into EXPECT_TOKEN."},
        "7508": {"synopsis":"Insists on a token: when the current token (B') is A, steps to the next item (NEXT_ITEM);\notherwise error 1, \"Not understood\".\nEntry: A = the token expected.","comment":"The current token?"},
        "7514": {"synopsis":"Error 1, \"Not understood\", through ERROR_ENTRY.","comment":"Not understood"},
        "7516": {"synopsis":"Reports an error and stops: where RST $08 (with its code byte) and RST $10 (with A) end,\nthrough the code copied to RAM $0008-$0014, and EXTH through EXTH_JUMP.\n1. Restores the expression stack saved at the statement's start ($171A). A break ($F5) goes\n   to the STOP code (STOP_CMD).\n2. Error 6 (\"No memory\") first resets the program's run state (the SYS low chip's\n   RESET_RUN); an open cassette channel is closed (CLOSE_IF_OPEN) and output goes to the\n   screen (OUTPUT_TO_SCREEN).\n3. Prints a new line and \"*** \", then the message of the code from ERROR_MESSAGES; a code\n   with bit 7 set (an error a system call returned) prints \"System error <code>\" in decimal\n   instead.\n4. Ends with \".\", $0B and a new line, sets bit 1 of $1700 (an error was printed, so\n   COMMAND_LOOP prints no \"ok\"), lists the current line ($170C; observed: a direct command\n   is listed without a number) and goes to the END statement (END_CMD).\nEntry: A = the error code.","comment":"The expression stack at the statement's start"},
        "7520": {"comment":"A break?"},
        "7526": {"comment":"No memory?"},
        "7528": {"comment":"Reset the run state"},
        "7531": {"comment":"Close an open cassette channel"},
        "7534": {"comment":"Back to the screen"},
        "7537": {"comment":"CR LF \"*** \""},
        "7548": {"comment":"A system call's error?"},
        "7552": {"comment":"ERROR_MESSAGES"},
        "7555": {"comment":"BC = -1: the first step adds 0"},
        "7558": {"synopsis":"Steps to the next entry (BC = the last entry's length + 1) and compares its code."},
        "7560": {"comment":"The entry's code"},
        "7563": {"comment":"Code 0: the end, print this one"},
        "7566": {"comment":"Is it our code?"},
        "7567": {"comment":"C = the length"},
        "7570": {"synopsis":"Prints the message after its length byte."},
        "7571": {"comment":"Spell out the message"},
        "7576": {"synopsis":"A code with bit 7 set: \"System error \" and the code in decimal (PRINT_DEC, no padding)."},
        "7577": {"comment":"\"System error \""},
        "7594": {"comment":"HL = the code, no padding or space"},
        "7600": {"synopsis":"Ends the message and lists the line the error happened in.","comment":"\".\", $0B, CR LF"},
        "7608": {"comment":"An error was printed: no \"ok\""},
        "7612": {"comment":"The current line"},
        "7615": {"comment":"Z, NC: no new line after it"},
        "7616": {"comment":"List it (PRINT_LINE)"},
        "7619": {"comment":"The END statement"},
        "7622": {"synopsis":"The error messages, one entry per code: the code, the length of the rest, then the text\nended by $FF (the length counts the $FF). Bytes from $80 in the text are word tokens, spelt\nout by PRINT_TOKENISED through the SYS low chip's TOKEN_TABLE; $8F-$93 are its MESSAGE_WORDS\n(\"Cannot \", \"No \", \"Bad \", \"rgument\", \" missing\"), others are keywords (\"No DATA\", \"Cannot\nCONTINUE\"). Codes 1-16 ($01-$10); the entry with code 0 ends the table and serves every\nother code below $80. ERROR_ENTRY searches it.\nError 1 ($01): \"Not understood\"."},
        "7639": {"synopsis":"Error 2 ($02): \"Line missing\"."},
        "7647": {"synopsis":"Error 3 ($03): \"Argument missing\"."},
        "7653": {"synopsis":"Error 4 ($04): \"Bad argument\"."},
        "7659": {"synopsis":"Error 5 ($05): \"Bad subscript\"."},
        "7672": {"synopsis":"Error 6 ($06): \"No memory\"."},
        "7682": {"synopsis":"Error 7 ($07): \"No DATA\"."},
        "7687": {"synopsis":"Error 8 ($08): \"No FOR\"."},
        "7692": {"synopsis":"Error 9 ($09): \"No GOSUB\"."},
        "7697": {"synopsis":"Error 10 ($0A): \"Cannot CONTINUE\"."},
        "7702": {"synopsis":"Error 11 ($0B): \"Cannot divide by 0\"."},
        "7717": {"synopsis":"Error 12 ($0C): \"Cannot READ\"."},
        "7722": {"synopsis":"Error 13 ($0D): \"Overflow\"."},
        "7733": {"synopsis":"Error 14 ($0E): \"Type mismatch\"."},
        "7749": {"synopsis":"Error 15 ($0F): \"Variable declared twice\"."},
        "7775": {"synopsis":"Error 16 ($10): \"Bad file\"."},
        "7783": {"synopsis":"Code 0 ends the table: any error code not found above prints \"BASIC corrupted\". Its length\nbyte, 15, counts the letters only, not the $FF after them (the search never steps past this\nentry)."},
        "7801": {"synopsis":"Prints the length-prefixed string that follows the call and returns past it (through\nPRINT_LSTR).\nChanges: AF.","comment":"HL = the string; the caller's HL saved"},
        "7805": {"comment":"Return past the string"},
        "7807": {"synopsis":"Prints the string at HL: a length byte (0 prints nothing), then that many characters through\nPUT_CHAR.\nEntry: HL = the string.\nExit: HL past it.\nChanges: AF, HL.","comment":"The length"},
        "7810": {"comment":"Empty?"},
        "7813": {"synopsis":"One character."},
        "7822": {"synopsis":"Starts a new line: $0B first when Z is set, then CR and LF (falls into NEWLINE).\nChanges: A.","comment":"$0B when Z"},
        "7827": {"synopsis":"Prints CR and LF.\nChanges: A.","comment":"CR"},
        "7832": {"comment":"LF, through PUT_CHAR"},
        "7834": {"synopsis":"Prints the character in A on the output channel (OUT_CHANNEL); an error the channel returns\nis raised (RST $10).\nEntry: A = the character.\nChanges: nothing.","comment":"Keep AF, BC, DE"},
        "7837": {"comment":"C = the character"},
        "7841": {"comment":"Raise the channel's error"},
        "7846": {"synopsis":"Sends C to the output channel: a system call with code ($1705) OR 1, bit 7 clear (function 1\nof the channel's entry in the $0B08 table, e.g. $21: device 2 writes C at the cursor), made\nthrough RAM $001B (copied from $1B6E), which puts A after an RST $30. Falls into\nCHANNEL_CALL.\nEntry: C = the character.\nExit: A = 0 or the error code.","comment":"Keep bits 6-0, set none"},
        "7849": {"synopsis":"Calls function 1 of the output channel's entry: code = (($1705) OR 1 OR D) AND E, run\nthrough RAM $001B. KEY_LINE uses D = $80, E = $FF to read a character from the same entry in\nthe $0B00 table ($A1 for the screen editor).\nEntry: D = bits to set, E = bits to keep, C = the character for a write.\nExit: A = 0 or the error code; C = the character for a read.","comment":"The output channel"},
        "7852": {"comment":"Function 1"},
        "7856": {"comment":"RST $30 with code A, in RAM"},
        "7859": {"synopsis":"Prints A for EXTH: EXTH's $FF8E calls it through its own EXTH_JUMP (which pages SYS back\nin), so the return address on the stack is in EXTH; falls into EXTH_RETURN.","comment":"Print A"},
        "7862": {"synopsis":"Returns into EXTH: pops the return address and jumps to it through EXTH_JUMP, which pages\nEXTH in. GET_DIGIT_EXT and EXT_CLEAR_TOP push its address so that a routine EXTH called\nreturns this way.","comment":"The return address, in EXTH"},
        "7866": {"synopsis":"Prints the number on top of the expression stack as PRINT does: a space for its sign when it\nis positive (bit 7 of the exponent, IY+8, clear), its digits (NUM_TO_TEXT writes the text,\nlength-prefixed, at $1930) and a space after.\nChanges: AF, HL and what NUM_TO_TEXT changes.","comment":"Positive?"},
        "7870": {"comment":"A space in place of the sign"},
        "7873": {"synopsis":"Prints the number without the leading space, then a space. The SYS low chip's title calls it\nhere ($DABA).","comment":"The number as text"},
        "7876": {"comment":"Print the text"},
        "7879": {"synopsis":"Prints a space.\nChanges: nothing.","comment":"A space, through PUT_CHAR"},
        "7883": {"synopsis":"A character below $80: a control code below $20 is printed with bit 7 set (KEY_LINE stores\n$80-$9F typed as $00-$1F); a quote switches the quoted state.","comment":"Bit 7 set for $00-$1F"},
        "7890": {"comment":"A quote?"},
        "7894": {"comment":"The closing quote?"},
        "7897": {"comment":"Opening: keep the state in B"},
        "7899": {"synopsis":"The closing quote: the state from before the quote back from B (enters inside an\ninstruction: 78 = LD A,B, then $0E skips the next byte)."},
        "7901": {"synopsis":"Prints tokenised text up to its $FF, spelling the tokens out from the SYS low chip's\nTOKEN_TABLE (and its MESSAGE_WORDS, tokens $93-$8F). Text in quotes is printed as stored,\nand after DATA, REM or '!' the rest is printed as stored until a ':' token; control codes\nare shown with bit 7 set (PT_CHAR). Used by PRINT_LINE (LIST, the error report) and the\nerror messages. Enters inside an instruction (AF = XOR A, the first byte of $1EDC's\noperand).\nEntry: HL = the text.\nExit: HL past the $FF.\nChanges: AF, BC.","comment":"C = 0: not quoted"},
        "7903": {"synopsis":"The next byte: $FF ends, below $80 is a character, else a token."},
        "7905": {"comment":"The end?"},
        "7911": {"comment":"A token"},
        "7912": {"comment":"Z: tokens are spelt out"},
        "7915": {"comment":"DATA?"},
        "7919": {"comment":"REM, ':' or '!'?"},
        "7923": {"synopsis":"DATA, REM or '!' (and ':' for the moment): what follows is printed as stored (C = $FF)."},
        "7925": {"synopsis":"A ':' ends the literal state (C back to 0)."},
        "7929": {"comment":"C was $FF: now 0, Z"},
        "7932": {"synopsis":"Spells the token out unless the text is quoted or literal."},
        "7935": {"comment":"A = the word's number: $FF - token"},
        "7936": {"comment":"TOKEN_TABLE, the $FF before it counts as word 0"},
        "7939": {"synopsis":"Skips A words, each ended by bit 7."},
        "7947": {"synopsis":"Prints the word's letters up to the one with bit 7."},
        "7953": {"synopsis":"Prints the byte (a letter of the word, or a quoted token as stored)."},
        "7956": {"comment":"Not the last letter: on"},
        "7961": {"synopsis":"Prints HL in decimal followed by a space (PRINT_DEC with B = $FF). PRINT_LINE (padding with\ncarry, so LIST shows \"  10 \") and STOP use it.","comment":"A space after it"},
        "7963": {"synopsis":"Prints HL (0-9999) in decimal, dividing by DEC_DIVISORS. Leading zeros print as spaces when\ncarry is set on entry, as nothing otherwise; 0 itself prints no digit (a direct command's\nline 0 is listed without one). A space follows when B is nonzero and something was printed\nor padded.\nEntry: HL = the number, carry = pad, B = trailing space.\nChanges: AF, C, DE.","comment":"C = $20 when padding, else 0"},
        "7968": {"comment":"DEC_DIVISORS"},
        "7971": {"synopsis":"One digit: the next divisor in DE, the number back in HL."},
        "7976": {"comment":"Count the subtractions"},
        "7977": {"synopsis":"Subtracts the divisor until it goes below zero."},
        "7982": {"comment":"One too many"},
        "7984": {"comment":"A zero digit?"},
        "7986": {"comment":"Digits from now on: zeros print"},
        "7993": {"synopsis":"A zero: prints C, the pad (nothing, a space, or '0' after the first digit)."},
        "7998": {"comment":"The last divisor (1)?"},
        "8002": {"comment":"A trailing space?"},
        "8007": {"synopsis":"The divisors of PRINT_DEC: 1000, 100, 10, 1. The loop ends after the divisor whose low byte\nis 1, so numbers have four digits at most (line numbers are 1-9999)."},
        "8015": {"synopsis":"Reads a line from the input channel into $1831: the count byte, then the characters, ended\nby $FF. Each character comes from CHANNEL_CALL with bit 7 set (the screen editor's $A1 for\nchannel $20) until CR. Codes below $20 are dropped; $80-$9F are stored as $00-$1F; at most\n251 characters are kept. INPUT and the SYS low chip's OK_CMD use it.\nExit: HL = $1831. Z, A = 0: a line. NZ: A = $F5 (a break, also when $0B16 is set) or $EC,\nwith an empty line. Any other error is raised (RST $10).\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"The buffer, kept on the stack"},
        "8019": {"comment":"No characters yet"},
        "8021": {"synopsis":"Reads the next character, keeping the count in B."},
        "8022": {"comment":"Bit 7 set: the input table $0B00"},
        "8029": {"comment":"B = the count again"},
        "8030": {"comment":"A character?"},
        "8032": {"synopsis":"No character: the line is made empty; a break ($F5) or $EC returns NZ, other errors are\nraised."},
        "8035": {"comment":"Count 0"},
        "8037": {"comment":"Ended at once"},
        "8040": {"comment":"A break?"},
        "8044": {"comment":"$EC?"},
        "8048": {"comment":"Raise any other error"},
        "8050": {"synopsis":"A character came: a break requested meanwhile ($0B16, set by CTRL+ESC) ends the line as $F5."},
        "8053": {"comment":"Break requested?"},
        "8058": {"comment":"The character"},
        "8059": {"comment":"CR ends the line"},
        "8063": {"comment":"Below $20: dropped"},
        "8067": {"comment":"Below $80: kept as it is"},
        "8071": {"comment":"$80-$9F become $00-$1F"},
        "8076": {"synopsis":"Stores the character unless 251 are stored already."},
        "8077": {"comment":"Full?"},
        "8082": {"synopsis":"CR: the line ends with $FF in its place and the count goes in front."},
        "8083": {"comment":"Store it"},
        "8084": {"comment":"Always taken"},
        "8086": {"comment":"$FF over the CR"},
        "8090": {"comment":"The count"},
        "8091": {"synopsis":"Returns with the flags of A: Z for 0."},
        "8093": {"synopsis":"Checks for a break: returns while the break flag $0B16 is clear; otherwise goes on into the\nSTOP code with the registers as they were (EXX twice). The SYS low chip's STMT_LOOP and\nLIST_ADVANCE call it.\nEntry: HL = the text pointer.","comment":"Break requested?"},
        "8098": {"comment":"Cancels the EXX at STOP_CMD"},
        "8099": {"synopsis":"The STOP statement (token $D4), and where a break ends:\n1. Clears the break flag $0B16, closes an open cassette channel (CLOSE_IF_OPEN) and prints a\n   new line and \"STOP\" on the screen (OUTPUT_TO_SCREEN).\n2. In a running program (bit 2 of $1700) keeps the text pointer in $1710 and the current\n   line ($170C) in $170E for CONTINUE, and prints \" at line \" and the line's number\n   (observed: \"STOP at line 10 \").\n3. Ends the line (NEWLINE_Z with Z: $0B, CR, LF) and goes to the SYS low chip's\n   COMMAND_LOOP.\nEntry: HL' = the text pointer (the statement loop's way); BREAK_CHECK and ERROR_ENTRY come\nin with it in HL.","comment":"The text pointer to HL"},
        "8100": {"synopsis":"Entry with the text pointer already in HL ($E1C8 jumps here).","comment":"Clear the break flag"},
        "8104": {"comment":"Close an open cassette channel"},
        "8107": {"comment":"Back to the screen"},
        "8110": {"comment":"CR LF \"STOP\""},
        "8120": {"comment":"A program running?"},
        "8126": {"comment":"Where CONTINUE goes on"},
        "8129": {"comment":"The line CONTINUE goes on in"},
        "8135": {"comment":"\" at line \""},
        "8148": {"comment":"Past the length byte"},
        "8152": {"comment":"The line number"},
        "8156": {"comment":"No padding"},
        "8160": {"synopsis":"Ends the message line and goes to COMMAND_LOOP.","comment":"Z: $0B first"},
        "8164": {"comment":"COMMAND_LOOP"},
        "8167": {"synopsis":"Maps an address of $C000-$FFFF given to POKE or PEEK onto the video RAM: HL - $4000 (page 2)\nand A = $50, the port 02h value with the video RAM in page 2 (and SYS in page 3).\nObserved: POKE 49152,170 then PEEK(49152) gives 170.\nEntry: HL = the address.\nExit: carry set: below $C000, HL kept. NC: HL in $8000-$BFFF, A = $50.","comment":"Below $C000?"},
        "8171": {"comment":"$C000-$FFFF to $8000-$BFFF"},
        "8173": {"comment":"Video RAM in page 2"},
        "8176": {"synopsis":"Jumps to HL in EXTH: writes $F0 to port 02h and its RAM copy $0003 (EXT in page 3: the card\nwindow and the EXTH ROM, U2 in page 2, U0 in page 0). The instructions after the OUT are\nfetched from EXTH, which has the same POP AF / JP (HL) here (its own $FFF0 writes $70 and\ncomes back to SYS the same way).\nEntry: HL = the address in EXTH.","comment":"Keep AF"},
        "8182": {"comment":"From here on EXTH runs"},
        "8186": {"synopsis":"Unused: nothing jumps here or reads these bytes (they look like the start of the code at\n$1F7A, but for the last byte)."}
      }
    }
  },
